      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <p>LADIES' LETTER.</p>
        <p>EY nun NELLIE,</p>
        <p>That the new, full skirt i- not to have things all its own wat is certair. I recently saw a new skirt, tight to the knees. The model is made of ;pale green accordeou.pleatei chiffon with steel- embroidered belt, epaulettes and vandyked trim- sning on the skirt. Another is made of asparagus leaf green silk of the softest texiure. It is of sheathlike tightness to the knees. The bodice worn with this dress is string-coloured lace with a short bolero of the green silk trimmed with emerald buttons. The long ends of the russet-green silk sash are sewn into little gilt cup, like acorns. The hat worn with this very novel and striking dress is entirely composed •of pink roses and soft swathings of black tulle.</p>
        <p>2elargoniurns are among the fashionable milli- nery flowers this season, and they are very deftly copied from nature in velvet and silk petals. One hat is made of black crinoline, with bands of black velvet round the crown and falling in a long, hang- ing bow at the back. Large pink roses are so arranged as to appear to be trying to peep over the brim to see their wearer's face. Another is made of tucked and gathered black chiffon or tulle, and is trimmed with bunches of shaded lilac. Folds of lilac ribbon are carried round the crown. It would be difficult to think of a shape that is not worn in hats. Even the mushroom, so hideous of cossibilities, has its fairly pretty develop- ment in a tilted shape, with three or four straws turned down all round. Any shape into which straw can be bent is worn, and a girl has been known to shut her hat half in and half out of a drawer to tame it into the most becoming curve, after having diligently secured the latter within the boundaries of a triplicate mirror. This is an easier way than sitting holding the hat all morn- ing. Thu lightness of the hat of the moment is one of its greatest recommendations. We all know the headache hat but too well. It is overloaded with velvet and flowers and big bows of ribbon, and the number of pins needed to keep it in posi- tion would suffice in themselves to bestow a head- ache on the wearer, apart from the weight. But the skill of milliners is now directed towards mini- mising the weight of headgear.</p>
        <p>you are in iing some home aakieg it is a good plan in order to obtain thoroughly correct measurement to first of all securely pin round the waist a measuring tape, and take all the measurements to this tape line at the waist. If a really well-fitting pattern can be obtained the amateur modiste will find her work wonderfully lightened, and innumerable difficulties will be thereby smoothed out of the way. She at first has the barest comprehension of the many pitfalls which lurk in her path, and how sur- prisingly easy it is to cut out two sleeves for one and the same arm from material which is not re- versible. Experience and care alone can put such errors altogether out of the question, and it is therefore a wise plan to experiment at first on inexpensive garments. I would accordingly suggest that before attempting the achievement of exploiting a smart blouee, it would be a good plan to try the tight-fitting lining in the making of a calico underbodice or some other easier made and cheaper garment.</p>
        <p>Eolienne and voile are the leading materials for very dressy frocks, as is also a charming fabric like a thin silky poplin. All these thin stuffs drape and fall admirably, being soft and cling- ing, but they have a drawback in not always being able to stand a shower successfully, some of them cockling a little. A charming evening gown is made of satin eicilierme, with raised and padded mousseline em- broidery on skirt and bodice ; tne becoming fulness at the waist is gathered in under a large softly. coloured Fart nouveau buckle—the long chiffon sleeves are puffed from the elbow. A walking dress is in shaded grey cloth, with strapped bands on the skirt, and large vandyked linen guipure Collar and cuffs finishing off the bolero.</p>
        <p>Your loving Jaways.</p>
