      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>When the Duke and Duchess of Cornwall and York return, they will bring with them a harvest cf pleasant memories, and we may be sure that not the least agree- able of their recollections will be these associated with their visit to the warm-hearted people of New Zealand, the most distant of His Majesty's possessions. Their reception by that interesting race, the Maoris—or, as some people call them, the Marys—must have been a particularly striking and mernoratee experience. Whilst we at home are enjoying the early days of our beautifth summer, the trees in New Z lised are b sting their autumn tints, but at any time of the }ear the climate of New Zealand—perhapsthe finest in the world —is aa agreeable as anybody need wish for. The only drawback is the sand storms which are not, happile, of Very frequent occurrence. Aseot,witale its many fashionable associations has+ been overshadowed by the sad event in the early pert of the year: The absence of Royalty deprived the meeting of its principal attraction and the fact that everyone admitted to the enclosure was expected to be in mourn- ing, had an effect which was in strieing contrast to the eaietyand wealth of colour of the usual Ascot gathering. Next year, if all goes well, Ascet will return to all its termer glory, as when Queen Victoria used to •ttend the Races in State. There is to be a new Royal Stand and other improvements, which show that the Court fully intenis to maintain its associations with Ascot. The Duchess of Sutherland has been very little in terve. this season, and Stafford House has been closed to all but charitable entertainments. A concert was given there the other day in aid of the Charing 0098 Hoepital, and the Duchess has spared no pains to make the fete in aid of the National L'feboat Institution a brilliant success. Another feshicnable event in the cause of philanthropy will be the County Bazar, which will take pace at Earl's Court, towards the end of the month. There will be stalls for each county of the United Kingdom, and each will be presided over by one of the principal ladle, of the diatrict they represent. The Bazaar has been organised in response to Queen Alexandra's appeal for the Soldiers and Sailor 2 Families Association. The term "dowager" and "chaperon" seem likely in the near future to fall into desuetude. Nowadays women not only refuse to grow old, but they dislike titles which seem significant of a too mature acquaintance with Anne D.amini,ln notting has Queen Alexandra been more enccessful than in introducing into this country the art of always looking young. N) one would imagine from mere appearance only, that she was a grand- mother of many years standing, mar, to mention an- other instance only, would anyone take Lady Warwick and Lady Marjorie G:eville, who will enter her eight- eenth year next autumn, to be mother and daughter, The Empress Frederick so disliked the title "dowager" that she would never permit its reference to herself, after the Emperor Frederick died, and in Holland Queen Wilhelmina caused a decree to be issued that her mother was to be known as Queen Emma of the Nether- lands, and net as Queen Dowager. The complete Census returns show that, in the bee- ance of the sexes, the number of women and girls con- tinues to increase over the number of men and boys. Is Ergland and Wales alone, there is an excess of 1,062,619 females, against 896,723 in 1891.—This means that there are 107 women to 100 men, and this increas- ing disprcrertion tends more and more to make the struggle for life all the harder for the weaker sex. The more women that are left to face the world without any resources of their own, the more they must compete with one another. The proper solution we are told is emigration to the 'Colonies, where women are want, d but this is a step which is not so easy for women to take, as for men, and no one ever welcomes the idea of leaving their native land. What most women do is to try and earn their living in any occupaticn they can enter, and though they mar, and do, compete with the men, who can tlarne them ? America is pre-eminently the land of that kind of invert lion which discovers a double use for the same article, and some of our fair visitors from the other side of the pond, have evolved a reversible skirt, which con be turned inside out, and sworn as a different article when desired. Thus'on one side it will do for a golf or walking skirt, with a box pleat at the back, while turned inside ou it will do for a bicycling skirt, the seams bsine Strapped in suitable designs. It is of course made of reversible material, and the idea does not seem a bad one for the seaside, where so many women delight to appear with as many changes as possible. The prophecy that this was to be a summer of =sling has proved correct. Muslin is the wear, weather counting for little, since nothing but a deluge apparently, interferes with its appearance abroad. Judiciously managed, muslin gowns are possible for all but the quite impecunious They can easily be run up at home, and by making the upper and under skirt separately, and treating the honed slip and tintve, in the same way, the average girl can dispense with the laundress or cleaner altogether, '' getting up " her frocks herself, after their journey through the tub. For the underskirt, s last season's linen or cotton gown can well be utilised, the hem being first let down, and hemmed up with the muslin. Then a deep full flounce should be added, and this portion is complete. Next comes the overskirt, which should reach to a little below the flounce in front, and at the sides, and there finish in points or tabs, as fancy may dictate, the back breadths falling to the edge of the underskirt &amp;ounce. This overskirt should be mounted in a separate band, to simplify matters when the " getting up " pro- cess rves necessary. For the slip, the fitted and boned bodices, to he had for a little over 24 of afildrapers, are admirable. When the sleeves are put in, and the neck band round, it is ready for use. The blouse—quite loosely 6tting—should be run up indepeneently, a tack being placed here and there when finished, to keep • /the two together, and give a trim neat effect to be whole. With a dainty fichu ruftl ) of net or chiffon, such a gown can quite easily takr, its place among the many costly creations in evi- dence, that have run their wearers well into two figures, and whose visits to the cleaners must indeed prove an expensive affair. Driving coate, and dust cloaks have been In full force lately, hut that they have, in the majority or instances, been designed for ornament and not for use,is apparent to all. P'rishable to a degree, a shower would reduce many a s^-ealled dust cloak to an unrecognisable mass. One item their wearers must appreciate, however, is the delightful porkets with which they are furnished. Several which made their appearance at Accot boasted Tan leas than fur or five. Modistes are,happy to relate, now permitting slits to be made in overskirts, a real pocket beirg placed in the underskirt !</p>
        <p>6ecni</p>
        <p>" DIADGE."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>AN ELECTION SEQUEL.</head>
        <p>AN ELECTION SEQUEL.</p>
        <p>£250 Damages against Publishers. Before Mr Ju,tice Bruc, and a special jury, on Tuesday, in the King's Bench Division, Mr John William Brian, L.C.C., brought an action against Messrs George S Smith and Co., Limitei, pub- lish-re, to recover damages for an alleged libel. Mr Bean was formerly M.P. fir St George's-in-tbe- Bast, and in October last he was Liberal candidate for the Bermondsey Division. On the eve of the polling day a pamphlet published by the defendants was sent to 11,000 electors containing statements to ti.e effect that he had criminally charged trades unionists for uniting in self-defence, and that he could not legally sit for Bermondsey if elected ow- ing to the judge's decision in the e'ection petition presented against him by Mr Harry Marks. Plaintiff attributed lais defeat to the publication of the pamphlet, which was printed by Meurs M'Cor- quodale,from whom he had already recovered £3OO damages. In the result the jury awarded plaintiff £.4250.</p>
        <p>To lidonszna.-1511rs. Winslow's Socthing Syrup has tie?ii *sod for over fifty years by millions of mothers for their children while teething, with perfect allure's. It will relive the rvorsufferer Immediately It is pleasant to taste produces natural ouiet sleep, oy relieving the child (ruin pa;11 iii the IMu cherub awakes "as brighttis a button." Of all C3emists la lid per bottle. Advt.</p>
