      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 21, 1901</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, FRIDAY, JUNE 21, 1901</p>
        <p>"No, no," said Low; "nothing of the sort. I was only pumping you to see what you were made of. The prisoner is a great friend of mine. I want to gain him some respite, a favour or two." "I understand," said Whitaker, but he winked solemnly all the same. "Good-night, and good luck ' to you, sir." So be would have to go on to Gradwell. That man would have answered Isis purpose well, he thought. He did not envy his cousin's experience under the wardership of such a fellow. The next morning he left the hotel and booked to the lonely penal settlement, where he hoped to discover his cousin. There was little difficulty in finding Samuels, the warder to whom he had the introduction. Samuels was a thin, wiry, crafty- looking man, the kind of man one would expect to sec in the detective department reher than superintending gangs of rough convicts in the stone quarries of Gradwell. He was amenable to the forceful argument of bribery. Whitaker had sent Low to the right man. "Unfortunately," he said, when Low had inquired about Martin, "No. 35 is not at Grad- well. He left three months ago." Low muttered a curse. "Where is he?" be demanded. Then he remembered himself and proffered money. Samuels was not slow to accept. "He's at Smilehurst," he said. "Invalided there. We send all the lame crocks there. I'm going myself in a few weeks. This rough duty takes the very life out of one, and I've been lucky enough to get a change. Perhaps then I can be of use to you. If you'll leave your address I'll write to you when I am transferred and make an appointment." Low fell in with this sugges ion and gave Samuels a London address. He was very anxious to learn Lore of his cousin's complaint. "Was it anything likely to prove fatal ?" "1 can't say it was anything so bad as that," said the warder. "Ilut he was terribly weak when he was transferred here; or, at least, the harder work in the quarries quickly found him out. You see, he had done a bolt at Minewood, and had knocked down a warder into the bargain, and they had been making it stiff for him with the cat ' and solitary; and, not being used to rough treatment, it told on him, I suppose. He looked more dead than alive, I assure you, when he left here. 8t...11 tmilehural Li a wsmkrful place, a jierrea paradise 1F the ' ' and ri the warden, too, according to all accounts, and I daresay he's picked up wonder- fully since he went. So you needn't have any fear about his welfare, sir. And when I go, if there's any little extra kindness I can manage, or get in any delicacy, or a bit, of tobacco, well, I daresay between you and me it can be managed." Low thanked him and smiled grimly. He was hardly taking all this trouble in order to make his cousin's prison lot any lighter; but it might be politic to keep his intentions and desires from Samuels for the present. The warder seemed to have a kindly-disposed heart. He was a fellow sufferer in health, add fellow suffering makes us wondrous kind. Three weeks later Low received a letter with the Smilehurst post-mark upon it. It was from Samuels, and said that he could see Low the following Saturday afternoon in the Bell Inn at Oldport, the little market town close by. Low kept the appointment. "We will go down by the river and talk," said 'Samuels, after they had had drinks together. "It Isn't safe to utter a word here." "Well, how is he ?" asked Low, when they were outside. "Getting on famously," said Samuels, "and he's in my batch. It's a grand place for everybody, is Smilehurst, especially for the lees. Plenty of food, good food, as little or as much exercise as they may happen to be fit for, isith a doctor always following you about with a clinical and chicken broth. By gum! Talk about prison! There's many a poor devil on the streets would be glad to get in here for a menth or two. It's a funny system, this prison system of ours. Down there, at Grad- well, and at Minewood, and all the rest, they beat and work the life out of them. Then they send 'em here to play the high and mighty. It's a runs e. thin, and no mistake." • Issisv had come to the conclusion during the interval between seeing Samuels at Gradwell and now that it would be useless to attempt to bribe this man to any evil compact against the prisoner. Whitaker would have been the man he could have dealt with. But Samuels, despite his crafty, cunning face, which clearly indicated amenability to the subtle influence of coin of the realm, was not to be cajoled or bribed into work of the nature Low desired. A different plan was in his mind now. He went into it at length with Samuels. "What happens if a prisoner does a bolt?" "Catch him, if we can. If he won't stop wo fire over his head by way of giving him warning. If he still runs on we fire to drop him." "And if he is shot dead by accident 7" "There is an inquest. But the governor saves his five hundred pounds, which is about all that matters." "For the sake of supposition, if No. 35 one evening did a bolt and got clear away ?" "It would fare very badly for me. I don't like shooting a man, but I would drop him rather than he should get clear. It would make my life uncomfortable." "Might I ask when you retire with your pension?" "In eight years' time." "A thousand pounds would be a very comfortable retiring allowance? " "I wish to goodness I might see it. I ain't very strong, and the missus has seven kids to look after. It isn't much of a spec' for me, even with ff my pension. "A thousand pounds is yours, and you can retire when you like, if you let convict No. 35 escape." Warier Samuels was silent. It was clear that the offer had impressed him. He was turning it over in his mind. Low forced the matter. "It will be the easiest thing in the world for you. When dusk comes on just as you are taking your prisoners in you overlook No. 35. He does • • bolt. You fire your gun, if you like, over his head. He gets clear away. Before:the rest of the warden have got on the track he has made good his start and elided you. After that if he doesn't get clear off that's not your fault. He may be shot by one of the warders and captured or killed. If so it makes no difference to you. Whatever happens you will get your money," It struck Warder Samuels that this was an easy way of making cash. He might be severely repri- manded, or he might get off scot-free. In any case he would be a gainer. "I am inclined to try it, sir," he said. "What day?" asked. Low, promptly. "I can't say any day particularly," returned the roan. "It's no good trying the game on until the evenings are a bit darker—say, in a month's time. Then the first drizzly evening you may look out for your cousin if he is inclined for the attempt. I'll manar,e a chance for him." "Thank you," said Low. "And now as to the question of terms. "I shall want a pretty good advance to mske sure." "A hundred pounds," said Low. They remained together settling this matter for some time. Then at length they returned to the town, and, in..thprivate room at the Bell, Low drew up a document which both signed. Each knew it was worthless, and Samuels meant to have the greater part of the promised money before he assisted in breaking the prison regulations and risking his positeon and the paltry but certain pension.</p>
        <p>than thirty yards away. He could have sworn tbill he had at least a hundred and twenty yards start, and he knew that he had been travelling quite ns fast as any of the heavily uniformed officers could go. Probably the warder who was so close had been out of bounds on some business, and had returned just in time to cut off his retreat. He turned, and there against the shadow of the first trees he fancied he could see a dark patch of shadow edging along the wood in his direction. Martin deviated a little. With clenched fists, straining every muscle, he sprang forward to the cover of the fir trees. As he entered their grateful gloom another shot whizzed past him. They meant to capture him at all hazards. He had not thought they would have fired at his head, yet both the shots missed his brain by a narrow margin. In and out through the trees he ran, but the man behind, though he did not fire again, stuck doggedly to the trail. He was evidently a good runner, and held on like a bloodhound. Presently Martin reached an open glade running like a road through the heart of the forest. He struck into it like one who knew his way, or at least had studied his programme thoroughly before- hand. Looking back after a time he found that he could, barely distinguish his pursuer. The thought gave him fresh strength, and be increased his speed. If only he could shake off the man behind there would be hope. He had run straight forward until at length the forest came to a sudden termination. There were open fields beyond, and on the top of a little ridge some quarter of a mile ahead he could just descry, outlined against the sky, a shepherd's but. This was his objective. Conscious that he would be more liable to detection in the open he bounded forth from the forest darkness, and, despite the fact that he had already run two good miles, breasted the upland like a deer. Ile had reached the top of the incline and in another moment would have been out of sight when the pursuer emerged from the wood. He was at fault, for Martin had utterly out-distanced him. But as the convict's ill-fortune would have it, the flying man appeared in relief against the sky just at the psychological moment when it was most unfortunate for him. A moment sooner and the traces of him .v7otin have been lost. As it was the human bloodhound, like an avenger of evil, set out with greater vigour than over for the shepherd's hut, behind which Martin had disappeared. He had nearly reached it when he pulled up short. On the other side of the hedge, which divided the field he was crossing from a narrow cart-track, he could hear footsteps. Quickly ho scaled the bank and looked over. It was a false alarm, for the man on the other side of the hedge was an ordinary countryman, the herdsman himself, perhaps, going home from late toil. The man looked up startled as he heard a voice. "Good-night, there. Have you seen a convict up yonder by the house?" "Naw. There ain't no convicts hereabout 'eeptie down at the prison," said the man shuffling along. "But one has escaped. He has gone over the hill, past the hut there. Sure you haven't seen anybody?" "Sure, certain," said the man. rri". come right by there," and the unintelligent yokel went on his war. "Thick-headed idiots they are about here," muttered the man in the field and ran on. "It's lucky for that numskull, perhaps, that he didn't see anybody, or, rather, thathe wasn't seen him- self, or he might have been knocked on the head for the sake of his clothes. He panted over the last dozen yards leading to the hut, and as he gained the top an utterly unexpected sight met his eyes. There, fifty yards ahead of him, running headlong down the slope, was his human quarry. The lighter convict clothes shewed up distinctly against the blackness of the ground. Evidently the convict fancied himself secure for a time, and had stopped to gain breath, when the sudden arrival of his armed pursuer started him forward again. On, on, ran the pair, the fugitive gradually gaining ground, though the man behind stuck steadily to the trail. It was easy to see that die convict was making for the strip of sea to the northward. The lights of vessels making from and to port could easily be seen now. To the right lay the little town and port of Oxenford. Right ahead was the secluded Bernard Bay. The convict headed for the narrow lane that ran down to the water's edge. Over stiles and ditches, breaking through hedge- rows and wading streamlets the pair raced on until at length, with. two hundred yards to the good, the hunted man at length reached the water's edge. He ran along it for a few yards, as if in search of something he had expected to find. Then suddenly he stumbled over a light boat. He was up again in an instant, and in a twinkling had pushed the boat out into the water. Already the crunching of the pursuer's feet could be heard across the shingle. Seizing an oar, the man in the boat pushed out into deeper water. He was reaching down for the other oar, pre- paratory to rowing out to where a dim light hang- ing from a tiny yacht's mast seemed to beckon, when there was a quick flash of light at the water's brink, and the silence of the night was broken by the sharp crack of a revolver. The hunted man, wounded in the back, threw up his arms and dropped limp and inert across the gunwale of his little craft. A moment he hung thus, thr the boat, sagging with the weight, shot out its helpless human cargo into the water and ths sea closed over him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>LADDER OF GUILT.</head>
        <p>THE LADDER OF GUILT.</p>
        <p>BY ITLYSS ROGERS, Author of "For Love and the Queen," "Wedded in Mystery," &amp;a</p>
        <p>CIIA.PTER X.XIX. TLIE GENTLE AET OF BRIBERY.</p>
        <p>"A strange, weird place that prison up there," he said to the host—who also officiated as iole waiter—after breakfast, the first morning at the hotel. "Ever break loose, any of them r • "Well, not much nowadays," said the waiter. "They're getting too strict on them. There was that outbreak a little while ago, you know. One offthem—let me see, what was his name P—Fenwick, I think—gave them a hot time of it for a day or two. But, law lless you, sir, they always get caught" "Oh, yes, I think I remember," said Low. "It was a gang in the charge of a arder named Whitaker that broke loose, I fancy ? " "That's it, sir. We often see Warder Whitaker In here; that is, in the tap. A fine fellow he too, as civil as they make them, and that's saying a great deal for the warders up at Minewood. Would you be thinking of staying any length of time hereabout, because—"</p>
        <p>He hesitated. "Well ? Because what ? " said Low. "Only if you'd like to have a chat with Whitaker or some of the warders,l daresay I could arrange it." "It would be interesting, I've no doubt," said Low. "I think I should like it. I suppose there's no chance of getting over the prison? " "None, sir, I'm afraid. But you can learn a good deal if you only go about it the right way. And, begging your pardon, sir, you won't forget me in any little Commission? I've worked a good many things all right before to-day. You can rely on me." "I am sure I fail to comprehend you What do you mean ? " -- "Any tobacco or anything of the kind. Even a nip of spirits can be managed." • Light beffan to dawn upon Low's mind. "Oh, I ''see. You work together, you and the warders, do you ? " "Well, not exactly that; but if you want any- thing got into the prison I think I can facilitate matters a little if you place the order witth me. And you won't get rocked either. I'll seie after that. Those warders will spy on one another with the eyes of hawks if you once set them going. Host Dobbs was telling nothing but the truth. The fact of the matter was that he was regulally inpplying at least half-a-dozen convicts ‘Ath tobacco at that moment, smuggled in at a guinea a time by the warders. Of course, the friends of the prisoners had to chance how much of the precious commodity ever reached the convict; but, as Dobbs had said, the warders were divided against them- selves over such matters, and held each other in Constant check, so that it was not impossible for an outsider who knew the ropes to ascertain ,if the friend inside had received the tobacco or not. "Thank you," said Low. "I don't know that I want anything smuggled in, but there are five pounds for you if you can get Whitaker into a private room within the next few days and let me have a short chat with him." "It shall be arranged, and to-day, if possible," said Dobbs, and he left to carry out the arrange- ments, mentally resolving that if there were a fiver in the job to commence with there might be more later on, if he'only played his cards properly. He determined that the conversation between his guest and Whitaker should be the property of s third. It was an easy matter to get Whitaker down'to the Highway Hotel. Dobbs arranged it persotally. Putting on his hat he walked up through the village until at length he came to a row of small houses on the very outskirts. At the gate of the third house a woman was standing. "Good-morning, Mrs. Whitaker," he said. "Husband on duty this morning ? "Yes, Mr. Dobbs, and is likely to be until— supper time, I expect. They're very busy up there just now." He knew what that meant. There was overtime duty going on, and one or two of the warders were specially reserved to do a little exercise with the cat," a duty which demanded an extra payment "Ask him to look down to the Highway when he comes in, if you please. Say I should like to speak to him a minute in private, and tell him to come in by the side door." The woman did not undertake to deliver the message with a good grace, for she knew that the mention of the side door indicated another per- sonal risk for her husband. She resolved that she would try and persuade him to have nothing to do with anything that meant bribery and risk, with dismissal and the loss of his pension if found out. Ile had run the gauntlet very closely once or twice already. Whitaker put in an appearance, however, just before nine that evening, and Dobbs took him into the little back parlour before introducing him to his guest. "Mind, Whitaker, this is a good thing. There's money in this, whatever it is. And you won't for- get to make it right with me ?* "I don't know that I'm taking on any job. I 7 ina natnyto hk unrorwy al tiamboautbi fir art gaiust o y ; though, needn't itt l e here' s anything good in it, I'll make it that you haven't lost anything by mentioning my name to your gent upstairs. But I'm not on anything fishy. What price that Tomlinson business you set me on ? A high time I had of it expecting the swop every minute when that young Lawson got out and blowed the gaff to his friends outside. But shew me up." Mr. Whitaker advanced to meet Mr. Low with the air of a man who had come to do sonic great favour. It was an air that Mr. Whitaker could well assume, and he gave a semi-official grunt when wished "good evening." He wished it to be distinctly understood that he was not the man to be easily got at. Low determined at once how to deal with him. .</p>
        <p>....... "It is very good of you, Mr. Whitaker, to give up your time to come and see me. Before we start conversation let me beg your acceptance of that as some compensation for your loss of time in coining here to-night." He handed over a crisp five-pound note and ostentatiously displayed a note-book containing several others. Dobbs in the adjoining room had listened'with his ear to the lath and plaster parti- tion and heard every word. Ile determined to try and find out what the value of the exchange was before Whitaker left. "I may as well come to the point at once," said Low. "There is a prisoner there named Penwick." "We know him as No. 35. We dispense with names in places like that up yonder." "Well, call him what you like. lam interested in the prisoner, lie has escaped once, has he not ? " "Yes, curse him, and got me into heavy trouble. I"</p>
        <p>"I daresay that little inconvenience can be set right. It may be that you can assess the monetary value of it."</p>
        <p>Mr. Whitaker evinced more interest. There was a bit to Is made out of this visitor.</p>
        <p>4ife-11,--what can I do for you ?" he asked, point b'an'. - .</p>
        <p>some men," said Low, in a whisper, seeing that his man could be Lought, "are worth more dead than alive." "That's true," said Whitaker; "but, to tell you the truth, you may spare yourself making any pro. poeals about No. 35 either living or dead." "Why?" "Why, the truth is, he isn't here." "Not here. Dead, do you mean?" "No, removed; nearly twelve months ago.* "Then where is he?" "That's my business. Government always makes a secret of removals. Even the+ men themselves don't know until they get there. But I happen to know where he is. The information has a, price. I should have been glad to have done busincis with you of a more substantial kind, but that's just my i • luck." "Well, I'll give you ten pounds to tell me where he is." Wliitaker laughed. "It's worth twenty at least." "Ten," said Low. "Come now, that s good pay for a slight piece of information like that.* "Twenty, said Whitamkearn,y"laitntdle el d give ve matters you an introduction to a warder, an old pal of mine; who can arrange a good for you, if you remain in the same frame of mind." Eventually Low took out two ten-pound notes and made them over to Whitaker. "lie's gone to Gradwell," said Whitaker. He took ,a pencil out of his pocket and wrote a Couple of lines on a slip torn from a pocket-book. "If you find out Warder Samuels at Gradwell, give him that and you'll be on the right track. A killing job, eh ? I'm not sorry to hays got out .lit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>CHAPTER XXX.</head>
        <p>CHAPTER XXX.</p>
        <p>A RACE FOR LIFE. A It was a dull November evening, and the after- noon had worn into dark storm clouds. The convalescents at Smilehurst were falling into deMch- ments to march in when suddenly away on the north-west a gun was fired. A thrill ran through every convict Ili the' place. They knew what the firing of the gun meant. , Under the shadow of the fast-falling night, taking advantage of the drizzle, a convict had made a dash for liberty. It was quickly made known that it was from Warder Samuel's gang that the prisoner had escaped. Moreover, that above all others •it was that quiet, exceptionally industrious convict, No. 35, that had made the bolt. In double-quick time tite gangs were hurried into the prison and every available officer started out in pursuit. It was known that No. 35 was in for murder. Meanwhile the fugitive, scaling the palissding which enclosed the agricultural holding surrounding Smilehurst, made a bee line for the forest that loomed dark and welcoming right before him. He ran zigzag fashion, for previous experience had taught him what to expect. The first gun had been fired just before he scaled the. stocking. Every moment he expected to hear the second report. It came at last, but, to his surprise, instead of coming from the rear, it came from his left. It missed him but he felt the slish of the bullet in the air. Then the warders were closing in upon him. The shot could not have been Bred more</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>CHATTER XXXI.</head>
        <p>CHATTER XXXI.</p>
        <p>ALARMING INTELLIGENCE.</p>
        <p>Grace Hendon was in search of another situation, Indignity after indignity had been heaped upon her at Albert Rise, until at length the. children, taking the text from the mother's tone, had helped in the tantalising insult. One day a word of reproof from Grace had resulted in a vicious kick from the eldest boy of about eight. It was an action that called for vigorous and prompt correction, and for this Grace appealed to Mrs. Groves. For reply she was laughed at and told that it served her right; whilst the youthful boor, plucking up his courage at this, administered tmother and a harder kick with his heavy-nailed boot. This was more than flesh and blood could bear. Grace seized the offender by the nape of his neck, and, fearless of the mother's presence, gave the young rebel the first salutary lesson he had ever had in his life. It was all done on the spur of the moment, and, almost before she had realised it, the energetic Mrs. Groves, flying to the rescue, had snatched the youthful reprobate from the governess's hands, and with a forceful push sent Grace out of the front door and turned the key on her. Her box followed its owner during the course of the day, and Grace that night fell back niacin the dreary Clapham lodgings. The next day she renewed the old search for employment.</p>
        <p>Every morning she scanned the newspaper at an adjacent free library, and during the day called at the addresses where governesses were wanted. On the morninor, of the third day of her search her eye was suddenly arrested by a report in the news columns. It was headed "Escape of a convict at Smilehurst," and ran: "Last night, just as the convict gangs were returning to their quarters for the night at Smile- hurst, a prisoner named Martin Fenwick made a successful dash for liberty. He was in the gang of 'Warder Samuels, working nearest the forest which covered his retreat. All the available staff at the prison and a number of constabulary are on the search for him, for, as will be remembered, Martin Fenwick was the murderer who secured the life sentence owing to the extraordinary refusal of the drop to act at his execution. He is a dangerous and desperate man, and has broken gaol before." All thought of her search for employment was banished from Grace's mind upon reading this exciting intelligence. Her lover was free again. Where was he? What was he doing? Had he got clear this time? What were his sufferings ? She lifted up a prayer to Heaven that God would help the "dangerous and desperate man" who had escaped the last penalty. Each morning she scanned the newspapers, hoping that Martin would not be found, yet dread- ing the worst. It was nearly a week before there was any really tangible news to give the public. Then one morning her eyes caught sight of a para- graph which took every particle of colour out elf her face. Her hands trembled as she held the paper and forced herself to read on to the bitter end. Then the room spun round her, her limbs lost every particle of strength, and she reeled so that she would have fallen had not a bystander held her in his arms. ( To be continued.)</p>
