      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>38, 40, &amp;, 42, BASINIETT-STREET, LIVERPOOL.</head>
        <p>38, 40, &amp;, 42, BASINIETT-STREET, LIVERPOOL.</p>
        <p>A LOCAL STEAMER SUNK OFF SOUTHPORT.</p>
        <p>The Crew Saved. Hairbreadth Escape.</p>
        <p>On Friday the steamer Blanche, belonging to Mr W Rowland, 9, Canning-place, Liverpool, and Rencorn, and commanded by Captain Brough, sank five miles from the Nelson Buoy, which is opposite Southport, in what 13 estimated to be twenty fathoms of water. She was proceeding from Barrow-in-Furness to Liverpool with a cargo of grave!, which is said to have shifted. Tremendous seas were encountered, and these burst the hatches. The water poured in at a fast rate, and the Grew, numbering nine, some of whom belong to Liverpool, had to take to one of the boats. The infle w of the sea caused the vessel to heel over. For over six I hours the crew had a terrible experience, and to. begin I with some of them had narrow escapee from drowning on leaving the vessel. Bw:kets had constantly to be kept going to keep the boat afteit Ultimately they landed opposite Ainsdale. The captain was injured in the region of the chest by being struck with an oar. A further report states that the exact position where the Blanche floundered is about six miles west of the Nelson buoy, which is eight miles from Southport. Altogether it is thirteen miles from the land. She shipped a heavy sea, which caused the cargo to shift, and the boat to list. The hatches burst, and she completely capsized. Just previously the crew took to the boat, in which they remained from ten o'clock in the morning till nearly six at night. After beaching the boat opposite Ainsdale the crew had to pass throegh a long stretch of surf. Angry waters repeatedly knocked them down.and how they escaped is a miracle. At Southport the men sought the advice of Chief Constable Elliott, who handed them over to Mrs Bolden, who acts on behalf of the Shipwrecked Mariners' Aid Society. A watch has been well kept on Southport shore during the gale, but nothing was ven of the boat. The names of the crew are : Captaie Brougb, Ulverston ; mate, William Parkinson ; Liverpool; chief engineer, T Ashworth ; second, H Hitzginson, Rock Ferry ; A B.'s, J Wilson ano J Fell, Ulverstion, J Williams, Ellesmere Port, and two firemen (-ernes unknown), who have returned home. The vessel was built by M'Keight's, of Ayr, and valued at between £6,000 and £7,000. The Crew left for Liverpool.</p>
      </div>
