        <head>Annual Meeting of the Wycliffe Society.</head>
        <p>Annual Meeting of the Wycliffe Society.</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. Society on day afternoon, the reports for the past year were mitted and the election of officers proceeded with. ItPl7 Yonge presided. gr 4</p>
        <p>THE SECRETARY'S RETORT. Mr B Faanate (bon secretary), said it gave him pleasure to Present the eighth annual report. Society could not now b • considered an experimeni an institution which offered facilities for fellow: communion, brotherhood and worship. The meet</p>
        <p>'created a fellow feeting;and sympathy one with am It was also a channel of communication of their ti he and difficulties as well as their joys and happiness. + ,rtit ' fact of brotherhood made them feel homely. Their fir' and foremost desire had always been, and was still re fill that church with men on Sunday afternoons t°,l hear the gospel of Christ. They had not yet reatbe the fulfilment of that desire. There were large nc,ru; bees of men around them who did not go anywhere 13 worship on Sundays, and the P.S.A. gave thema ef opportunity of trying to bring these to the meetitiasi He believed the future of the brotherhood for uStli . and aggressive work depended largely upon thematebr`Ai under God's blessing. It they made up their adorn, that the society should grow in numbers and usefulnM they would succeed in accomplishing these desirsi, ends. To leave this work to a few was a mistake. 1.130 them all hear the burden and the responsibility of t„ work. N4thing in this life would so much cheer us : rescuing the perishing and lifting up the tall" and how much of this could be done the society they could not tell. The meetingq 1r ' been conducted under God's guidance and blear - The year closed with an enrolled membership of with an average attendance of 330. For the year PP" views, the numbers were 614 with an averge ettee.. once of 310. It would be seen that while the rnentiterels VMS rather less, the average attendances were a !ILI, better. During the year 634 peva had been gainede7ti In the first six months, and 313 during the second fro months, making a total of 7,833 nrizes during the eig„fc years' existence of the society. A large number of eererlot certificates had been given for regular t.ttendance. ...: sick club was still quietly working with satlBl6ctor4 results,and at the close of the year a division was madly; the members. The secretary of the club urgently cif vited the members of the PSA to join them, %at numbers now stood at 67. It was ploasing to the membership membership fur the present year was gr''''oo than it was twelVo months ago, it log standing at 437 or 21 more than at tho same period 00, year. At the simultaneous mission, some of the °lays hers stood up for Christ. The committee felt that. 0 , evil of intemperance was a great stumbling bloclg to many members. It made one almost tremble et temptations put in their way by the drink traffic ',,i the misery brought upon them and their fainiliesio vtl. their fall. They were delighted to welcome bsetr reek their midst, R Irons and J Buckley who had from South Africa, and Walter Newton, from Aldera,lle'Js: where he had been at the head of the military Itbe Two of their members—Savory and Cliff had 1010 tar regular army and had gone out to India. The en,,,,,aitil proceeded to refer to the losses by death and Mace l.t.'-oto mention of the dece,se of Mr .1 Gough, Mr Lonfls„3-r1 and Mn J Irish. lin conch/don he urged the metatle to spare no effort to brlng as much sunshine iallaif homes of Warrington as possible. There were things in the past hi6tory of the Society which b- them cause. tor encouragement. 'tate' Mr R Aarersseti aubmittt-d the annual financial 0-06 ment, which showed that the income for the year oee £l4l 2s 4d. Th 9 year commenced with a debit balltsoa of £l3 8,3 111, and cl^se.d with a balance at the 13 amounting "o £2 54 3d. Mr Mitts, in submitting the absentee visitors' rePs a stated that 382 visits had bees paid by the member the Committee. e 0 Mr tiAWTHIRN presented the account of the Bee ,ed , lent Fund, which showed that 15 cases had been reli.e: of . to the extent of £2 16i Si, leaving a balance in hacc 0 , 103 lid. He asked members to report deserving c. , to the Committee. ,ed, . Rev. JOHN YONGE, in commenting upon the rePe—a said the secretary had given them On the Vlbr:lbs O. cheerful and encouraging report. It was true that of membership was not so large as in the early yerloat the El-tclety but there was still much to encourage lt hip I. the work. Ile believed tbat a larger membere to. could be obtained if individual members would alseif themselves missionaries for the P.S. A. amongst to„ho fellow workers. Mr. Yonge proceeded to refer to t.d value of the Visiting done by the Visiting CommitteessPot hoped that this department of work would not fail ' I lack of helpers. 1 WA The reports were adopted on the proposition Of I Riley. `I</p>
