      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>Meeting of the Parish Council. raeetiog of the Stockton Heath Parish Council waa held in the National Schoolroom, Stockton Heath On Tuesday evening. Mr Mason presided and there were also present :—Messrs Clarke, Pearson, Mclntyre, Fair- tdUrst,Pemberton,and Whitley. The clerk (Mr Bradshaw) Was unable to be present. ~."AIIRINGTON AND NORTHWICH LIGHT RAILWAY. kihairmain reported that Mr Clarke and himself a.Ltendedthe e Commissioners, Light Railway inquiry at Northwich, and t after an inquiry extending over aseven hours, practically stated that they would recom- Mend the Order to be passed. Mr Pemberton : On condition that the Light Rail- way promoters made an arrangement with the Ship Canal Company. The Chairman There were many conditions. Mr Pemberton : Earl Jersey pointed out that the Promoters had no power to compel the Ship Canal. People to allow them to go over the bridge. It was the &amp;atilt in the Order that they had not aoy compulsory De, Were. They were entirely at the mercy of the Snip kanal people. THE WARRINGTON CORPORATION TRAMWAYS. A STOCKTON HEATH GRIEVANCE.</p>
        <p>The Chairman said the Council had to consider what Otiort they would take in connection with the Corpora- tion scheme for electric tramways. Mr Pemberton said from the plans submitted at the 'ecent inquiry the Warrington Corporation only pro- Posed to take the trams to toe Saracen's Head Hotel. he Council had always understood that they would ':itne to the bridge, to Scaff ord-road, and the Town Clerk said they had the power, which they intended to 138, to stop the 'buses twining into Stockton Heath. It "atilt% he (Mr Pemberton) contended, be a great hard • 114iD to the Stockton Heath people to have to walk from ~,.. rlio Saracen's Head to Red-lane Bridge, and the top of 4:-'ilPpenhall-road, which would be half or Oar&lt; equar- eiof a mile away. Toe Parish Council ought to ask fjf Local Government Board, if they granted the loan r: the Wilderspool trains, to put a proviso in to pre- the Corporation from stopping the Stockton Heath th'ges until they had come to some arrangement to run trams as far as the Mulborry Tree. It would be a reat drawback if the tramways were only made to the 'tracen's Head and the 'buses t +ken off. ;Mr Waitley did not thi dr the Board of Trade could interfere in such a matter. Mr Pearson thought the Parish Council ought to meet the Corporation in a friendly wav to endeavour to come to terms to do away with all this scheming to force their hand or anyone else's hand. He did not think the Corporation would be hard at all. It was plain that the benefit to the Corporation of coming into Stockton Heath would be as great as to the Council, The Chairman: No doubt. Mr Whitley: That is my opinion. Mr Pearson : I want to know whether it is true the Corporation intend to stop at the place named? _ Pensbeston ;If you had attended the inquiry you !Mild have seen the plans. Continuing, he said that !!!.e proposed that the Parish Council approach the Local trOvernment Board and ask thorn not to allow the Cor- Doration to atop the Stockton Heath 'bus service 412ti1 they could offer them one equally as good at the Battle price. ,After further dise.ussion Mr Pemberton proposed the following resolution t—" That we petition the Local u,,ci„vernment Board not to authorize the loan for the ••nderspool Tramway unless the Corporation will rse to continue the licence for the 'bus service to toektou Heith as at present existing, or until a tram- Way IS substituted to give an equal advantage." Mr Mclntyre seconded. r Mr Whitley moved an amendment that a deputation. 'Mtn the Council be appointed to meet the Corporation 12,nd discuss the matter with them. He felt sure that if t!?ist were done the Corporation would meet them in a different spirit from what they did on the previous occasion. Penolonton thought it would be better to approach the Local Government Board who would give them better terms. He had had a great deal to do with the Corporation lately. (Laughter.) Now was the time for the Parish Council to open their mouths or "for ever hold their peace." (Hear, hear.) The Chairman said he did not think anything was to be gained by going to the Corporation. Mr. Clarke did not believe the Corporation had poser I to stop the 'bus traffic ; it was not practicable. They might have the power to regulate it and grant the licences, but to say they gould stop the 'bus service from Warrington to Stockton Heath was monstrous and bluff on their part. He thought so when he read the • teport of the inquiry at Warrington in the newspapers. R e did not fear for a moment the threat would be (tarried out. , There was no seconder to the amendment and it fell to the ground. The original resolution was then put and carried. 411 Angler's Visit to Northwich and its Sequel. t Sohn Atuerton, who said he came from Warring- ftlat was charged at the North wich Police Court,on „40nday, before Messrs J Weston and GB Cliff,with Ving obscene language in London-road, at 10.30 on 'Ainclay night. P.O Inglesfield gave evidence, and when he spoke to the prisoner he sat down on the footpath and still:Continued to use the language e.2nlplained of. The prisoner said he had come to the town with an angling society, and got a little 00 MilCh drink. He had never been to N nthwich before. The Bench ordered the prisoner to pay a Rue of 10s. United Alkali Company's Appeal Dismissed. In the Court of Appeal, OU Tuesday, the hearing was te2timed of the case of the St. Helens Corporation v the .Ittited Alkali Company Limited, on the application of ,he defendants for judgment or new trial on appeal, it3trt a verdict and judgment, dated December 19th at a trial before Mr Justice Sucknill, and a special JurY, at Liverpool. Mr Elorridge, K.C., following Mr J. Walton, K.C., for the appellants, submitted that nefen• ants had no reason to believe that potassium chlorite nil likely to explaie.—Mr Danckwertz, K.C., for the ✓espondents said that his learned friends could not deny 14t1,d had, in fact, admitted that chlorate of p3tash, /..uen it did explode, was a dangerous substance. did explode in this case, and there it was clear that chlorato of potash by itself, in fact, a dangerous explosive. The learned Counbel then proceeded to argue that the 'l2nditions under which the chlorate of potash was ;50red were highly improper, and that sufficient precau- Z" to guard against fire had not been taken. The a ;341ter of the Rolls, in giving judgment, said there was th„^,d9 of evidence called in this case which warranted Jury in finding that although the defendants did not now that potassium chlorate unmixed was dangerous 1, ought to have known it. That being so, he could ✓Bee how it could be argued with success that this e,siti.loaion was too remote a consequehce of the fire to leVtia the plaintiffs to recover. In his opinion the tifftsned judge rightly entered judgments for the plain- the finding of the jury, and the appeal must be "'llssed with the costs.—The Lords Justices COW. Cunt d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>HANDSOME RESULT. • Last day of the Bazaar at Stockton Heath.</head>
        <p>A HANDSOME RESULT. • Last day of the Bazaar at Stockton Heath.</p>
        <p>The churchpeoPle in Stockton Heath who have worked so enthusiastically to ensure the success of the bazaar and open-air fête which was held last week-end in order to raise the nucleus of a fund to provide the stipend of a permanent curate for St Thom is' Church have every reason to feel eminently satisfied with the result of the undertaking. As was stated in our last issue the amount realized on the opening day was £352, including several donations. Friday was another successful day, £3llO being raised, but the crowning day of all was Saturday, the proceeds of the day's business being £490. The total amount raised is there- fore £1,182, and it speaks volumes for the untiring efforts of the workers in connection with the bazaar, that this sum was realised. The weather on Saturday was ideal, with the result that the pleasantly situated grounds of Mr Henry Greenall, in which the bazaar was taeld, were inundated with visitors, a large number of whom came from Warrington. The re-opening cere- mony was performed by Mrs J Charlton Parr,i Mr Henry Greenall presiding. In opening the proceedings The Chairman explained the object of the bazaar, and said it was unnecessary to enlarge upon the necessity for a permanent curate, as they all knew how rapidly the population of the parish had increased during the past few years. Continuitii,, he said that between that moment and closing time they meant to raise £BOO, and then they would have reached the sum they had been aiming at all along. (Applause). He begged to thank those connected with other denominations for the kind assistance rendered by them during the continu- ance of the bazaar. (Hear, hear.) It was now his pleasing duty to introduce Mrs. Charlton Parr, if that was necessary, to those present. She was the wife of their County Council representative, and whom they had returned for a considerable time without opposition. (Applause). Mrs. Parr, who was warmly received, said it was very kind of them to have asked her to open the bazaar, and she had great pleasure in doing so. At this late stage of the proceedings she could only say that she hoped the receipts would take the same line as the weather. She trusted, therefore, that the receipts would be even better that afternoon than they had been on the two preced- ing days, and that Mr and Mrs Greenall might be amply rewarded for the kindness they had shown in connec- tion with the arrangements for the b.zo.r. (Applause.) A hearty vote of thanks was accorded to Mrs Parr for her services, and business again commenced. The various amusements were again liberally patron;e3d. The concerts in the billiard room were again a popular feature, and the outdoor attractions provided enter- tainment for young and old alike. The Volunteer band was in attendance, and under the able conductorship of Bandmaster Richardson rendered selections in their usual effective style. The proceedings were brought to a close about ten o'clock and there was a concensus of opinion that, considering the wretched weather experi- enced on Thursday, the result of the bazar was most ncouraging.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>ST. HELENS EXPLOSION. The Corporation and the United</head>
        <p>THE ST. HELENS EXPLOSION. The Corporation and the United</p>
        <p>Alkali Company.</p>
        <p>-- Hearing in the Appeal Court. In the Court of Appeal on Monday, before the Master of the Rolls and Lords Justices Vaughan Williams and Stirling, the hearing was commenced of the case of the Mayor, k 3 , of St. Helens v. the United Alkali Com- nany on appeal by the defendants from a verdict and judgment given in the action • brought by the plaintiffs tor damages for injury done to the gasworks and other , property by a fire and explosion, which tolk place at the defendants' works in May, 1899. The defendants now asked for a new trial on the ground that judgment was improperly entered for the plaintiffs upon the findings of the jury,and,further,that the findings were against the weight of evidence. Mr J Walton K.C., and Mr Horridge, LC., appeared in suppert of the appeal, which was opposed by Mr Datickwerts, K.C., Mr Clarke Hall, and Mr Hutchinson. Mr Walton said that the learned judge had upon cer- tain findings by the jury entered judgment for the plaintiffs, who sought to recover damages for injury caused to their gas works and other property by a fire and explosion which took place at the defendants' works in May, 1299. The defendants were a chemical manufacturing company, and they had a number of works. They manufactured among other things chlorate of potash, and the place where the explosion took place was known as the Kurtz Works at St Helens. The Master of the Rolla :—What was the cause of the explosion according to you. . Mr Walton :—lt seemed that the theory was an extra- ordinary fierce lire, and that it was the intense heat which caused the explosion. Lord Justice Vaughan-Williams said the jury seemed to have thought that, although chlorate of potash was not dangerous, and that although the defendants could not have been expected to have anticipated that it was dangerous, yet they ought to have known that even un- mixed it might explode from external causes other than by admixture—to wit, by the heat of fire. Mr. Walton said they muse have assumed that. The learned counsel, proceeding, said that the learned judge had held that it was the duty of a manufacturer who knew that a thing which by itself was harmless, but yet, when mixed with other substances, was highly dangerous, to take the gieatest possible care that such a possible mixture could not be brought about. There- (the judge) did not say that the del en- to expect that a fire in the tankroom dfoaruet,9ahltandough b reasone would necessarily cause an explosion, yet he con- sidered there was sufficient evidence to justify the conclusion of a jury that the defendants did not take all reasonable care, with the knowledge they Shad that if a fire took place some admixture of chlorate of potash , might occur which would cause an explosion. He (Mr Walton) submitted that the judgment which was given by the learned judge was not justified by the findings of the jury, and teat the application for a new trial ought to be granted. Mr Elorridge, following on the same side, Said that his clients accep' el the finding of the jury as to negligence with respect of the fire. The Master of the Rolls.—Then what you rely upon is that the damage which was caused by the explosion was too remote. Mr Horridge sail that was so. The damage caused by the fire was cot.fined to the defendants' works only, and did not kill anyone ; but the explosion was dins. Crone in its results. It shattered thousands of panes of glass, did great injury to buildings, and caused loss of life. Now, nobody but chemists knew that in certain circumstances chlorate of potash of itself would explode, and that was only in the laboratory, and certainly nobody expected that in the present case an explosion would take place. Even Mr Best, the chemical manager of one of the defendants' works, had stood looking into the storeroom when the fire had firm hold of the build- ings, and be w s not likely to have run the risk attend- ant upon the knowledge that an explosion was likely to occur. The hearing was adjourned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>'IIIE EXAMINEE, FRI! AY, JUNE 21 1901</head>
        <p>'IIIE EXAMINEE, FRI! AY, JUNE 21 1901</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. The Condition of Sankey Brook.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council was held at Bewsey Chambers on Wednes- day afternoon, Mr J.ahua Ball presiding. There were also present—Messrs J W Kuowles, Houghton, Dr Murray, Halton, Brooks, Llngton,Mather, Forshaw and Backhouse with Dr Sephton (medical offic.a), F Kerfoot (inspector), B S Newcombe (surveyor), J C Sutton (clerk), and S Sutton (assistant clerk). MEDICAL OFFICEIeS REPOIIT.—THE EPIDEMIC OF MEASLES. Dr SEPEITON reported that during the month there had been 23,births and 17 deaths. The death rate from all causes was Equal to 16.8 .gainst 86, the average of the past three m.,nths, and 17 for the corresponding month of last year. Owing to a further spread of the epidemic of measles at, Croft, he had ordered the B mrd Sc.:tic) )1s to be ekeed for a fortnight.</p>
        <p>Mr ROUGIITON asked if the epidemic was still extendiag. Dr SEPIITON said it</p>
        <p>Mr HOUGHTON: You may even have to prolong the closing of tho school? Dr SEPLITON said there was a probability. He would do so if tucessary• The report was adopted. SCARCITY OF WATER AT HOUGHTON GREEN.</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR reported twit the occupiera of houses situate at Houghton Green complained bitterly of scar- city of water for domestic use owing to the wells being dried up by the new water shaft which is being sunk by the Corporation at Myddleton. In one case the Corpora- tion bad supplied a farmer with a water cart and given him permission to obtain a supply from the water mains This appeared to boa matter for the property owners to deal with for he was given to understand they are pro- tected by a clause in toe Act of Parliament obtained by the Corporation. The CLERK advised the Council that the Act of Par- liament did not enable them to interfere so long as the Corporation were not exceeding their powers under the Act. When they found that there was no water for the peeple they would have to:provide it for thern,at the cost of the parish. Mr MATHER said there would not be much difficulty in doing this at Houghton Green, because the Corpora- tion mains were close by. Had the Infectious Diseases , Hospital been erected there the mains would have been extended at once. ' Mr BALTON : It ought Co have gone there. (Laugh- ter). Alter some discussion the Surveyor was instructed to make inquiries and preseat a report at the next Council Meeting.</p>
        <p>MORE AB )UT SANKEY neonc.—" THE STENCH INEVITABLE."••••AN INT MERABLE NUISANCE</p>
        <p>A letter was received from Mr Joseph Smith, clerk to the Great Sankey Parish Council sating that at a meeting of that authority he was instructed to write to the Council and the Corporation respecing Sankey Brook and the nuisance caused by the smell arising therefrom and Mr Halton wag r, quested to again bring the matter before the Co u ncil.l: had been sugg-st-d that the Rural Council and the Town Council snout(' take up the matter jUntly, so he enclosed a c)py of the lettPr which he had sent to the Town Clerk on the subject. Tne Clerk also real the letter which Mr Smith had written to the Town Clerk. In this communi• cation it was mentioned that four letters had been received within the past few days, containing very strong complaints, and verbal complaints were being made daily. Mr W D Houghton, Sankey Wire Mills, Mr Sloane, Sankey White Lead Works, Colonel Ridgway • rid others ha i called upou the Council to make every endeavour to bring about some remedy which would relieve the district from the nuisance and danger. Attention was called to the fact that the brook was polluted to a very great extent. The Parish Council I.f-quested the Town Council to take such meas- ures u; would prevent a continuance of this most , intolerable nuisance. . . .</p>
        <p>Mr HALTON made some remarks on the subject. At the outset he was at a loss Co know why the brook should be called Sankey brook. It was detrimental to the interests of building in Great Sankey.ello one would come and build there when they heard of Sankey brook It ought to be called Warrington and Sankey brook, half of it being in the borough of Warrington. Continu- ing he said it was no doubt known by the majority of the membersf that the Corporation had considered this matter time after time but they never seemed Co make any headway. The CLitax : They talk about it. Mr HALTON : That is all. Continuing, he wished it to be understood that the brook was polluted by being drained into every two or three hundred yards between Winwick and Warrington, and he thought this was a scandalous shame. The Corporation must know about it. He took it that that Ceuncil had the power to stop the nuisance, but be thought it would be better to work In conformity with the Warrington Corporation and see if they could not prevent a certain amount of sewage being emptied into the brook at St Helens,Earles- town and other place; without getting crossed with the Corporation. The brook was in an abominable conditioa at the present time, and people could not live in the neighbourhood. Mr Halton went on to speak about Whittle Brook, which was frequently mentioned at the Local Government Board inquiry in connection with the pro- posed small-pox hospital. He said the Council ought to put their fcot down firmly and make the Coraoration pipe the brook the whole length, despite their new system of drainage. They did not want a big sewage scheme to be forced on Sankey. At the Local Govern- ment Board inquiry the Town Cleric said the Corpora- tion would not pipe the brook. The CLERK : You would be obliged to get the con- sent of the land owners. Mr HALTON : We could get it, lam sure. Netarly three hundred head of cattle drink from the brook at different periods of the year. Reverting to Sankey Brock be moved that the Clerk be requested to write to the Town Clf rk with a view to obtaining the co-opera- tion of the Corporation in the matter. The CLERK said that for twenty-five years, as Clerk of Sanitary Authorities, he had had experience in endeavouring to do something to ameliorate the state of things existing at Sankey Brook, but so far they had not been able to do anything at all. A Local Govern- ment Board inquiry into the condition of the brook took place, but nothing came of it. The Local Government Board were disposed to do all they could in the matter, but there was one great difficulty in regard to the smell. There was a great deposit of chemical refuse at St. Helens that nothing could remove, and th• effect of which would not be removed for a hundred years to Come. When the rain fell it palsied through this great sediment into the brook, The stench was inevitable so , long as that exiited. With regard to the pollution they I were all guilty, there was no doubt about that. The ; different authorities on the line poured their sewage into the brook and into the brooks that communicated with Saakey brook. It vi as uaed-es for the members of the Corporation to discuss the matter in the spirit that they were innocent people, because they were contributors to the nuisance. Mr Miami: Then we ought to atop the Corporation The CHAIRMAN: We are guilty. Dr SEPHTON said the first question to decide was - whether they considered Saukey Breok was a river. If It was they had a right Cu call upon people to obey the Rivers Pollution Act and not pollute the otOok. On the other hand lilt was a common sewer, which it v. ould evidently be proved to be as so many kiJuies drained into it, they had a perfect right to use it. Mr Houcarrow : It is a common sewer by long usage. Mr EIAL•roe then mentioned that at the Local Govern- ment Board inquiry in connection with the proposed Smallpox Hospital the Town Clerk made a statement that the Rural Council was in favour of toe hospital being erected at Sankey. The ASSISTANT CLERK was present and said that the Council offered no opposition to it, which looked as if they were working in the right direction. He thought it was radically wrong, as no discussion took place at the previous Council meetings. The matter stiould have been thrashed out so that things should be in woming order when they were represented at the inquiry. The CLERK said the suLject did come uefore the Coca cil and it was decided to put no obstacles in the way. Mr HALTON: Would it not have hem bdtter not to have put in an appearance at the inquiry. The CLERK pointed out that the toorporation would make arrangiments to accommodate small-pox eases arising in the Council's district, and to avoid the risk of the Council being called upon to provide a small- pox hospital they felt that unless they had some strong grounds of opposition for sanitary reasons, they must not oppsse the erection of the hospital. The very first ease of small-pox that arose in the great epidemic of three or four years ago was in weir district and coot about £7O. Mr Harmon contended that the CJunc•l should not have been represented at the incidiry vitamin a resolu- tion was passed to that effect.</p>
        <p>The mater then dropped. Mr HOUGHTON asked it Sankey brook was any worse than it was twenty years ago. Mr EIALTON iti twice as nad. It was ultimately decided that the Medical Officer,</p>
        <p>Surveyor anti Inspector, should make in quiri- s and report at the neat meeting. THE MANCHESTER PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY. Messrs A BROOKS and W. HOUGHTON who have repre- sented the Council in this matter reported that they attended a meeting at the Town Hall, Warrington, on Saturday morning last. Representatives were present from th 3 Council, the Corporations of Warrington and Widnes that Rural District Cluncils of Runcoro,Whiston Wirrall and Chester. The Town Clerk of Eccles (Mr Hickson) was unable to be present and his Councii's attitude in the matter was doubtful. The Clerk of the Rural District Council of Barton upon Trivet! was also unable to bl present but from the reports re- ceived it appeared that the authority was willing to join in the °pl.() .ition. It appeared to them (Messrs Brooks and Houghton) most advisable to oppose the confirmation of the Order in Parliament, and they recommended the Council to jl'n the Corporation of Warrington and Rural Daudet Councils of Runcorn, Wirral!, and probably Bar ton-upon-Irwell in opposition. The Cnester Rural District Council was taking no action and the Widnes Corporation and the Whiston Rural District Council contemplated opposing upon their own account. _ _</p>
        <p>TOo ASSISTANT.cLFUK reported that th Town Clerk of Warrington bad written to him stating that the Runcorn Rural Diserict CJuacil had now refusoul to oppose the Order. Ai it was arranged at Saturday'd conference that if any authority refused to j nu in with the opposi. bon no further tteps should be taken, -the Warrington Corporation was nov, taking independent action in the matter. '</p>
        <p>It was decided that that Mr Brcolto and the Clerk attend before the Parliamentary committee ta •watch the interests of the C</p>
        <p>WINWICH ASYLUM SEWAGE-AN IMPORTANT QUESTION.</p>
        <p>The CLERK reported that he wrote to the Lao cashire Asylums Board with reference to their proposal with regard to the sewage from the new Winwick Asylum He informed the Board that their suggestion to connect their sewer with the sewer which had been provided at Winwick was altogether unpractic- able. It would be, utterly ruinous to the ist,i t to deposit in the brook the large amount of sewage which would be accumulated when 2,000 lunatics and the staff which would be employed, came to the Asylum. 'lhe brook ran in the midst of a district where the popula- tion %ould no doubt increase more rapidly than it had done in the past. In reply he received a letter stating that if the Council would submit an alternative scheme of dealing with the Asylum sewage the Board would be prepared Co consider it. Commenting upon the question Mr Sutton said the Asylums Board and their officials were endeavouring to impose a great responsibility upon the Council. A responsibility which they would no doubt willingly un- dertake if they could practically carry out the scheme. It seemed to him that it was absolutely impossible to expect that they would be permitted to turn all the sewage Into the brook. If the Council permitted them to connect their sewer with the Council's sewer and pour that immense amount of sewage into the district, it would in his opinion be an evil of great magnitude. After some discussion the Clerk was instructed to write to the Asylums Board informing them that the Cott ocil had no alternative scheme to suggest. This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>WATER POLO. Warrington Defeat St. Helens.</head>
        <p>WATER POLO. Warrington Defeat St. Helens.</p>
        <p>Protest Raised.</p>
        <p>Teams representing Warrington and St. Helens met in a Liverpool and District League match at the War- rington Corporation Baths on Tuesday evening. This was the opening league contest rot the season, and there was a large attendence of spectators to witness the en- gagement. Botb clubs were well represented, entering the bath as follows .—St. Helene, Mann- ing, goal ; Houghton and Smith, full backs; Walker half back ; Lomax, Burrows and McKay, forwards. Warrington, Robertson, goal ' • Boscow, and Lowe, fullbacks ; C Ashton, half back; Hughes, W Ashton and W Sutton, forwards. Walter Ashton, who has just returned from South Africa with the Warring- ton Volunteers, made his reappearance with the home team, and played a splendid game. Mr Finnegan. of Liverp iol was the reteree. During the first half War- rington defended the deep end, and at the onset ass- umed the 'aggressive. During the first few minutes Walter Ashton dribbled nearly the whole length of the bath and registered the first goal for Warrington. Shortly afterwards C Ashton made a good shot, the ball passing over the cross bar. St Helens now attacked and Burrows equalised. For some irregularity St Helens were awarded a penalty and Lomax scored. Half-time arrived with the score St Helens two goals, Nvarringten one goal. On resuming the Warrington men invaded the visitors' quarters and Hughes notched another goat from short range. The homesters continued to press and Ashton sent in a capital shot, but the ball rebounded. Hughes, however, was on the alert and again scored. Matters were now even until the close, time being called with the score,Warrington three goals, St Helens two. St Helens lodged a protest against W Ashton playing, on the groand that he did not sign on early enough to qualify him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CIRCUIT. Quarterly Meeting.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN WESLEYAN CIRCUIT. Quarterly Meeting.</p>
        <p>The June quarterly meeting of the Runcorn Wesleyan Circuit was held at the Halton-road Schoolroom on Tuesday last. There was a very large attendance of representatives from the several societies, who were entertained to tra by Mr and Mrs J R Frith, and to whom at the close a very hearty vote of thanko was ten- dered for their hospitality. We were glad to see at tea an old Runcorn Methadisc—the 11&gt;v Henry Lewic—who 38 years ago entered upon the work of the ministry. The business meeting was bold immediately after tea, the chair being taken by the Rev A Wells, superinten- dent, who was supported by his colleagues 'ttie Revs Morrison, and J B Foster), and Niessis B H Posnett, arid J M Frith (circuit stewards). The financial statement as presented by the senior steward, Mr R U Posnett, was of a highly gratifying character. N otwithstanding the loss of two Sunday offertories in each place of wor- ship devoted ti spacial objects outside the circuit, the income was £176 74 2d, being £34 5.1 in excess of the expenditure. The circuit accounts were healthy and showed a balance on the right side of £95. —The numerical statement presented by the chairman I was none the less gratifying, for notwithstanding the heavy loss by deaths, removals, &amp;c, the returns showed a membership of 741, being an Increase of 1 ;with 102 persons on trial for membership i . and 2y3n junior society classes. The number returned on trial was with one exception the largest ever reported to the quarterly 'sleeting since the division of the circuit in 1872 —At this meeting the Superintendent is requ,rel to submit a statement showing the amounts contributedtothe various connectional funds during the preceding year. In presenting this report Mr Wells Rave an interesting address upon the matter. For connectional purposes there had been contributed, for home and Foreign missions ; the theological institution ; general chapel fund ; educational fund 80., £420 3s 61, an amount which showed a suitable increase. For special objects £687 25 was raised ; (including £25 65 to the Indian Famine Fund : £l6 16s to the Local Preachers Mutual Aid ; and £645 paid to the 20th Century Fund), amounts had ad schbueoern, being a total for the year of £1,107 5s 61. For ordinary circuit purposes the following raised : Chavol trusts, £Bl7 104 101; £233 14e ; circuit quarterly board, £615 125 2d ; choir exoenses, £5O 103 31; poor f und.£ll.l 6 8i; incidental. £7B 10S ! being a total of £1,637 3s lid for local pur- poses ; and a gross total for the year of £2,744 91 51. This interesting figure does not inelude any centribu„ tion made to the splenoidly successful circuit scheme..-.. I The meeting expressed itself vary pleased with the statement, which was remarked to be proof posi- tive that though Nonconformists may be poet they are very liberal.—During the quarter the cir- cuit had lost two - devoted workers by death—Mr J D Jones and Mr John Haddock. restimony was borne to their earnest services, and the meeting placed on record its appreciation of their character and work and directed a letter of sympathy to be sent to the bereaved in each case.—The Chairman referred to the recent &amp;nod at Blackpool, and cslled upon the junior representative Mr L Rosento.rgorho gave an interesting sclsunt of his visit there.—The meeting also decided to take steps for revising thri circuit Sunday &amp;tool Union ; and after a brief conversation on the condition of the " Million Scheme "a wry pleasant meeting closed with the Doxology and Benediction.</p>
