      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>KING FOR AN HOUR.</head>
        <p>A KING FOR AN HOUR.</p>
        <p>BY ARTHUR W. MARCEIMONT, Author of "Sir jaffray's Wife," "Parson Thring's Secret," "Madeline Power," Sic., &amp;c.</p>
        <p>When Digby Grenville lost his head so far as to propose to Sybil Mounteney, all the world smiled. Their mutual love was common know- ledge, and everyone admitted that they were the handsomest couple in every ballroom where they met, danced, laughed, sighed and dreamt them- selves into ecstasy; but that they should think of marrying was sheer absurdity. Grenville was the all but penniless younger son of a man half-ruined by landed depression, and was waiting for an appointment in the diplomatic service. Popular he was everywhere, and usually so tactful, ccol and level-headed that his proposal astonished people the more. The girl was miles out of his reach. She was the one hope which that proud old pauper, Lord Kilmenny, had of retrieving his fortunes; the beauty of the season, with half eligible London in general at her feet, and in particular Lord Marlinford, the heir to the Caresford dukedom and wealth that was simply fabulous. The proposal was like fire to a powder-train. In less than four-and-twenty hours afterwards, Grenville's appointment abroad was fixed, and he was told quite plainly, both by Sybil's father and his own, that an engagement was a mad absurdity. He was a fellow of grit, however, and insisted on conditions: that he should have a certain time in which to try and make his way, and at least one private interview with Sybil before he left. At the interview the girl, for all her pride, was very tender and nervous, and both hands and lips trembled as she sat waiting for him to speak. "I've had a big fight," he said, with a smile that made his face wonderfully attractive, "and I've been a fool—l suppose. But the folly was very sweet, Sybil," he added, with a change to a low tone; "and I shall never forget it—all my life." "Do you mean you sacrifice me at their mere orders 1'" asked the girl, with a flash of quick resentment. Her lover looked at her an instant. _ _ _</p>
        <p>;1-15-o you think that ? " he asked. "No, I don't. They've played on your feel- ings. But what I told you is trIIA I will be no man's wife but yours—unless Irt•U release me. They may do as they will." ."I ought never to have spoken, Sybil. It was cowardly." "Then I like cowardice," she said in a low voice, and smiling. But added instantly, with passion: "Your speaking made no difference. What do I care for money? I'd rather be your colleen in a cabin than another man's duchess in a palace." His eyes shone at this. It • was sweet to hear; but he had himself well in hand now, though he dared not trust himself to speak. "And what test do they propose?" asked the girl, in contemptuous impatience that anyone should doubt her earnestness. "A simple enough one," he answered with a short laugh of bitterness. "I have only to make my position in a year. lam going out to Copen- hagen as junior attaché; and all the world knows that every attaché makes his fortune in a twelve- month." "I will wait longer than a year, Digby. You trust me ?" "That's the pinch of it! " he replied, not look- ing at her, and dropping his voice. "It's not only my fortune to be made. Even if that were possible—which it's not—l—" "I know what you're going to say," she burst in, "and I won't be sold to make my brothers and sisters rich. I won't. Let them sell themselves. Even Lord Alarlinford—" .</p>
        <p>..... "Don't say a word against him, Sybil. He's sound right through. He's behaved awfully well in this. He knows just how things stand all round, and he gave me his word that he'd help me all he could; but he warned me that ten years in the service, with all the influence il3 England at my back, won't make my fortune—unless I strike a vein of luck; and as for making two fortunes--" He ended with a shrug of the shoulders and a smile of despair. "I will wait the ten years, then," she said. "I'm only allowed one," he answered, shaking his head. .</p>
        <p>"Well, they can't allowance me, I suppose! " "If I can't do something in one year, ten will be no use, nor twenty." "Why do you fix one ? " Lord Kilraenny had told him freely that he could not fend off ruin for more than one year, and that if Sybil were not engaged by then to Lord Marlinford, or some other wealthy man, the whole family would be hopelessly ruined; but Grenville could not tell the girl this, and his silence roused doubts of his strength of purpose. "Why do you fix one year ? " she repeated. • "I have given my word," he answered. "Yes—but why ? " "If I can do nothing in one year, two or five will be no better. I ought never to have spoken," he repeated. "Tell me the whole arrangement," she said, after a pause of thought. "I am going to Copenhagen in three days from now. If in twelve months' time I can sbew that I have even a prospect of doing well, I may —come back. If not, I have passed my word not to return at all till you—" "Till I—what?" she asked, looking intently at him.</p>
        <p>"Are married, Sybil." "Till I am dead, say rather. I will never marry"; and then she burst out into tempestuous protests against what be had done and the pledge he had given, vowing that she would be no party to it; that she would not be sold and bartered like a slave; that he could not care for her to give her up so lightly; with much more to the same effect, as her love dashed against the barriers between them in the vain effort to break them down or persuade herself they could be broken. Nor was she comforted till she lay in his aims, and felt his kisses on her lips, and listened to the vows he uttered, with all the passion that was throbbing and thrilling in him, that he,would strive to win her. A week later he had reported himself at Copen- hagen, and commenced the round of duties that seemed intolerably irksome, petty, and wearying in the first hours of separation. For three months he worked indefatigably with hope; in six hope languished; in nine he realised the hopelessness of his task; and as the year approached its end he admitted defeat. "Nothing but a miracle could do what we want —and nowhere are miracles more strongly combated and deprecated than in the diplomatic service. God knows I would do anything, -however mad and desperate, to win you, but Romance is dead, and one must be a tailor or a Jew to make money. By the time I'm fifty I might be in a position to marry, and you would have wasted your sweet life and worn your heart to tatters waiting for me. Ah, Sybil--" He had got thus far in one of the many letters which, to vent his spleen, he used to write, but never send to the girl, when he was interrupted by the entrance of a friend, Major Dekker, the head of the Danish Police. He was a grim, reserved, and rather soured old bachelor, who had taken a strange fancy to Digby. "What are you doing? Writing? Some woman, I'll be bound, by the way you popped it out of range of my old eyes. Why aren't you about some of your finicking duties with this great King and his young fool of a Queen? "—with cynical stress on the "great." Copenhagen was mad with excitement over the Visit of one of the most powerful rulers of Europe, who had brought his newly-married bride to the Danish Court. "I have nothing to do just now,' answered Grenville, smiling. "Ugh I Well, I can give you something to do If you want it. I don't think you'd be afraid of a bit of danger, eh? With something good dangling at the other end, umph ? Have you stomach for a bit of a fight? and will you hold your tongue? I?ve answered for you, boy." "If there's anything to be gained, I'd face old Nick himself !" answered Grenville. "You mercenary young devil! T4hy, in my time, I'd face old Nick for the mere sake of the adventure. But there is something in it, if you can carry it through. Do you know why this blessed and righteous King has come visiting here ? " "Yes; to bring his Queen to see her relatives here." "Oh, the wonderful intelligence of these cunning Foreign Offices!" exclaimed the old man, with a gesture of mock admiration. "Do you know that You're very much like his Most Gracious Majesty? and with a little make-up could pass for him ? Would you do it, if I chewed you how you could 'Perhaps serve him such a turn as would mak.e</p>
        <p>i persuade herself;,</p>
        <p>him your friend tor Me—or, on the other hand, perhaps cost you your life? " "That's rather a —"</p>
        <p>"Quick—yes or no ? " "Yes, then." "Good. Now, wait for me here," and without I saying more he went out. He came back in less than half an hour, with a companion whom he introduced as Colonel Dubose!. , —a man whom Grenville recognised as chief of the Royal visitors' police, charged with the responsi- bility of guarding his master. "This is Mr. Grenville, and I'll answer for him with my life," said old Dekker by way of intro- duction; and with scarcely another word the two explained their plan and purpose. "His Majesty is here in Denmark for a purpose which is secret. You may have heard of the Countess Pobla, Mr. Grenville? No? Well, she had been a very close friend of the King before his marriage, and she has possession of a number of letters which, if published, either now or at any future time, will have a very serious effect upon his Majesty's reputation for—common sense and—good faith. He promised to marry her morganatically and—did not do so. She played her cards in her own way, and now that the King is married she vows she will use the letters. The King had, before marriage, to give a pledge that the connec- tion should be broken off, and that he would not , speak to her again; and now the Countess swears she'll give the letters up only into his hands, and 1 she has named this place as the scene for an inter- view."</p>
        <p>"Well ? " "We've tried every means to get them—offered a fortune for them and other advantages; but she won't; and in my own mind I believe she thinks she can draw him back to her side—she is a most beautiful woman—if she can get a chance to speak to him alone. But that is not by any means the worst part of it. I know that she is in touch with a number of desperate men who, if they could get the chance, would kill his Majesty—only too readily. She thinks to try coaxing first and threats afterwards to win him back; and I feel sure there is a very ugly business behind." "But what says the King himself?" asked Grenville. . . .</p>
        <p>"What would such a man as he is say in such a case? He believes in her entire good faith; he has 9greed to the terms, and will meet her—in fact, do anything to get the d—d letters out of her hands."</p>
        <p>"But why come to wash the dirty linen here?" "The Countess chose the place, as—body of my soul I—she chooses everything; and she thinks this safe and out of the way. She has made the terms. He is to meet her unattended, save by me—she knows me, and means to keep me in sight and out of mischief; and then she's to take us somewhere—where she won't say—and give up the letters. It's like a story-book. But it's all she'll consent to, and there's no other way of getting the things. Though, honestly, I'm afraid of the whole business." "But what do you want me to do?" asked Grenville.</p>
        <p>----- "To be the King for an hour," said old Dekker bluntly. "Trick Jein both. W shall contrive to keep his Majesty shut up in the Palace, gnashing , his teeth, at the time of the appointment; and you'll make up like him and go and bamboozle the lady. Gad! I wish I was young enough; I'd do it myself 1" "You must clearly understand all that it means, young sir," said Colonel Duboscq seriously. "In the first place, by personating the King you are Committing something like high treason, and if his Majesty takes it the wrong way you'll be In a very tight place indeed, and your career as a diplomatist may be over. If you escape that, you have to risk this woman's anger. She'll be certain to find you out the instant you speak, and then, if she has the means handy, she'll try to kill you is some way. Again, there is the grave risk that the whole thing is merely an ambush, in which you may be trapped and killed before your identity is discovered. If the Countess doesn't find you out, and yet you don't get the letters, you'll have the King on you, again, for daring to meddle in his matters and pry into his secrets. Your one chance is that by your own wit,. or your luck, or your pluck, you can manage to get the letters—and frankly, the risk is a thousand to one." "I'll take it," answered Grenville. "And now tell me all I ought to know. At any rate, I'll be a King for an hour, and that's something." "Ah, it's the rough side of the crown you're going to wear, my lad," said Duboscq; and then a long discussion of the scheme followed, at the close of which Duboscq clapped the young attaché on the shoulder, and exclaim ed: "D —n it, man, I'm not so dead sure as I was that you'll be killed, after all! " And with this cheering phrase they parted. During the next two or three days they had one or two further consultations, and Grenville made a close study of the Royal visitor—noting his manner of carriage, his walk, his postures, and everything that would help the impersonation. The assignation was fixed for the evening ,if the fourth day. The wily Duboscq dropped a hint to the Queen that there might be danger to the King if he left the Palace that evening, and she kept persistently in his company until there was nothing for the King to do but send a message of postpone- ment. This he entrusted to Duboscq, who went straight to Digby Grenville, who was waiting, made up like the King, and leading him to where the horses stood, the two mounted and rode off. "It is a good make-up, your Majesty," said Duboseq, with a grin, as they rode. "Excellent, if you'll only be careful not to speak. But if you open your Royal mouth, the whole truth will come tumbling out of it. I shall tell the Countess you will not speak, partly for fear of her charms, and partly because you're pledged not to; and you must bear me out." A sharp ride of some six miles brought them to the place of meeting. "Mind—not a word. Leave me to talk. If possible, don't get off your horse till the last minute to enter the house, wherever it may be. Remember, you have to keep your wits about you till you can find out where those confounded letters are; and then make a dash for them. All's fair in this sort of work, though it is against a woman. And mind, if there should prove to be an ambush, sell your life as dearly as you can; we shall be together. We have to hold our own for a quarter of an hour somehow. My men will follow at that interval, and I shall leave a trail broad enough for them to follow, wherever we go." Five minutes later they reached the meeting- place. Duboscq dismounted, and Grenville, his rites partly muffled, sat silent on his horse, in an attitude he knew to be characteristic of the Bing. Almost as soon as they had drawn rein, a carriage was driven rapidly towards them, and as it stopped Grenville saw that its one occupant was a woman. Duboscq went to the door, bowing and uncovered. "His Majesty, madame." "The Xing can ride with me—and you also." "We have our horses. But we will follow wherever you may be pleased to lead." "Has his Majesty nothing to say for himself ? " she asked impetuously. "My master is afraid of fire, madame; old wounds scorch, and the fever of them is not yet dead." He said this in a low tone, as if for her ear alone; but Grenville caught it. The Countess laughed; and a sweet, musical laugh she had. "I thought he had found a Royal salve for them! " she cried. At that the "King " moved restlessly, as though the shaft had struck home. "The King, as you know, is pledged not to speak to you, madame. But he has come; and if we are not to return, say where are we to go?" She laughed again, this time confidently; and giving an order to her coachman, she glanced out at the "Ring" as the carriage was turned round and the horses whipped up at a smart pace. The two followed through a number of narrow cross-country lanes, keeping close to the carriage until it turned into the courtyard of a large and gloomy house, the gates of which were closed as soon as the horsemen had passed. As they clanged to, Duboscq glanced round uneasily. "I don't like that," he said gruffly. Grenville dismounted, and keeping his face in the shade, and still somewhat muffled, offered his arm to the Countess, thinking that if there were any mischief intended he had better be close by her side. Duboscq followed close at his heels. Grenville could feel the Countess's hand trem- bling as it clasped his arm, but the pressure tightened as she Whispered, in a caressing tone: "It is good of you to yield to my whim and come to me." He bowed, but said nothing. "You must speak, sire, if only to say you for- give me." "His Majesty's presence is a sign of his forgive- ness, madame," interpolated Duboscq, who had overheard the question. "We are here for a purpose." "1 have much to say first." she answered. "But</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
