      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>If you Want to Win</head>
        <p>If you Want to Win</p>
        <p>£l,OOO</p>
        <p>SEE THIS WEEK'S FAMILY HERALD</p>
        <p>NOW OUT. PRICE id.</p>
        <p>PERMANENTLY ENLARGED.</p>
        <p>come, let us go in here. At least you will breat bread and take a cup of wine with me, sire? It Ii all prepared." She pushed open the door of a room on her right as she spoke, and entered. The place was snore gloomy than the entrance ball, and there were no signs of any preparations. An exclamation of anger broke from her lips, and taking her hand from Grenville's arm, she went to the bell and pulled it vigorously. No one came in reply to it for a minute or so. and during the interval all three waited in silence. She rang again violently; and then in an instant the place seemed alive with men, all save one of whom were masked. "By God, it is an ambush!" cried Duboseq; "for God's sake, your Majesty--" He had no time to finish the sentence, for two or three of the men had thrown themselves upon him, and bound and gagged him. Meanwhile, the Countess set up screaming, until one of the men clapped a pistol at her head, and ordered her, with a threatening gesture, to hold her tongue. For the moment no one touched the "Bing," who stood apart on the defensive, with his revolver drawn and ready for instant use. His hour of royalty seemed likely to cost him dear. "'What is the meaning of this? The sentence came from ther.Countess, and was addressed to the unmasked leader. "You thought to use us for your purposes, and we have used you for ours. That's all," he answered with a sneer.</p>
        <p>The reply seemed to rouse her. She uttered cry of fear, and rushing across the room threw herself in front of the "King," and dared them to lay a finger upon him, vowing they should kill her first. But the men were not of the sort to be stayed from their purpose by a woman's anger, and at a sign from the leader two of them n advanced, and disregarding the "Ring's" revolver, which was levelled point blank in their faces, they seized her and dragged her away. As soon as the Countess had been dragged out of the way, the unmasked leader turned to the "King," and said, in a voice hard with the ring of personal hate: "You will know my face. lam the brother of the man your minions beheaded for daring to strike a blow for freedom. Our law is a life for a life. Your life for his. You have been trapped here to die. Make your peace with God; for in five minutes you will be in Heaven—or Hell." Grenville breathed hard, and his thoughts for an instant flew to the girl to win whom he had faced this danger. But his wits were quickly at work, and he close his course. He would wait in silence till the time nearly ran out. He glanced round the room calmly, and drawing himself up, stood with his arms folded across his chest, as if awaiting death with dignified resigna- tion. He calculated that when the five minutes had expired Duboseq's men would be close to the place. The leader stood watch in hand, counting the minutes as they sped; and the silence in the room was broken only by his voice and the laboured breathing of the half-fainting woman who had caused the trouble. "Make ready!" cried the implacable leader, when four minutes and a half had been told off; and he himself poised his rifle ready to bring it to the present, while the rattle of all the others' guns rang through the room. Grenville chose that moment to intervene. Raising his hand, he called in a loud, firm voice: "Stay! There is a desperate mistake! lam not the King!" and as he spoke he threw aside his cloak and hat and plucked off the disguise he had worn. This act caused the profoundest consternation. The Countess was the most affected of all, and in her rush of relief she uttered a cry of "Thank God!" and fell to the ground unconscious; while the men started back, grounding their muskets with a loud noise, and stood staring hard at Grenville with fear and doubt and menace. "I came here disguised as the Ring," he said, "for a purpose you may or may not know, con- nected with the Countess Pobla there. It seems to have been a good disguise, for it tricked her at well as you. But lam not the King, and if you choose to kill me it will be deliberate murder—and for no purpose." "But that is—" said the leader, pointing to Duboscq. "Colonel Duboscq, whose men are even novo round the place, hemming you in like rats l" said Grenville boldly. "If we are killed in the trap; you will be as certainly be caught when you try to escape from it." The effect of this speech was instantly apparent. The men turned from one to another in fear, and one of them, more reckless than the rest, levelled his rifle and fired at Grenville, wounding him in the left arm. This added infinitely to the coda- Mon, and in the midst of it Grenville spoke again. "It will do no good to kill us; and I win offer you good terms. You shall go scot-free, every man of you—even the miscreant who shot we just now. Quick! Do you accept ? " A hurried consultation followed, and that the leader accepted. Almost as he spoke, a loud knocking and clashing without came to their ears. "By —, we've got them now!" burst out old buboscq, who had been released. "Now. you villains, you shall pay for this night's work i" and he whipped out his sword. The men turned, as if at bay. "Colonel Duboscq," cried Grenville in a voice of thunder, "nut up your sword! You will hold your men back as my escort to the Palace. I have passed my word; and so far as you and that pledge are concerned, lam still the King. Gentlemen, you are safe. Let us pass." Duboscq fell back before the other's resolute manner, and with a smothered oath thrust his sword into its sheath... Then he whispered:</p>
        <p>"And the letters? " "We have got our lives, man; hang the letters I" cried Grenville, and hurried away, bidding his companion follow him under pain of being arrested by, his own men at the "King's " order. "If only King for an hour, I felt one of its pleasures—what it is to be obeyed," he said as they mounted. •</p>
        <p>"And what did become of the letters?" It was a much sweeter voice that asked the clues. tion, and quite three months afterwards. Gran- villa had got leave—and a good deal more. "It is they have brought us together. The Countess Pobla was not altogether so bad a sort— only a jilted woman, and therefore angry—and in the rush of gladness that she had not led the real King to his death she gave them to me, on Con- dition that I told the King everything and asked rs sufficient reward for myself." "And what did you ask? " said Sybil. "Leave to serve his Majesty and means to win— ray wife. He declares he will have no more treasured servant, and that the Court shall know no more brilliant marriage than ours." [Tule END.]</p>
