        <p>Education : The There is no mistake about Clerical Demand. the demand made by tha</p>
        <p>clerical friends of edues' tion. They are willing under the adopted proposals of the "National Society "' to draw a line between denominational education and other kinds of education; but they will have control of all. This is what the ARCHBISHOP OF CIDITERBITIZI says on the subject: "They must see to it that the system was not so changed as to make it impossible' to secure that the school masters be really religions people, that they (Churchmen) had the right t° teach in their own schools and to insist that parents had the right of obtaining not only undo' nominational teaching, but also that which thei , desired." That is to say that while churchmen re" ' fuse any longer to pay the piper, they 'gill still insist on calling the tune. These gentrY appear to be sublimely unconscious of the faet 1 that when a school comes to be supported by the State it becomes a State institution, and isale longer a "Voluntary School," nor in any seuse "their own schools." If they will only be financr ally responsible for one hour's teaching of denorar national instruction in the morning, they cannot expect to be left in charge of the school during the whole day. The idea that in this 20th CenturY national education is to be committed to teacher° selected under a religious test is the dread of s blind enthusiast. The highest principles of free equitable administration and constitutional government forbid such a thing, even if the record of the Church Schools had not been as bad as it is' Teachers who are selected for their posts becaUs° they are "denominationally religions,7—becauee they subscribe to the 39 articles with the varioo° additions which a modern craze for Zeithetic aro ceremonial observances has made to the require ments of the dutiful churchman—because the can pia., the organ, lead or sing in a choir, asslsta in the duties of clerk or churchwarden,. se, give up their leisure to the various pursuits eelo braced in the elastic term "church work," are 00 likely to shine as stars in the educational firreer; ment. In the past they have not done so;far fre .1 it. One of the latest demands made in the inte.tir oat of such teachers is that the certificates Wh under the national system are given as a distio°. tive mark of merit and efficiency after hard stilel.P,, hard preparatory practical work and Bearable: examinations. the certificates which indicatebe teacher's professional status and capacity should . given at sight by any inspector, without exao2l`l'' 1 tion. This reactionary step towards lowering efficiency of the teaching of the" other subjeo in a school,it is seriously proposed should be taer:4 while the school is to remain under the mist° ahr control of people whose sole aim according to. tali of own admission is to teach a particular lau_ter• creed,which no two people can agree about un"feo standing or interpreting and which verY people really believe. The people of this CO i will not have it. The cause of sognd -</p>
