        <p>Wm</p>
        <p>Cannot afford it. There is one condition upon Which the nation will take over the present Volun- tary Schools with or without denominational teaching—it matters not—the education mainly given must be taught and managed in precisely the same way as in other schools managed by the State. Sound principles of administration require that the test of a teacher's fitness for his work must be efficiency in the work which he is tailed upon to perform; and if his character is Irreproachable his religious convictions ought to be</p>
        <p>eacreel from the prying- scrutiny of priests:and con- The people who at this time of day can 1110 pose to perpetuate such a mediaeval system Only show their utter unfitness to manage the education of the people; the control of their tichools ought therefore to be given to constitu- tional representative bodies who can take a higher view of their educational duties. Mr</p>
        <p>It is unfortunate for Mr. Labouchere and the Civil List. LABOUCHERE, and not less so for the House of Commons, that he has lost his popularity as a de- and has become discredited as a political leave. When he rises to address the House only a handful of members remain to listen. He seems tO have lost his humour along with his audiences; 111 is rarely that he rises above a dull plodding rnediocrity of arguments, and the bright flashes of satire and brilliant flights of witty invective which 743 ed to be associated with his public appearances ore like angels visits, few and far between. This nate of things in the Senate represents a corres- Pending loss of influence in the country, where Ida reputation once one of the foremost taa experienced a period of decadence if not of eclipse. Into the causes of the change It is unnecessary to inquite ; but it is an unfortu- nate thing for others besides Mr LABOUCHERE. With a daring that is unique he ventures upon ground which many politicians av lid out of sheer Shamefacedness, and a fear of being thought outre even among Radicals. With a courtesy of demeanour that is almost apologetic he disarms I, offence while he probes the secrets of the most , flagrant jobs, and exposes the tricks of a Govern- went which is wedded to privilege and corruption as twin guiding stars. Were he more discrimin- ating in his choice of material he might be more emphatic and successful in his attacks. As it is his criticism is remorseless ; and he is invariably prepared to back up his charges with statistical and other information which reveal facts not generally known to his audience, and which could 4ot:fail to carry conviction, if they had the chance °1 a fair unprejudiced hearing. There is much of Vhat ho contributes to the debates in Parliament , ,that will bear thinking over, and much that would , lead to the elucidation of problems, especially 41 connection with finance, which the country has ' to face. As an instance may be cited the question , 01 the Civil List, which he has made a subject of careful study, and the figures of the division show- an enormous majority of 310 for the Govern- tent on the 3rd reading, while they represent the conventional attitude adopted by a large majority L5l the House, do not by any means indicate the itt"e value of the criticisms passed by the Hon. '4ealber for Northamptonshire. On Tuesday tttitneon he directed special attention to the un- ;lNtitutional position which the Government li 140 taken up with regard to the hereditary reven- :."vhicti are included in the Civil List. The attitude of the Government according to Mr BAL- 4-(VCIL at least appears to be that in the Duchies of Lancaster and Cornwall the Crown has personal [, property which cannot be alienated. Mr Lanett- ' Canan pointed out that if this position had been • correct then the revenues would have gone to JAMES II of unblessed memory and his heirs. But they were included in the State revenues given to WILLTsm and MARY as an acknowledgment of the . Cervices of the King. The right of the Sovereign Was restricted to moneys voted by Parliament light through the reigns of ANNE and the first two ChORGES. It NVELS GEORGE 111. who introduced the idea 01 personal property when the "surrender" of ea, nein of the smaller hereditary revdnues wa S lutroduced into the Civil List Bill: The accession 45 a new sovereign affords a favourable opportunity for a definitive statement on the point, but the c, .T.,`lnvarnment will not face the issue. Sir WILLIAM aaancoura rising from the front opposition bench atietained Mr. LABOUCHERE'S contention, quoting /I.E'lwEn WaLroLE as a clear authority for the</p>
        <p>'Statement that if thelHereditary Crown Lands were claimed as personal property this would involve the whole civil service of the country being main- tained by the SovEntiox. It is wonderful how the Government will daily with a grave constitu- tional question of this kind, without considering the danger it involves. For tie sake of a foolish satisfaction derived from the contemplation of our aovereigns and princes as memners of the great and owning class, in their own right, the Govern- laent are prepared to involve them in all the invid- itala responsibilities of the modern landlord. But 4Part from the undesirable relationship which this itaPlies, a great principle of the British constitu- t.i°l3, a bulwark of our liberties, will be endangered it! the SOVEREIGN is to be placed in a financial p05i- :1)4 in which he will be independent of the money tiltited by the House of Commons. The voting of ita‘l civil list is the " pull " this country has upon /h. constitutional SOVEREIGN but it is precisely la feature which is being deliberately weakened 4" may be in time destroyed,</p>
        <p>1 tockton Heath Parish Council was sadly in of an adviser on Tuesday evening when the ' rt°l3osed electric tramways for Warrington were 11,t171cler consideration. The Clerk was unfor- t 111,eately absent and therefore the members were - tett to their own devices and a pretty mess tioleY made of it. Mr John Pember- -414 took a prominent part in advis- g the Parish Council to" petition the Local i the Board not to authorize the loan for , ti Wilderspool tramway unless the Corpora- -00 will will agree to continue the licences for ° 'bus suvice to Stockton Heath as at- V.esenc existing or until a tramway is sub- 41.1ted to give an equal advantage." The . parish Council was so guileless as to accept the eqt.uggestion and a resolution was passed. We gOUld like to know what the Local Government lear•l have got to do with the matter. We have thWays thought that the Board of Trade was a4responsible authority for tramways, and was it a Board of Trade Inspector who tem ected the inquiry at the Town Hall last th-e;. ? Yet the Parish Council have petitioned L i„..e ocal Government Board! We can almost Km • b glee the surprise with which the Government e Pertraent will receive the petition, and it would 40 tut be difficult to anticipate the reply. A further 'plaili,ng of the Parish C ,uncil will be necessary to tha Te resolution in order, and it is to be hoped members will this time hit upon the g t atithority. Ths .</p>
        <p>reFort of Wednesday—evening's "stop-the- meeting in London makes curious reading. °l2 affair which had been organized with ea-</p>
        <p>treme care to prevent any miscarriage or failure of the purpose for which it was called, and the organization had the desired effect. Principally it consisted of the employment of 700 stewards, or as one writer bluntly styles them " chuckers-out." Elaborate instructions were issued to these gentry, many of whom hailed from Battersea, and were of the genus not inaptly described as Hooligans. Their capability as chuckers out," however, was unmistakable. Squads of them guarded the doors and "flying columns" patrolled the corridors, while the remainder plumped themselves down in the best seats. The meeting was, by the way, announced as" public," but only those persons who possessed tickets were admit- ted, and they, of course, were known sympathisers with the Boers. One or two college students and outsiders did succeed in obtaining entrance, but immediately they attempted to interrupt, the proceedings, they were "fired out,' to use an Americanism which fits the case. This packed "public" meeting passed a resolution by a unanimous vote demanding that the Government should sue for peace by granting" the complete lindependence of the two republics." While admit- ting that the "stop-the-war" agitators have a rightl to their opinions and a right to express them we must emphatically discountenance both "public" meetings and resolutions of this kind. Tlaey can do no good and may very possibly do harm, while their futility is apparent in the firm determination of the people of this country not to have undone by an unwise peace, the work which has cost so much in life and treasure to accomplish.</p>
        <p>The advantage of having practical men in th Houses of Parliament is becoming more evident each day. Only a week or two ago the new Bis- hop of London astonished a slumbering assembly by his intimate acquaintance with the methods of betting touts in the East-end, and of the weakness of the law in dealing with them. He related how they used to offer odds beneath his window, and then smiled and went into the next street when he instituted a prosecution. This week Mr John Burns has been explaining to a Home Secretary not very familiar with such details the sanitary defects of restaurants in London--how, for example, a man following the calling of vermin and insect destroyer finds employment in irregular attention to these places. Mr Burns is anxious that underground kitchens which are inaccesaible to the public should be brought within the Factory Acts, in order that they may be regularly inspected. There is something to be said for this,for the leataurant- keeper who has a staff that keeps things clean could not be injured by such an examination. Moreover, there is the other consideration of airing these chambers. If ventilation be required anywhere it is where foods are stored and being prepared. Mr. Burns spoke more especially of the restaurants' in Soho, but it would be unfair to make the accus- ation sweeping. For the most part, wherever the eating-house be situated, whether in London or the provinces, there is a danger of carelessness ' when the prices are cheap; and as most of the population can afford oLly cheap prices the ques- tion is one of concern to them. We know what' the analysis of some ice-creams has revealed, but , the foods served up from dirty kitchens are dis- guised in sauces, and we barely discover the truth about them. It is to be feared that the inspectors of animals often let pass unwittingly stock affected with tuberculosis, and it is probable that disease is spread that way—that is, when the food is not thoroughly boiled or roasted. The occasional presence of an inspector in the kitchen would be useful in more ways than one.</p>
        <p>The bombardment of the skies, which the Ger- mans in their extraordinarily pliable language have termed " wetterschiessen,' or weather-shoot- ing, is devised to save vineyards from hail. What has been wanted in this country is some form of weather-shooting which will cause a precipitation of the water which is always suspended in the atmosphere in large quantities, even in the driest days of the east wind season. Scientific men are by no means unanimous in believing that the firing at the clouds which is now so largely practised in parts of Austria and Italy has any effect. In fact, it seems that the whorl or gyrating wave which is sent upwards from the conical pieces of artillery used for the purpose, though it can be heard whistling upwards for many seconds after the detonation, can have no appreciable effect at a distance beyond six hundred yards, while the hail-clouds which are the mark of the artillerist can seldom be at a less altitude than fifteen hundred to two thousand yards. How- ever, even before these special appliances were de- vised great differences of opinion have prevailed as to the effect of artillery on the weAther. Many of the great battles of history have been followed by deluges of rain; but, on the other hand, most , of them have been fought in climates where such an occurrence is a daily feature. The American morning bombardments of Santiago were regularly followed by rain; but then, rain at that season of the year would have fallen every noon had the guns of the Brooklyn and the Oregon been silent. On the other hand, there are not wanting stories of thunderclouds being dispersed by gun-fire. The probability is that the influence on the weather, whichever way it goes, is so slight as not to be worth the powder, whether to keep off the hail from the vineyards or to break a long season of drought.</p>
        <p>Last week the scheme for the provision of Turkish baths in Warrington was abandoned, and we hope we have heard the last of it for some years to come. There was never any genuine public demand for such an expensive luxury as a Turkish bath, and the Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee has acted wisely in withdrawing the application to the Local Goverment Board for sanction to borrow the money. We have pre- viously pointed out in this column, the undesir- ability of embarking upon such a large expenditure in order to meet the wants of the few, and we think we are entitled to some little credit for help- ing to bring about the withdrawal of the scheme. Very few workingmen desire, or can afford, such a luxury, and the statement that workingmbn want Turrrish baths may be dismissed as idle non- sense. There are, of course, several members of the Council who are keenly disappointed that the scheme has been dropped, and some criticisms will probably be passed on the action of the Com- mittee in withdrawing the scheme at the eleventh hour without the consent of the Council, but on reflection we believe these members of the Cor- poration will feel that the present is an inoppor- tune moment for providing luxuries at a cost of from £4,000 or £5,000.</p>
        <p>Will electric tramways pay in Warrinc,iton ? This was the question discussed in all its be-strings at the Board of Trade inquiry held at the Town Hall last week into the application of the Cor- poration for sanction to borrow £60,050 for tram- way purposes. There is no gainsaying the fact that the scheme is a speculation and may or may not prove to be a good investment of the rate- payers' money. It all depends upon the measure of support which is given by the general public to the electric cars when they have commenced run- ning. Basing his opinion on the amount of omni- bus traffic at the:present moment Alderman Monks avers that the electric trams will pro- duce an income which will more than cover the estimated expenditure. Wilatever may be the disadvantages possessed by Warrington in being a self-contained town with- out a thickly-populated neighbourhood, it must not be forgotten that the Warrington Corpora- tion commences its tramway system with an ad- vantage over other centres. Here there is no necessity to buy out private tramway companies as has been the case in some places, and which has seriously handicapped the financial success of those particular tramway schemes. I Nearly the whole of the capital spent by the Cor- poration will be for plant and rolling stock, and if the cars are liberally supported there should be a fair prospect of making a profit. Such must be the opinion of those connected with the private company which has offered to take the risk and recoup the Corpora- tion for the expenditure involved. If a private company can go so far there is no reason why the Corporation should not take up —as they are doing—the financial responsibility themselves.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>EXAMINER FRIDAY, JUNE 21 1901:</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER FRIDAY, JUNE 21 1901:</p>
        <p>A " PERFECT FARCE" AT WIDNES.</p>
        <p>I At Widnes Patty Sessions on Thursday morning, Catherine Fallon, was brought up on a charge of , neglecting to send her children to school. On the summons issued she was described as a widow, but she stated in evidence that her husband was work- ing at Prescot, She had done her best to send the children to.school, but they would not go. Major Lewis described the summons as a "perfect farce," and said it was quite unfair to summon a woman Who had no personal estate, while her husband was still legally in charge of them and only five miles away. It was decided to dismiss the summons, Mr Danby intimating that he would re-issue it against her husband, but he was sure he would not appear, or he would plead that he could not look after them while he was away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</head>
        <p>MATTERS OF MOMENT.</p>
        <p>A Provincial Letter.</p>
        <p>Just before the longest day it is customary for newspapers having an agricultural connection to collect facts and give approximate 1 irecasts affect- ing at least the more important of the crops. This is what is now being done, and from the returns sent in it would appear that, on the whole, the present year, if not really disastrous for farmers, is certainly causing them much anxiety. For al- though the welcome rain which fell about the end of May freshened up and quickened the grewth a little, it has not hitherto been followed with the "dripping June' which, according to the provero, "sets things in tune." frue, it saved what would otherwise have been a hay famine in some parts of the country, though not sufficient to benefit man- golds, cabbage, or late sown spring corn. South of the Tweed the rainfall has been much below the average, and all agricultural crops, except wheat, will be the same. This year the area sown to wheat, it is calculated, is less than last year by about twelve per cent on the twelve months.</p>
        <p>From what transpired in the House of Cctomons on Tuesday evening it may be gathered, once for all, that the Chancellor of the Exchequer has not the slightest intention of dropping any portion of the coal tax. While adhering to the principle of the proposed duty he professes to still have an open mind as to the method of giving effect to it. So that whatever the coal-owners have to say on this point. Sir Michael Hicks-Beach will have his pound of flesh. In answer to Mr Renwick he said there was no truth in the statement that he was disposed to reduce the duty on small coal, which the owners themselves appear at some loss for means to define with sufficient clearness.</p>
        <p>Since Parliament three years ago, in what the medical faculty regard as a moment of weakness, passed the Act exempting the parent who could plead conscientious objections from the obligation to have his children vaccinated, an impression appears to have grown up in the public mind that vaccination is no longer compulsory, but merely permissive. The idea is,of course,wholly erroneous. Possibly all parents do not know that the Vaccina- tion Acts require every child to be vaccinated before it is six months old. If the child is not in a fit state, the vaccination may be postponed, but in that case a medical certificate has to be sent to the vaccination officer. Parents can, if they desire, have their children vaccinated free. Formerly they were obliged to attend with their children at the public vaccination station and take their turn. But this practice, which many people naturally found objectionable, is no longer necessary, since the public vaccinatoewill, when desired, visit the house of the parents for the purpose of vaccinating the child. It is as well that these particulars should be made widely known just now, because of discussions in some of the leading papers on the subject of vaccination, the publicity given to cer- tain challenges made as between vaccinators and anti-vaccinators, and the indifference of the public generally.</p>
        <p>A superabundant fruit crop is no new thing in the south-west of England. Apples in the Devonshire orchards have repeatedly been so plentiful towards the end of the season that it did not pay to pick I them, and still the importation from abroad has vone on, particularly for cider-making. But there seems a little novelty in the circumstance that in the gardens attached to the Bristol workhouse the gooseberry crop this year has been so large as to make it a perplexing problem what to do with the fruit. In the great establishments under their care it might be thought the Guardians would be able easily to deal with the surplus, but the suggestion that the gooseberries could be distributed among the local workhowes andlcottage homes, and thus utilised. has been met with the statement that the I quantity is more than sufficient, even if the fruit be used all the year round. It has been mach the same, also, with rhubarb, the production of which appears to have been on a scale far ahead of the! greatest possible requirements ; and here the diffi- culty is considered greater because there is no market for the article. The singular state of affairs is to be dealt with by distributing as much of the fruit as can properly be used in the establishments under the Board, by selling part of the gooseberries to the highest bidder, and making jam with what remains.</p>
        <p>It is noteworthy in its way that the more the Government's Education Bill is discussed in the provinces, the stronger appears to become the op- position brought to bear against it. On the day that the Primitive Methodist Conference at Sheffield passed a resolution expressing the strongest dis- approval of the Bill, the S3merset Teachers' Feder- ation bad not a good word to say at its conference in reference to the measure. But a still more noticeable incident at the meeting of the latter was that the strongest denunciation came from the lips of a gentleman who confessed be was a Conserva- tive and in agreement, with the Government on everything but its educational policy, while it was reserved for one who pronounced himself a Liberal to plead that instead of ending an admittedly bad bill, the teachers should, through their organisation endeavour to make the best of it. It only needs to be added here that the counsel of the Liberal advo- cate prevailed, and that one was glad to find it so. The "all or nothing" policy has hindered, or totally prevented, the passing of many a useful measure of legislation in the past and it is time to try the merit of something less exacting and unyielding. •</p>
        <p>Efforts have been made by some writers to ex- tract a little humour from the new malaria expedi- tion which set out from Liverpool for West Africa on Saturday. New, because this is the fifth expe- dition sent forth by the Liverpool School of Tropical Medicine. To the lay mind, it may appear that, considering the millions upon millions of mosquitoes that swarm even within a very restricted space, any attempt to exterminate them would be about as productive as beating the wind. The campaign organised on the basis of the discoveries made by the bacteriologist and his cc-operators, however, seemed quite an hopeless at the beginning; and yet, what enormous progress has been made in the contest with morbific germs I These unseen foes have been attacked in their lairs,and the result has been a marked improvement of the environment. Food, drink, and air have all been rendered less in- jurious to life, simply because the bacteriologist ' has shown how the disease-bearing germs might be destroyed or rendered innocuous. So with the campaign against the malaria-bearing mosquito, which already claims more victims in this country than is dreamt of outside medical circles ; it is not hopeless or extravagnnt as ic looks to those who have not taken the trouble to inform themselves as to its feasibleness. The larva of the dangerous mosquito can be identified without difficulty. The insect affects certain breeding places which are likewise easily recognised. The expedition has taken out large quantities of petroleum, creosote, and cement, and the attack will commence at the mosquito nurseries, the fountain head, where vast syarms can be destroyed without much trouble. The cement will be used for filling up pools in the rocks, eta., where the anopheles propagate their species.</p>
        <p>There are facts and figures in the Itegistrar- General's preliminary report on the Census of 1901 which are regarded with more than ordinary, and some of them with a painful interest. It is sad to think, for instance, of the tendency to depopula- tion manifested in so many of the "Sweet Auburns " scattered over various parts of England and Wales. The figures quoted into the report shew, among other things, that the mean annual death-rate in some of these districts has been declining steadily since 1801-71, and that the birth- ratethas declined with still greater rapidity since 1871-81. This dellension in the birth-rate is the most serious featu,.e in the census, and no doubt will be explained in the light of the continuous exodus from the country to the towns, where people live under cenlitions widely different from those which prevail in the rural districts The columns showing the increase or decrease of population in the registration districts should stimulate effort on the part of the authorities, Imperial and local, to devise means with a view to stetaming the leakage which thus drains the country of •its bone and sin ow.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>WE PUT THIS HERE</head>
        <p>WE PUT THIS HERE</p>
        <p>BECAUSE YOU'LL SEE IT AND TO REMIND YOU THAT FOR GOOD BOOTS FOR BYStiYBODY. In all the Latest Styles and at the Lowest Prices, ALF TAYLOR, CANNOT BE BEATEN. 45, HORSEMAIIKETI STREET, WARRINGTON. PATRON.= YOUR OWN TOWNSMAN.</p>
