      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>.. HILTON WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.</head>
        <p>.. HILTON WESLEYAN SUNDAY SCHOOL.</p>
        <p>The anniversary of the above schools was held on Sunday and Monday last. The school is a very successful one, the nurnb 3r of officers and teachers being 33 with an average attendance of 31. The scholars numbered 255 with an average attendance of 182. The annual cost of the school is about £33. Sermons were preached on Sunday afternoon and evening by the Rev 11 J Clifton (a former resident minister),and whose old friends rallied well to greet him and support the cause. On Monday evening there was a splendid service conducted by the Rev. A Wells, who preached an able sermon on "Our Lord's teaching with regard to little children." The singing throughout the anniversary was simply splendid, the children having been specially taught by Mr Rowe. The congregations were large, and the collectionstotalled nearly £3B. Summed up the anniversary was in every sense a great success. APPOINTMENT FOR A WIDNES VICAR.</p>
        <p>At a service held on Wednesday evening at St Paul's Church, the vicar (the Rev C K Stocks) announced that the living of St Mark's,Preston,had been offered to him. He had decided to accept the inyitation,and would take up hi duties in his new sphere of labour in a very short time. He expressed his regret at having to leave Widnes, but he felt under the circumstances that he could do more good in a wider sphere of labour. It is interesting to note that the Rev C K Stocks took up his appointment in Widnes about three years ago. Since that time his parish has been divided,and for some time he has been vicar'of St Paul's only. The vicariate of St Mary's is still under consideration. During the rev gentleman's sojourn in Widnes the church has passed through deep waters principally owing to the vexed problem of Sunday trading upon which the vicar and his curate (the Rev R E Corlett) hold the most advanced views in favour of its abolition.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>HITTING THE AVRONG MAN.</head>
        <p>HITTING THE AVRONG MAN.</p>
        <p>Constable Wilson bad an amusing if somewhat painful experience, on Tuesday morning last, when investigating the cause of a disturbance at the back of Mr Craddock's pawnshop in Church-street. It appears that an erring wife had not returned to her husband up to as late an hoUr as 12.15 a;m, Whilst the husband was sitting in the kitchen patitntly awaiting the arrival of his spouse, he heard her in the back entry evidently in the com- pany of some other man. He crept out with the intention of "milling'. that other man. Just at the same time Constable Wilson was investiga- ting the cause of the noise he beard, and had found Mrs Newport, Brunswick-street, Mrs Jones, Lock-street, and a sailor in the entry ; and, from what he was told, was about to knock at Newport's entry door, when lo! sud- denly it swung open and he received a stinging blow on the side of the head.—Naturally, Wilson brought David Newport up for assulting him, and Messrs :Imison and R H Posnett, adjudicated thereon,on Tuesday morning,at the Runcorn Police Court. The court inclined to the humorous side of the story, and prevailing on Wilson to see it in the same light, dismissed Newport with a caution to betsure and hit the right man next time.</p>
        <p>.--............</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</head>
        <p>RUNCORN BOARD OF GUARDIANS</p>
        <p>Mr J St lkes presided over the fortnightly rnneting a this Bpard on Tuesday afternoon, and there were also present—Mr A R Norman (vice-chairman), the R.v Dr Gastaldi, Messrs S Taylor, T Reynolds, T Wood, J Douglas, the members of the Rural Council, and Messrs G F Ashton (clerk), W E Dough (deputy clerk), and W Cunnah (master).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>MASTER, MATRON, AND THE SALARY. . _</head>
        <p>THE MASTER, MATRON, AND THE SALARY. . _</p>
        <p>Almiral Parry reported the result of the meeting of the Visiting Committee held that morning to consider the application of the master and matron for an increase of salary. After a very exhaustive enquiry the corn- mittee arrived at the conclusion tint the master's salary be increased from £9l to £ll4, and the matron's from £54 to £64. He gave notice that he would move a resolution OD the subject at the neat meeting. Mr Linaker disagreed with Admiral Parry that that matter received very exhaustive consideration at the meeting, for they were only occupied discussing it about fifteen minutes. In view of the fact that they had had information before them, and not sufficient time to con- sider it, be moved that the matter be referred back to the Visiting Committee for further consideration, and that a recommendation be submitted to them at their next meeting. While admitting that they had an excel- lent master and matron, he suggested that the increase in the salary was out of proportion to the requirements. Mr Woodward agreed with Mr Linalor. The subject required very careful preparation and investigation,and they could only say that it was hastily settled that morning. He seconded the motion. Admiral Parry never ceased to be surprised at what he heard in that room. He could not imagine why they should adjourn the matter for a fortnight. There was nothing further to be made known on the subject. The Master had been with them for 15 years on a salary of £94 a year, and had undertaken all the responsibilities, and yet they wished to let the question of an increase in his salary go on for a fortnight. The question was left over until'a later stage of the meeting, when on the suggestion of Mr Norman, it was decided that the applications should be considered a month hence, the committee to meet at 10.30 in the morning.</p>
        <p>FIRE PRECALTTIONS.</p>
        <p>ADMIRAL PARRY RESIGNS.</p>
        <p>Mr Dewey, the Local Government Board Inspector, who attended the meeting directed the attention of the Board to the question of facilities for the escape of in- mates from the diff,,rent wards in the house, in the event of an outbreak of fire. They seemed to possess a good supply of water hydrants all over the place. and a set of hose, so they would experience no difficulty in keeping a fire pretty well in hand for a time, but if the outbreak was a large one they would be unable to cope with it. He suggested the advisability of them placing themselves in communication with the Warrington Fire Station. He understood the Board had done something in that line but nothing effective. The experience of the fatal fires at Stafford and Newcastle, showed that a second means of escape from the upper rooms was indispensable. That was what they were trying to carry out, and they would not shrink from the heavy responsibility. He had been through the house, and noted at what points alterations could be made. The most important was with regard to the hospital. They had two large wards in that institution and practically only one staircase. To reach the second staircase they had to cross the first. It was proved at Stafford that it was not the ere, but the smoke which killed the in- mates. For that reason he recommended that two or three doors should be placed in the passage downstairs to prevent the smoke from going from one end of the house to the other. He thoueht they would have to put outside staircases off the children's—boys and girls— ward. In several cases the windows ought to be altered in such a manner as to allow of a man with a ladder effocting a rescue therefrom. The mister and matron were in possession of all his recommendations, and the House Committee would take them into con- sideration at the next meeting, and they would, he un- derstood, report the result to the Board. Admiral Parry said what they really wanted in the way of fire escapes, were men. If he was the president of the Local Government Board he would see that they had them. (Laughter.) Mr Woodward moved that a special committee he appointed to go into the details of the requirements with the Master, and prepare a report to be presented to the House Committee, and the Board at the next meeting. They wanted a few experts on the matter. Mr. Linaker seconded. Mr Woodward mentioned the names of Messrs Daven- port, Ant wise. Linaker' Reynolds, and the Chairman and Vice-Chairman of the Board for this Committee. Mr Norman considered that it practically amounted to an insult not including the Chairman of the House Committee (Admiral Parry) in that special Committee. Mr Woodward subseqiently proposed that Admiral ParrY, Messrs Rsynolcia, Davennort, Antwiss, and Linaker, and the Chairman end Vice-Chairman of the Board comprise the Committee. Admiral Parry declined to attend the Committee and resigned the chairmanship of the House Committee. The resolution was adopted. TEM RASTER'S REPORT. The Master reported that Mr Joseph Spruce, Guardian for Preston-alb-Hill. had sent a quantity of Illwers for the sick ward for a flower service, and also a parcel of literature for the inmates. The matron had appointed Ellen W Ryder, aged 22, as cook and laundress, at £25 per annum, with board, lodging, and washing, her duties to commence on July 16th. The report was approved. FINANCE. Mr`Woodward reported that the Finance'Committee hsd made payments for out-relief as follows :—Runcorn,£Bo: lirodshm. £24: and Budwortb, £25. He moved the adoption of the report. Mr Wright seconded and carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RESCUE BY A LADY AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>A lady showed great pluck and presence of mind on Saturday last, by jumping into the lake in Run- corn Park and rescuing a little girl who had un- fortunately fallen in. We record the fact to illu- strate that the deed was witnessed and duly appre- ciated, but withhold the names as a matter of delicacy. RuNicoux CYCLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>The destination ot this Club's run on Wednesday was Whitchurcb, but owing to the exceedingly bad weather the "dozen" who turned out to participate therein, were compelled to return after getting as far as Helsby Honsit AND CART IN THE CANAL AT RUNCORN. . _</p>
        <p>About nine o'clock on Friday morning a rather exciting incident was witnessed by a large crowd at Top Locks, Runcorn A horse and cart belonging to Mr H. Cawley, coal merchant, Runcorn, were standing at the bottom of Pig-lane, at the side of the Brid4ewater Canal, for the purpose of taking a load of coal from a boat. A dog belonging to Mr. Cawley, jumped at the horse's nose, making the animal start back, and in a moment the cart and horse were both in the canal. A number of men were soon on the scene, and under the supervision of Messrs H. Cawley and H. Ackerley the horse was freed from the straps fastening it to the cart. A rope was then attached to the animal, and by this means it was hauled across to the steps near the foot of Waterloo Bridge, where it got out of the water. After a smart trot it seemed none the worse for its bath. A rope was next attached to the cart and this was hauled across to the crane by the Manchester Ship Canal Company's warehouse, where it was lifted on to terra firma.</p>
        <p>AN EXTRAORDINARY CIGARETTE SMOXER. Coroner R. Dobson, held an noqueat at Runcorn Police Court, last Tuesday, on the body of Alfred Stringer, aged 16 years.—John Stringer, the father a labourer of 15 Water-street, said his lad, who was employed as a" boiler scaler" at the M.S.C: Co.. engineering works Old Quay, generally enjoyed good health; though he occasionally suffered from head and earache, About a month previously he was taken ill and died on Saturday last. Ile used to smoke a large quantity of cheap cigarettes, and when he had money would smoke several packets in an afternoon.—Dr Robinson testified to the deceased having succumbed to inflammation of the brain. Questioned as to the effect of the cigarette smoking, the doctor remarked it would naturally have an injurious effect on the undeveloped nerve centres of young people, but he inclined to the be- lief that entering boilers before they were properly cooled or under bad atmospheric conditions was probably the principal factor in the development of the disease.—The jury returned alverdict in accord- ance with the medical testimony. Is CHINA STONE POISONOUS ?—A LABOURER'S SAD DEATH. A case, which gives rise to the above query, came tinder the notice of Coroner R. Dobson at Runcorn Police Court on Tuesday, when an inquiry was made into the circumstances attending the death of John Corcoran, labourer, of 3, Vicar-street, Runcorn.—Mr F Whalley was foreman of the jury and there were also present :—Mr Yates (F 131.1. Factories) ; Mr F A Lake, solicitor for the widow; and Mr JR Digges (Manchester Ship Canal Co.)—Agnes Corcoran identified the body as that of her husband, aged 38 years. He had been obliged to leave his work at the Manchester Ship Canal Docks, Runcorn, as the result of a scratch he received whilst lifting a piece of china stone about 9 weeks previously. and had died on Sunday.—Bryan Conway, a labourer, 8, Cross-street, said he was shown the "scratch " by the deceased shortly after it occurred Corcoran told him, he got it whilst " trimming " a wagon of china stone. Deceased continued at his work for two or three weeks, but the finger got worse and worse, so that eventually he gave up.-- Dr J B Delaney, who attended deceased first on the 30th of April, then continuously from the 6th of of May, illustrated, how the inflammation from the scratch had gradually worked up the hand and arm until the latter had assumed abnormal proportions, and until practically the whole system had become poisoned. Deceased had not streagth enough to combat it, and finally succumbed to "exhaustion following septic icellulitis." Dr Robinson had also kindly assisted him with the case.—The jury returned a verdict in accordance with the medical testimony. WEDDING AT ST PAUL'S CHURCH. St Paul's Church sheltered a large congregation on Thursday afternoon, assembled to witness the nuptials of Mr John Robert S3ipley, son of Mr Samuel Shipley, "Westfield," Langtoft, York- shire, and Miss Alice Cooke, daughter of Mr Thomas Cooke,(relieving officer),Church-street, Runcorn. Miss Cooke is well known, and as a member of St Paul's Church Choir is deservedly popular, which added to the esteem in which her parents are held accounted for the largeness of the congregation. T•le bridegroom has only recently , returned to England from the Transvaal, where he has been resident for the past thirteen years, and has just completed twelve months honourable service in the ranks of the now famous "Cape Pioneer Regiment." The ceremony I was impressively conducted by the Rev A Wells (superintendent) assisted by the Rev J D Bisbrown (Cardiff) and son of Mr Daniel Bisbrown (Runcorn). The bridesmaids were Miss Annie Cooke (sister of bride) and Miss Tess Shipfey (sister of bridegroom), Mr F Shipley Leeds, (brother) being the best man; and Mr W Shaw,l Barton-on-Humber, groomsman. As the bride entered the church, leaning on the arm of her father, who gave her away, she looked very pretty in a dress of cream . fancy Foulard°, trimmed with mervileux silk and Honiton lace. This in turn was set off with a very becoming hat, and a choice bouquet of roses. The bridesmaids costumes were of French grey vigoreux cloth, trimmed with crepe de chene and lace, and they wore hats of har- monious character. Both carried bouquets of pink carnations and lilies of the valley. The bouquets were all gifts of the bridegroom as were also the charming assegai brooches worn by each. The serice was choral and the hymns "The voice that breathed o'er Eden," and other appropriate ones were heartily rendered. Mr W Cooke (brother of the bride) presided at the organ, and the music included :—" Wedding Chorus" (Cohen) ; "Bridal March Lobengrin ' (Wagner); and Mendelsohn's " Wedding March." ' After the ceremony the bridal party adjourned to the home of the bride's parents, and subsequently the happy couple left for Scotland. where the honeymoon will he spent. In August both will set Out for their new home in South Africa, and we are sure the good wishes of their Runcorn frien Is will be with them.</p>
        <p>CRAWFORD'S</p>
        <p>•</p>
        <p>CREAM CRACKERS, CRISP AND CREAMY. o ; •-•=;"?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>RUNCORN NOTES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN NOTES.</p>
        <p>[BY A CORRESPONDENT.]</p>
        <p>" Old Brunswick "celebrates its 101st Sunday School Anniversary on the 23rd June next, and in place of Mr Watkinson, hag secured the services ef the Rev E Lloyd Jones (Manchester). Mr Jones is an eminent and telling orator and will preach morning and evening, ae well as deliver his popu- , tar lecture "The Pilgrim's Progress," on Monday. A general desire is felt by the Brunswick friends that their Ist anniversary in the Twentieth Century should be a highly successful one.</p>
        <p>At length we learn of an entertainment and con- cert to be held in the Drill Hall, Runcorn, on June 26th, when public presentations will be made to the returned Runcorn Vilunteers. The occasion will also be notable on account of the unveiling of the" Roll of Honour," recently affixed in the Drill Hall, The proceedings are free to the public, daors being thrown open at 7.40 p.m.</p>
        <p>The resignation of Admiral Joces Parry from the position of Chairman of the House Committee of the Board of Guardians comes somewhat suddenly, like everything else the Admiral does. It is to be hoped, however,that he will recunsider his decision and be prevailed upon to again take up the reins.</p>
        <p>The Runcorn magisterial bench are accountable for some peculiar acts of justice. For instance they allow a strapping young labourer to "ran- shackle "an infirm lock-render for 16s 6d; and have his 'bit of fun." Whilst the first " beggar " who cornea along destitute has hardly uttered his plea for alms, before be is seized, tried, convicted,dubbed a " aisance " and sent into quarantine at Route- ford for seven days. lam not seriously objecting to the way they dole out justice; only remarking how peculiar it is!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>MISSIONARY EXHIBITION AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>MISSIONARY EXHIBITION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>In connection with the observance of the bicentenary of the Society f)r the Propagation of the Gospel in Foreign Parts, a missionary exhibition was held on Wednesday afternoon in a large field in Moughland-lane. The annual report states that while the gross total ex- ceeds that of the preceding year by about £42,000, there has been a decrease under the item of collections. subscriptions, and donations to the amount of about £1,200. The number of ordained mis- sionaries including twelve bishops, is 700. Of these 131 are natives labouring in Asia, and 53 in Afrira. There are also in the various missions about 2,900 lay teachers, 3,200 students in the society's colleges, and 38,000 children in the mission schools in Asia and Africa. Two hundred and fifty-nine persons were elected by ballot and added to the roll of incorpor- ated members during the year 1900. The supporters of the society in Runcorn, who nad arranged the exhibition under notice, had been most indefatigable in their efforts to make the enterprise a success rot only from the point of view of disseminating information as to the wide field covered by the society, but also from the financial standpoint. That they succeeded in intesesting and instructing those who visited the exhibition goes without saying. The financial result, however, will not oe such as to gladden the heart of the treasurer. The afternoon was about the most miserable which has been experienced for some time. From about 2.30 until late in the evening rain fell heavily, and instead of being able to enjoy a stroll on the greensward the few visitors who braved the ele- ments were penned in the tents during the whole aftes- noon. Among those present were :—The Revs Canon Maitland Wood (vicar), W H Binney (Northwich), H B 810-g (Frodsham), E Jertnyn (Helsbr), F J Phelps, W H Stables, H B Firth, E S Oliver, N J Poole, Colonel Lascelles, Mr E Imison, and most of the workers whose names are given below. The exhibition comprised curiosities from all parts of the world, which were suit- ably arranged in sections and carefully described. The committee were fortunate in securing the loan of soma very valuable exhibits from the society, and also from local people including the thrones and sceptre of King Prempeh, and a Chinese emperor's coat, belong- ing to Colonel Lascellas. The interest attaching to certain sections were considerable enhanced by excellent photographs of the natives and noteworthy places. In the centre of the marquee there was a stall containing the society's publications. Owing to"the unpropitious elements the committee bad to practically abandon a programme of outdoor sports and pastimes upon the arrangement of which much thought and labour had been bestowed. Refreshments were served in a tent, and a portion of this cover was used for an entertainment at intervals by a String band. The various committees who were responsible for the arrangements comprised the following:—Mesdames Kay, Ire_ison. Lake, Cropper, Guest, Littler, Owen, W Yates, A Abel, W Cellier ; Misses A Smith, Bate, Boyers, Buckle, Crosby, Dykes, Hankinson, E Johnson, Littler, Mack, Meredith, Pierpoint, Wiswall, Horsefield, Johnson, Joynson, Marshall, Percival, and Warner ; IRevs W H Stables, H B Firth (secretary), W Scholes. E S Oliver, Dr Howarth, Messrs Littler, Hewitt, Weston, Lightburn, Markham, Carlsen, Bankes, Neild, West- brook, Blain, Haddock, Dudley, Clare, Bowles, Austin, P Robinson, W H Bankes, Boraston, Lawrenson, Pres- ton, Shaw, Armstrong, Banner, Brown, Clarke, Dun- bavand, Hitchmough, Hopwood, Lane, Sherlock, F Sherlock, J Wilding, G Cropper, B Gibbs, Gibson, Kill- cross, Peacock, Pearson and Williams.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>JOINT WATER CoMMITTEE. —At a meeting held on Friday, in the Court-room, Mr John Barrow presiding,a letter was read from Mrs Bowden, of Overton, com- plaining of the quality of the water, and the Clerk was instructed to intimate that the mains were being flushed weekly to cleanse the joints.—A long letter was read from the Lordship Water Collector, Informing the Committee that his remuneration for the work done was inadequate. After some consideration it was resolved that toe sum of £lO be paid to him for work to the end of March and that the Committee fully consider the future remuneration to be paid to him.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER T IN CLOCK. BRIDGB-STREHT.—Advt.</head>
        <p>LADIES' BEST GLACE BOOTS AND SHOES UNDER T IN CLOCK. BRIDGB-STREHT.—Advt.</p>
        <p>OUR LOCAL CHURCHES.</p>
        <p>The Ragged School Mission.</p>
        <p>Anniversary services were held on Sunday afternoon and evening, when collections in aid of the funds were taken and amounted to £8 7s 20. The address on each occasion was given by Mr R A Naylor, of Thelwall. In the afternoon the chair was taken by Mr J Charlton Parr (president of the mission), who expressed the pleasure it afforded him to be present and to know that such a good work waq being done by the officers of the 'Shoo!. He spoke words of encouragement to the chil- dren and his address was listened to with the deep est Interest throughout. Mr Naylor in the course of his address at the evening service said be .was very pleased to hear the children sing so nicely,aad they were thankful to Mr. Fanshaw for his kindness in raining them so well and devoting so much time to the work. Mr. Naylor then proceeded to deliver an address 011 There is another and a better world," words which were, he said, taken from a play written by a German strd translated into Engli.h and performed almost over the whole of the Uruted Kingdom. The play was entitled "The Stranger" and in connection with the words be quoted a sorrowful incident which happened in Liverpool The leading part in the piece was taken by a celebrated actor named John Farmer and shortly after he had uttered the words "There is another and a better world" he fell down and died. This world, the speaker went on to say, was very beautiful but it would be more beautiful still if it were fall of love. Suitable hymns were sung at each service by the cbildren,who acquitted themselves remarkably well, vsluable assistance being also rendered by a small orchestra. Two hymns, com- posed by Mr. Naylor, were also sung with effect, and the manner in which the whole of the music was ren- dered was a credit to all concernti. Mr. Fanshaw, who had carefully trained the children, made a highly efficient conductor. Mr Frank Owen was at the organ. Sunday School Anniversary at Weston</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>Point.</head>
        <p>Point.</p>
        <p>The anniversary services in connection with the Wes- ton Point Wesleyan Sunday School were held on Sunday and were laigely attended. The preacher was the Rev C H Hocken, of Warrington, who gave two thoughtful disc-mrses on Sunday school work. In the afternoon a musical service was held, at which the choir of Camden Wesleyan Chapel, Runcorn, distinguished themselves with a number of approprie.te selections. The service was preFiled over by Mr F J Norman. The collections for the day realised over £l9.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</head>
        <p>Bold-street Wesleyan Church.</p>
        <p>The preacher at this church on Sunday morning was the Rev J Wells, of Runcorn, and in the evening the Rev H G Roberts. The latter gentleman based his remarks on A religion of self-respect—commendation of the conscience," speaking from the words, "By manifestation of the truth, commending ourselves to every man'a consciPnce in the sight of God." The chmr creditably rendered the anthem, "My soul doth magnify the Lord," Mr F Hickman presiding at the organ.</p>
        <p>OLD FALSE TEETH BOUGHT.</p>
        <p>Fall value in cash or offer p3r return of post. R.D. and J. B. Prater. Ltd., Princes-street. Ipswich. The largest and 'oldest buyers In the world.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <p>WARRINGTON Funeral of the Late Mr. H. Toft. The funeral of toe late Mr H Toft, tobacconist, Sankey-street, whose death was reported in our last issue, took place in the Cemetery on Sunday after- noon in the presence of a large number of relatives and friends, the former including the father and son of the deceased. At one o'clock a special service was held in St. Paul's Church, conducted by the Vicar (the Rev E R Terbuck), who read the greater part of the service for the Burial of the Dead. Mr. Tarbuck also officiated at the graveside. A large number of beautiful wreaths haa been received. St Barnabas' School. "The St Barnabas Church Magazine" or this .4, month states that there are as many children on the books as can be accommodated by the present buildings. One sign of the fulness of the schools at present was shown in the Ascension Day school children's service, which had to be continued on Friday, owing to the combined attendance of all the scholars being too large for seating in the church, The district has been fortunate this year in escaping the epidemic of measles which has been so prevalent in the other parts of the town, and, as a consequence, the school has continued its work during the month, when all the other schools of Warrington, with one other exception, have been closed. This, of course, must mean an advantage to both school and children, as the allotted work must be done in each case, and twelve months' work spread over twelve months must leave better and more permanent results than the same work compressed into eleven months. A Warrington Prisoner Dies in Strangeways Gaol.—An Obstinate Patient. The death occurred in Strangewsys Gaol, Man- chester, this week, of William Oldham, a Warring- ton man, who was servings term of two months' imprisonment for larceny. Deceased who was 52 years of age, was admitted into the gaol on April 125th and as he was suffering from bronchial catarrh was ordered light labour only. On the 3rd instant he was admitted to hospital, but be frequently refused to take either medicine or nourishments. At the inquest which was conducted by Mr F J Price, on Thursday, Dr Edwards stated that the man might have recovered had he not been so obstinate. The jury returned a verdict of death from natural causes.—lt will be remembered that Oldham had frequently been before the magistrates for theft, and the opinion has often been expressed by the Bench that he was not exactly in his right mind. Doctors, it appears, were of a contrary opinion. ALLEGED CRUELTY TO A COW AT R1XT0N,..." WORM IN</p>
        <p>THE TAIL."--bINGULAE CASE. At the Warrington County Petty Sessions on Wednesday, John Hindley, farmer, of Riston, was summoned for illtreating a cow by cutting off a portion of its tail on May 24'h.—Mr T 9 Steel appeared for the defendant and pleaded not guilty.—P G Spence said that at 145 on the afternoon of May 24th he saw one of defendant's cows hoing along Manchester-road, Rixton. It was bleeding at its tail, and there was blood on its hind quarters. When asked to account for the blood, defendant said the cow had suffered from "worm in the tail," and he bad treated it by cutting off an inch and a half from the end of the tail and letting it bleed. This would be beneficial to it, and was the right way of treat- ing it.He performed the operation at 6 30 that morning. Inspector Print, R.S.P.C.A., stated that at 11.30 on the 25th May he examined the cow and found its tail was still bleeding. He and P.C. Spence bandaged the tail, and the blood came through the rag. He went again on tbe Sunday morning and saw the defendant who told elm that the animal was suffering from " worm in the tail "and fie had cut the end of the tail off to care the disease. When he saw the cow a few days later it was being taken care of in a shed and was certainly getting better.—Mr Steel : Where do you suggest the cruelty comes in ? —Witness said it was cruel Co out the tail off at 630 on Friday morning, and allow it to bleed until the following day —Mr J Dixon,V.S., who had not examined the cow,said there were no such disease as " worm in the tail," and it showed gross ignorance to treat for such an ailment. Mr Steel, who defended, then read from a treatise by ' Lambert, in wbich it was recommended that when a cow suffered from " worm in the tail "a portion of the tail should be taken off and the tail allowed to bleed. Mr Dixon, however, maintained that there was no such disease, and that the only reason for amputating , a cow's tail was in case of an injury, and even then it should be ligatured.—Mr Steel stated that it bad always been the defendant's practice, when a cow was suffering from "worm in the tail" to cut off a small portion of the tail and allow it to bleed. When a cow was suffering from that disease the end of the tail became paralysed,and unless it was attended to, paralysis would extend up the whole of the tail and probably mortification would set ia. On the date in question defendant cut off the end of the tail but it did not bleed much. The cow was turned into the field, but unfortunately got through the hedge and was found in the road by the policeman. It most probably caught the tail in the hedge and caused it to bleed. Stephen Dean, of Orford, who prior to the passing of the Veterinary Surgeons' Act, practised al a farrier, said the only remedy for" worm in the tail " was to bleed the tail. Be had cured many cases. It would have been better to have kept the cow in the shippon after the operation had been carried out.—The Bench charactized the pro- cess as absurd, but, as the society did not wish to press the case, dismissed it with a caution.</p>
        <p>R. A. GITTOES,</p>
      </div>
