      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</head>
        <p>BEFORE the MAGISTRATES</p>
        <p>POLICEMEN HAVE A WAIIM TIME.—WOMAN CHARGED WITH OBSTBUCTION.—THE CHIEF CONSTABLE INDIGNANT. Mary Ann Steel, wife of Samuel Steel, 1, Old-eardens, appeared at the 'Warrington Police Court on Monday, in answer to a summons charging her with wilfully obstructing P,C.'s Clough and Rylett whilst acting in the execation of their duty on the 9th inst.—Mr Browne represented the defendant, who pleaded not guilty.— The Chief Constable said the proceedings arose out of a case which was before their Worships the previous Monday, when a young man was locked up for gamine in Garden-row and was convicted. Be (Mr Talbot) then mentioned that two constables had been obstructed very considerably by a certain woman. There might have been ancther man arrested if it had not been for the obstruction. He then said he 'would take proceed- ings against that woman with the result that this case was now before the Court.—P.C. Clrugh said that at twenty minutes past eleven on Sunday morning,the 9th mat, he was in plain clothes in company with P.C. 'ellen near the Old Gardene,Winwick-road. There was large gang of men gambling there and witness severed one prisoner. He would have caueht another but the defendant was "hustling and jostling " in front of him and prevented him getting hold of another young man. PC. Rylatt was pursuing some other men. The obstruction was wilfully done. One of the young men dropped a watch and guard and this woman picked them up. She behaved in a most disorderly manner, using bad language. and the police were obliged to take her to the Police Station.—By.Mr Browne : Defendant never touched him with her hands. He saw her put the watch in her dress rocket. Owing to her disorderle conduct'she was taken'to the Po‘iceStation It Was nrt bec3use they suspected her of taking the watch that she was talc. n into custodv—P.C• It'vlatt stated that when he and dough approached the men a whistle was blown and they ran a. ay. Witness chased One of the men and Clough arrested another. He was prevented from arresting another of the off 'riders by the prisoner. Thee were both considerably obstructed—ln answer to Mr Browne witness said that the defendant did not olestruet him.—Mr Browne contended that this was a case which ought not to have been brought into Court. Tbe defendant had lived in the Old Gardens, where she was born, frr 54 years, and she had never been charged with an effence before. She had actually reported men to P.C. Clough for placing at cards Evian admitted that she did not obstruct him and he submitted that if further investigation had been made the case would net have b ten brought into court. The proceedings had been taken in a !rose manner, and— The Chief Constable (warmly) : I protest against such a remark'—Mr Browne : You can protest as much ae you like.—The Chief Constable : I put it to the Bench littat Mr Browne's remarks are improper,—Mr Browne : Why Rylatt admi'l that he was not interfered with. The woman did not t uch him. Continuing Mr Browne said defendant never interfered In the slightest degree. She ran out on hearing the whistle to see whet was the matter. She did not pick the watch up or obstruct the officers in any shims or form.—After hearing further evidence the Bench decided to tinerthe defendant 10s.—Mr Browne asked for a copy of the conviction. Be said his client had been charged with obstructing both rfficers, and the summons had not been amended. Rylatt had admitted that the woman did not obstruct him.—Councillor Smethurst : This Is not the full decision of the Bench.—Mr Browne : I saw that, it is the decision of the majority. Rylatt has sworn he was net interfered with. How, therefore,can yen convict ? It is a farce.—The Clerk said the sum- mons could be amended.—Mr Monks : It is only a technical matter.—The Clerk : That is so. It was ultimately decided to strike out the name of Rylatt in the summons. NIAnsiAnINE DEALERS SMARTLY FINED. At the County Borough Police Court on Monday, be- fore Mr F Monks and other magistrates, John Jones, a provision dealer carrying on business at 71, Catherine-- street, was summoned- for exposing for sale on May 24th a quantity of margarine which was not prsperly labelled.—lnepectar Norman stated that he visited the shop for the purpose of obtaicing samples of food and inside a gime case saw twenty-five pieces of margarine which were not properly labelled expesed for sale. Pointing to one piece be asked for three-quarters of a pound of that 'butter," and was told that it was margarine.—ln answer to defendant, the Inspector admitted that the greater portion of the margarine was made up in half-inch packages and the word "margar- ine " was printed on these. The letters were enly half an inch square, and the law required labels of one atel a half inch letters. Another piece was not labelled at all.—Defendant, who pleaded ignorance of the requirements of the laws was fined 40s, Richard Nerraway, Hoyle-atreet, Was fined 40s for a similar offence.—Renecca Penketh, 237, Winwick-rosd, was fined 10a for a like offence, the magistrates being of the opinion that she committed it in ignerance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>MINOR OASES.</head>
        <p>MINOR OASES.</p>
        <p>Warrington Borough Court.</p>
        <p>FROIDAT.—Before the Mayor (in the chair), Councillors Smethutst and J Roberts and Mr Thornton. - THEFT nom THE YMCA Rooms.—William Henry Boyle, . a stranger. was charged with stealing a macintosh value £l, the property of George Samuel Heathdale. Irons the Young Men's Christian Association Rooms, Friars Green, yesterday usorning.—He pleaded guilty.—The Chief Constable asked for a remand for eight days in order that inquiries might be made into the prisoner's antecedents. The prosecutor called at the Association rooms at 11 o'clock the previous morning and placed the macintosh in the cloakroom., On going for it a quarter of an hour later he found it had gene. Prisoner was seen handling the macintosh by the wife of the caretaker, and later In the day he pledged it at a shop in Yifinwick-road for 2s 6d, stating that it was his property. He was arrested at Preseet In the eveniug.—The remand was granted. FIXED FOR ABSSUL'r.—John Barry, 32, Parr-street, was tined 5s and costs for assaulting Eliza Ellen Skelhorn, 46, Chorlev-street, on the Nth inst.—lt was stated that defend- ant kicked the complainant and also struck her in the face, saying that he " would do six mouths for her."—Clara Kelly 2, Back Stamforcl•street was fined 6s 6cl for assaulting Catherine Nally, Smith's Court. ITALIA'S ICE CREAM WESDiR IN TROUBLE —Malpelli Pieluto, an Italian ice cream vendor, living at 102.Winwick- street, was summoned for committing wilful damage to a fence, the property of John Warburton.6, Ellis-street, to the amount oflOs.Prosecutor stated that defendant went into his garden and on being told to come out,climbed over the fence and in doing so, committed the damage.—After hearing evid- ence the Bench came to the conclusion that defendant was in Ignorance that he was doing wrong, and dismissed the summons on payment of costs(33 6d.)—Defendant could not understand English and an interpreter had to be employed</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>EVERY WOMAN</head>
        <p>EVERY WOMAN</p>
        <p>, Should send two stamps for our 32 page Illustrated Book, • containing valuable information how all Irregularities and _obstructions may be entirely avoided or removed by simple - means. Recommended by eminent Physicians, as the owy Safe, Sore, and Genuine BemedY. Never fai.s. Thousands of Testimonials. DU P. BLANCH4II.I), Dalston Lane, London.</p>
        <p>• • • -N., 4 or I,* ,r .</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Before Mr 7. Monks (in the chaff). Alderman Heaton, Councillor Smethurst, Messrs Wiight, Hayward</p>
        <p>and Thornton. YOIITHFUL Galll3l.NßS.—Three small boys named Michael Towey, 6 Foundry-street, James Hickey. 4 Brown's Yard, and Charles btuart, 1 Silver square, were summoned for playing pitch and toss in Garven Place, Sankey-street on the 20th inst—P,C. Clough proved the case, and the magistrates ordered the boys to be kept in the cells for 24 hours- ' Tag SICVAE tHROWING NUISANCE.—Harry Hunter a boy Iliving at 13, lierfoot-strcet, was summoned for throwing stones in Dallam-lane on June Ttla.— P.C. Cicagh stated that the defendantiwas throwing macadam stones at the lamp posts and telegraph poles.—The Chief Constable informed the Bench that the same day on which the offence was com- mitted lie received a complaint from the Post Office engineer about stone throwing at telegraph wires and insulators in the district.—Mr Taylor, the secretary of the Corporation Gas Deportment, also complained strongly of the damage to gas I lamps in this way.—A fine of 2s 6d was inflicted. No LIGHT.—On the evidence of P C Corbett, Jonathan Broadhurst, '6l. Bottock-street, was fined 5s Tor not having a lighted lamp attached to his vehicle on the night of June 4th. WEDNESDAY—Beton, Mr Ifirchall (in the chair), Alderman Heaton and Mr J Geddes. SUSPICLOUS CIIARACTER.—Mary Ellen Folen, of no settled address, was charged with having been found in possession of A pair of lady's boots without being able to give a satisfac- tory account of them.—P.C. Turner said he was on duty in Rose and Crown-street at one o'clock that vnorning when he met the prisoner in company with a man. Noticing that she was a stranger and was carrying something wrapped in white paper he stopped her and asked her what she had in the parcel. She told him that he had better look, and on doing so he found the pair of lady's boots (produced). She told him that she bad bought them in Manchester, and had given 2s lid for them. He asked her if she knew what shop she got them at, and prisoner replied that she was in drink at the time and did not remember where she had bought them. When further questioned, she stated that a gentleman with whom she travelled in the train from Man- chester to Warrington gave them to her. These answers not being satisfactory, witness locked her up.—Superlntend- ent Monaghan asked the magistrates to remand prisoner until Saturdsy In order that inquiries might be made.—The magistrates agreed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
