      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>Warrington Occasional Court.</head>
        <p>Warrington Occasional Court.</p>
        <p>TUEBDAY.—Before Mr. F. A. Frost. DAMAGING POT•T 3 itooirs AT GB APPENHALL.— John Massey. lodging in Jockey-street, Warrington. was charged with damaging, with intent to steal, at Grappenhall. on Monday, a quantity of potato plants, the property of Levi James..—John James, Cheater-road. Grappenhall, said his father was a farmer. At a quarter to seven on Monday morning he saw prisoner about half way down his father's eld. Prisoner looked up Chester-road and then hurried tack again. When witmess got up to him he was pulling several potato roots up and putting them into the bag (pro- duced.) The potatoes were worth 2d and he had done damage to the amount of Is.-- lergeant Bourne said prisoner used to live at Grappmthall. and was much addicted to drink. He asked for him to be made an example of, as numerous com- plaints had been reeeivai about this class of offence.—A fine of 2s 6d and costs was inflicted, or, in elefault, seven days' imprisonment. Prisoner went to gaol.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</head>
        <p>Runcorn Petty Sessions.</p>
        <p>MONDAY.—Befo,e Mere Henry Reynolds and W</p>
        <p>Timmins.</p>
        <p>KILLING GAME.—Joseph Dobson, 60 York-street, Runcorn, was fined 40s for killing game without a certificate. The case ,was proved bytalr J Donnellan (I. 11.0. and John Lloyd Lunt, a youth of Halton Castle View.—Mr H M Rogers, Chester. prosecuted. Scnoor, Casus.—John Jones, Porter-street. and Mary Jane Ferriday, Surrey-street, were fined 6d and 4s 61 costs for bleaches of the Education Act.—Under the new-byt. laws George Colciough, Norton-street was fined ss; Emma Water- , son, WiTerafs-place 10s 6d and A Ravenscroft St John-street 5s for similar offences The Chairman intimated that all future cases would come under the new bye-laws, whereby they woilld have power to Indict fines up to ZOs. He advised the public to take notice of the fact. A PECULIAR CASE.—Kate Ethel Woods. a married woman summoned her husband rhomas Woods. for desertion, and was desirous of obtaining a separation and maintenance order under • the Married Woman's Act, 'lB9s.—From the evidence, it appeared the parties were married about two years ago and resided at 33. Portland-street. Runcorn. The , mother-in-law lived with them, and owned the furniture, , whilstilthe' house was in Woods's name. Defendant Was a chemical labourer, and earned 188110 d a week at Brunner Mond's works,Lostock Gralarn. The wife alleged he deserted her on the 13th May- She had two children aged 13 and 5 , months, respectively, and since the 12th of May she had only received ls from her husband.—The husband denied having deserted his wife. All he could say was that he could not live with her mother. Frequently they were falling out, but he would not give any details. He had made his wife an offer to go and live with him at Lostock, and tne offer was still open ; but it was quite clear he could not keep a house at Runcorn and one at Lostock on 13s 10d a week.— The Chairman said they did not consider it a ease of deser- tion, or that complainant had been treated hardly. They felt inclined to dismiss the case, but if that was done complain- ant would have no remedy. Conseqnently they would alourn the case for a week to see whether the parties could make it up. 1 "A BIT 01 Ftrx."—Frederick Langley, labourer, oi Perot- , val's-lane. ItunCorn was brought up on remand on a charge of doing grevious bodily harm to William Hignett, a lock tenter in the employ of the Manchester Ship Canal Corn- ' pany. The charge was reduced to onelot common assault.— It appeared that Langley in company with Thomas Conner. was coming along the Canal bank from Weston, and being primed with drink to an extent to cause him to go in for "a Mot tun." he naught hold of Higglns—(who by the way is 61 years of age and in weak health)—and ••ranshackledr him. The process of "ranshacklisag,- as described by Higgins. was grasping him by the shoulders, shaking him and dropping him on the It or.—Defendant was ordered to nay costs. OTHER CASES.—Williarsi Penny. a Liverpool man was sent to prison for one month for refusing to pay £7 4s arrears of a maintenance order granted his Wife in 1897.—Frederick Duckett of Crewe. was obliged to go to prison for 14 days, for using obscene language on the 9th of July 1899, inasmuch as he amid not pay the fine 16; 6,1. At the time the offence was-committed Duckett was a labourer at Runcorn Docks. , WARRINGTON TANNERS IN TROUBLE.—George and Henry Foster, tanners of Warrington, pleaded guilty to being drunk and disorderly in Lord-street, on Sunday the 16th June.—P.C. Bowyer proved the case. The men were light- ing and creating a great disturbance.—Defendants who said they were sorry were each fined I.os. A DIFFICULTY WITH I WOMAN.—Mary Ellen Bones, who was remanded last week, on charges of drunk and disorderly on the sth and 9th of June respectively, was again before the eon&amp; -.Superintendent Pearson intimated that the woman had been in the Workhouse during the week and was seen by a doctor. It was evident that her extraordinary conduct WRS due to her having been drinking heavily, and to her recent confinement of twins. He would withdraw the charges if she would return to her husband.—Prisoner "I will not live with him on any consideration. Am I bound to live with him when he is killing me r —Aslthe prisoner seemed determined not to reside with her husband the oases were proceeded with.—The Bench imposed a line of 5s and 85 6d costs Or seven daya• A DISGRACE TO RUNCORN RintavEn.—Emma Burrows, Theresa Yarwood. Henry William James.and Frank Garland, were charged, on the Information of the police, with nnlaw- fully twisting in the management of a house of ill-repute, at No, 1 Egerton-street, Runcorn, on divers dates between the ' sth of February and 6th June.—Only the defendant Burrows answered to the summons, and pleaded not guilty.—After a revolting story had been told by the Police. the Bench sen- tenced Burrows to two calendar months with hard labour.— 1 Warrants were issued for the anprehensien of the others.</p>
        <p>A PINT LEMON BLANCMANGE FREE. For the bmefit of those who appreciate a good Blanc- Mange. we with to make it as widely known as possible that a pint of most delicious Lemon Blanc-Mange, deli- cately tinted, and eaquisitely favoured with Lemon can be made from a Id packet of E ffel Tower ' Lemon Blanc-Mange Powder. You should obtain it' from your Grocer. But for those who cannot get it we may say that Messrs Poster. Clatic and Co.. 3796 " Eiffel Tower " Factory. Maidstone (who are also the makers of the celebrated " Eiffel Tower " Lemonade), are wisely sending sufficient to mak/ a pint free on I receipt of a postcard—we say wisely, because those I who once try it wilt certainly continue to use it.</p>
        <p>State of Employment in Lancashire. According to the reports ot correspondents in the Lancashire district to the " Labour Gazette " spinners in the Oldham district report employment as good. In Rochdale it is moderate, and in Bolton and vicinity it shows a decline. In Chorley, Bury, and Wigan it is moderate. Employment is fair at Blackburn, and good at Darwen and Accrington, and moderate in Manchester. Employment in the weaving trade shows a decline at Nelson and Black- burn. fairly good at Burnley, slack in Accrington and Preston, and not so good at Colne. In Darwen a number of looms are stopped for want of warps. In Macclesfield spinners and weavers are on short time. In Oldham employment in again reported slack in calico, and fustian weaving, and, moderate in velvet weaving ' No change is reported in the Bolton District, several mills being still on short time. Employment with engineers and metal trades continues moderate generally but fair in the Burnley district. Building , trades are moderate in Oldham ; slightly better in Bolton. Good with painters and decorators in Blackburn. In Itanchester and lVarrington employ- ment is reported as moderate,and in Stockport bad. Coal miners in the Oldham and Shaw districts are working five days a week. They are less actively employed in Bolton, but in Burnley and Accrington employment is fairly good. Printers report ' employment as bad in Manchester,fair at Stockport Macclesfield, and Warrington ; bookbinders and lithographic artists as moderate ; lithographic printers t•Ad;'stereotypers fair. Cabinet makers and French polishers in Manchester are in fair employ- ment. Tailors in the bespoke and ready-made departments in Manchester are fairly busy. Felt hat makers, cloth cap makers, and umbrella makers are quiet. I ROYAL</p>
