      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>WARRINGTON TRAMWAYS. . Inquiry at the Town Hall.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON TRAM- WAYS. . Inquiry at the Town Hall.</p>
        <p>Interesting Proceedings.</p>
        <p>At the Town Hall,Warrington,on Thursday last week, Lieutenant-Colonel Von Donop, RE.' conducted an inquiry into the application of the Corporation for sanction to borrow £66,050 for tramway purposes. Amongst those present were- the Mayor (Alderman Roberts,) Aldermen Heaton, Sutton, Shaw Green, Coun- cillors Plumpton. John Evans, William Pierpoint, Wilkinson, Peacock. Brough, Tinnion, Smethurst, A J Jolley, Bennett, Dr Fox, Messrs J L Tunstall, Hatton, E L Fleming, J W Hayward, Gougb, J Cliffe J Pem- berton, Charles Longshaw, A Horrocks, R Lewis. The Town Clerk represented the Corporation ' • Mr A M M Langdon, barrister, Manchester (in.atructed by Mr Steel) opposed the application on behalf of Mr Adam Horrocka, a ratepayer; Mr A Browne represented the Manchester and Liverpool Transport Company, and other opponents of the scheme included Mr John Pemberton, Mr S Gough and Mr Cliffe. THE CASE FOR THE CORPORATION.</p>
        <p>The i•OW-N CLE-ITE;" i-11.--0/301-13g the ir-r";C•ecii-e.gs said the application was made under the Warrington Corporation Tramways Order. By that Order eleven tramways were authorized to be con- structed in the Borough. The present scheme was for the construction of tramways from Sankey Bridges through the town to the Kautsford-road swing bridgc. and also along Wilderspxal Causeway. ITenders had been provisionally accepted by the Cor- poration, acting upon the advice of Messrs Preece and Cardew, the consulting engineers for the project, for the construction of the Liverpool-road to the Kautsford road tramways. The tenders accepted were the lowest sent in as follows :—permanent way, £28,789 ; overhead equipment, 44,833 193 ; cars, £5,058 15s. The estim- ated cost of the Wileerspool Causeway lines was based upon the tenders which had been received for the other system. In addition to the tenders accepted there would be an estimated expenditure of £6,000 for the erection lof a! carshed and workshop on the site of the old gas, works in Mersey-street, £350 for feeders, and £3,368 63 I for contingencies and engineering. The present scheme would involve widenings of the roadway which would cost a furtheroum of £2,2oo,hringing the total estimated cost of the two lines to £66,050. The question had arisen as to whether it would be possible to use the present setts with which the roadways were constructed in connection with the proposed tramway schemes,butthe Tramways Committee was advised by Messrs Preece rnd Cardew that a deeper set than 4 inch cube was necessary. Wannoarleigh-street would be paved with wood blocks the whole length and the existing granite setts would have to be taken up. This was owing to the County Court abutting the street. The Corporation had come to an agreement with the promoters of the Warrington and Northwiets Light Railway to purchase a loop line from them which would connect the Warrington Tram- way terminus at the Stockton Heath swing bridge, proceed along Grappenhall-road to Kautsford- road swing bridge and there rejoin the Warrington system. The Corporation had also entered into an agree ment with the South Lancashire Tramways Company to connect their trarn.way from Barlestown with the Corporation tramway which would terminate on the northside of the borough. The road wicienings which he had referred to would be at the corner of Wininar- leigh-street,the corner of Rylands-street where the Cor- poration had paid £6OO for taking off the corner of Mr Ralph Johnson's shop, and also at the corner of S. James-street and Wilderspool Causeway.</p>
        <p>EVIDENCE BY THE ELECTRICAL ENGINEER. Mr PREECE, Of Messrs Preece and Cardew, of London, the consulting engineers to the Corporation, was then called. At the outset he stated that his senior partner, Major Cardew would have been present, but unfortn nately he was ill, and therefore he (Mr Preece) had to take his place. Mr Preece, after detailing the estimates, stated that the rails would be six inches deep with 4 grooves and 901 be. weight per yard. It was proposeu to pave within the rails with 5 inch granite setts, and also outside the rails for a distance of eighteen inches. It would be impossible to utilise the existing 4 inch setts owing t 3 the depth of the rail. The 4 inch setts would have to be taken up, but they could be utilised in other parts of the town. Some of the setts were much worn and possibly these could not be used again. The over- head equipment would be provided, and would be attached to poles. A few rosettes would be fixed to buildings in certain places. The INSPECTOR : Are they side poles or centre poles ? Mr PREECE : Practically all side poles. Continuing he said the cars would be of the double-decked pattern The accommodation at the old gas works was ample for the cars. The Corporation owned the whole of the land and in addition to the car sheds a repairing shop would be provided. The site was exceedingly convenient for the present scheme and for any further developments. THE STATE OF THE BRIDGES. , Mr. BROWNE said he appeared on behalf of the Man- chester and Liverpool Transport Company who had a wharf near Warrington bridge. The company's objec- tion was that the bridge was not suitable to take the tramways over. The INSPECTOR : I am afraid I cannot go into that. Parliament has considered that matter and the Cor- poration have got a Parliamentary order authorising them to cross the bridge with the tramway. Mr Gouou : Surely the Board of Trade can inter- fere if it is not safe.</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR : The Board of Trade cannot over- ride the decision of Parliament.</p>
        <p>Mr Gonon : If it is -i;-; narrow and dangerous surely the Board of Trade will not sanction cars passing over It?</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR: lam not here to settle whether the tramways can be made or bow they are to run. lam here to ascertain whether there is any reason why the Corporation should not borrow money to construct tramways which have been authorised by Parliament. Mr Gocos : There is Black Bear Bridge which Is too narrow.</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR: That is a question I cannot discuss now.</p>
        <p>-- - Mr GOtrou : We are raising an objection to it. The INSPECTOR: I will make a note of it. Mr BROWNE : Is It a single lino that you propose to run Over the bridge or a double line? Mr PREECE: One line only. Mr BROWNE : I suppose you know there was a pro- posed arrangement with the County Council respectiog a new bridge? a ,The INSPECTOR : lam afraid I mustlstop you. It bas mAlaing to do with this inquiry. Mr BeovniE : Your Inquiry is a purely financial matter then The INSPECTOR: That is so. Mr LANGDON (on behalf of Me Elorrocks, a ratepayer) asked Mr Preece whether:he had considered the ques- tion of electric power. So far as he could see there was no provision in the estimates for power. MR. PREECE UNDER CROSS-EXa/MINATION. Mr PREECE pointed out that the Electricity Com- mittee would supply the necessary power for the tramways. In answer to further questions be said he Could not say what charge the committee Intended to make for power but he had estimated that it would be supplied at not more than a penny or three half- pence per unit. The plant which was being put down at the electric lighting station would be sufficient to supply the necessary energy for the tramways without additional capital expenditure. The capacity at the present moment was 1,000 horse power, and that would be sufficient for electric lighting and driving tilt tramcars. It was a new industry at Warrington and the station was only started at Christmas. The power required for the present system of tramways would not exceed 100 horse power. He believed 8 cars would be sufficient for the Liverpool-road and linutsford-road route and 3 cars for the Wilderspool route. It was intended to have a ten minutes service.</p>
        <p>Mr LANGDON : Here you are selecting certain roads out of Boyersl which have been authorised for tramway purposes. If they fail El pay the burden will have to be shared by the whole body of ratepayers, despite the fact that only a certain portion will benefit by them, Mr PREECE : I think it is the intention of the Car poration to construct the whole of the tramways au' thorised, but it was felt inadvisable to pull up the fine main arteries of the town all at the same time. The inconvenience would be very great and therefore certain sections are being proceeded with at first. The TOWN CLERK: It is the intention of the Corpora- tion to proceed with the other tramways when the present are completed.</p>
        <p>. - Mr PREECE, in answer to further quntions by Mr Langdon said In order to make the tramways pay they would have to earn lOicl per car mile. His figures were based upon the returns published by the Board of Trade. He believed the tramways would yield enough to make a profit. The 'I own CLERK : The Corporation Faye received offers from a large company to run the tramways for them and pay interest and sinking fund on the whole of the capital. Mr LANGL.OI4 : Oh, that alters the view of my client. Mr PREECM, re-examined by the Town Clerk, said it was noc intended to use the proposed line in Mersey. street for passengers. It was simply in order to get the cars to the shed in Mersey-street. By Mr (30171311 : 119 W many passengers would be required to make up this 100 per ear mile Mr PREECE: It all depends upon the fares charged. Mr notion : Is the charge to be a penny a mile ? Mr PREECE : I cannot tell. MrG 017G11 : How many would it require in the car at one time to make UID 104c1 a mile? Mr PREECE ; Well, at a penny a mile I should say 10i , persons. (Laughter.) I Mr GOUGH : Are they going to charge a penny a mile? Mr PREECE : 1 cannot answer. The usual way .of calculating the income of a tramway is by taking Am income-, dividing it by the mileage run by the cars, and you arrive at a certain revenue per car mile. The average amount in towns of this class is somewhat over twelvepence per ear mile. I would suggest that lOdd is not an excessive sum to state for Warrington.</p>
        <p>. . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr GOUGH : But 'Warrington has not a moving popu- lation like some towns ?</p>
        <p>Mr PREECE; We will move them when we get electric trams. (Laughter). Mr GOUGII : Is it not a fact that Liverpool cannot make their trams pay? Mr PREECE : I don't think that is true.</p>
        <p>Mr Gonna I sAw an article in the 'Dispatch" (Oh, oh and laughter)—and it said distinctly that the Liver— pool trains were not paying. Are you aware that Bury lost £6OO last year on its trains? Mr PREECE : I should not be at all surprised; Mr GOUGH : Have you any knowledge as to whether the present 'wises are paying? Mr PREECE : I should not think so.</p>
        <p>Mr GOUGH : Could you give us any estimate as to the financial value of the 'bus service? Mr PnExcx : I could not. I think the horses take funerals on Sunday. (Laughter). Mr Gonna: Would you be surprised to hear that the owners would sell tile 'bus service for £5,000 ? Mr PREECE : lam not an expert on tte value of 'bus services.</p>
        <p>ailr Gonciu : But you suggest the expenditure of £66,000 on electric trains ?</p>
        <p>The INSPECTON ; I would suggest that you put defi- nite questioos to the witness Mr GOUGH : Have you any idea what the inspection of these lines will cost ? _ _</p>
        <p>Mr PREECE : From £2,000 to £2,500 per year. Mr GOUGH : What amount do you suggest the cars will have to turn over in order to pay their way? Mr PREECE : From £7,000 to £B,OOO a year. Mr Gonna : What will be the cost of electric power? Mr PREECE : About a thousand a year. I think you told me the electricity department would not pay at the last inquiry at which I gave evidence. Mr GOUGH : Would your firm lilcp to spend £60,000 on tramways in Warringtsn ? Mr PREECE : Yes, with pleasure. By the Tows CLERK : Liverpool did not construct Its own tramways? Mr PREECE : No, they had to buy out ths old tram- ways ~company.</p>
        <p>„ .37 Mt PEMBERTON : These cars will sometimes run empty and then von will not ger 100 a mile? Mr PREECE : You can earn 40 pence per mile when they run full. MR PEMBERTON CROSS-EXAMINES• Mr PEMBERTON : Have you taken into consideration that the charges for 'buses in Warrington are for a certain distance and not per mile ? Mr PREECE : It has been taken into consideration. lln any Lancashire town you will find that the trams earn more than lop a mile. Mr PEMBER,TON Warrington is not the same as other towns. It is a self-contained town and there is no comparison between the towns you name. The INSPECTOR : Restrict your question. Mr ItonseaTort : Don't you think it will be danger- ous to run a tram over Warrington Bridge? The INSPECTOR: That it a question you cannot go into. TIN Corporation have Parliamentary power to run over that bridge. OTHER INQUIRERS.</p>
        <p>_ Councillor SMETIICRST asked whether it would not be possible to alter the existing retort houses at the old gas works so as to prevent the building of new car sheds? Mr PREECE : It would cost more to alter them than to erect the sheds proposed. By the Tows CLERK : The existing buildings have been used as far as practicable? Mr PREECE : They have. By Mr JAMEs CLIFFS : Don't you thick it will be a gross injustice to those ratepayers who will be deprived of the use of the trams,to be called upon to pay for those proposed ?</p>
        <p>Mr PREECE : I don't think that is a; matter upon which I can express an opinion. MR LONGDIN'S EVIDENCE. Mr Tnomes Lonannv,borough surveyor, gave evidence as to the cost of the wideniags proposed to be made in the roadway. He estimated the total cost at £2.200. With reference te the removal of setts he said it would be a mistake to put a four inch cube against a six inch rail. With regard to Winmarleigh-street the Corpora- tion had agreed with the Board of Trade to pave it with wood on account of the County Court being there. Mr LANGDOW cross-examined the witness at consider- able length with reference t 3 the revenue likely to be derived from the trams. Mr Longdin replied that the committee must be satisfied on that point or they would not have made the application. Mr LANODON : Is there anyone here who will under- take to say' I have gone into the question and believe the trams will pay."</p>
        <p>. - The TOWN CLERK : Yes I am prepared b say it. By Mr Gocau : Can you give us any idea of the number of people who travel on the Stockton Heath route daily?</p>
        <p>The Bonouent SURVEYOR : For the seven days from June 20th to June 27ch, 1900, 12,325 rrople travelled on the Wilderspool-road in 'buses and 11,790 on the Latchford route. Mr Gonon : How did you arrive at that? The BOROUGH SURVEYOR: I had men detailed off to count every passenger. The results were given before the House of Commons Committee. By kfr,Pcmscirron : Is it not a fact that the Stockton Heath Parish Council endeavoared to come to terms with reference to the running of trams in Stockton Heath?</p>
        <p>The BOROUGEI SURVEYOR: The terms asked for by the Stockton Heath authority were of such a nature that the Corporation could not agree to them.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN MONK,' THINKS THE TRIMS WILL PLY.</p>
        <p>Alderman MONKS said he had gone into the figures obtained by Mr Longdin. On the two routes mentioned there were 11 million passengers yearly. In order to make the trams pay Mr Pr?ece estimated that the revenue would have to be 1,864,000 pennies. The number of passengers on the Sankey Bridges route had not been taken into consideration by Mr Longdin. It was not unreasonable to suppose that the increase of electric tram passengers would be more than 50 per cent because the present 'bus service was exceedingly bad; it was very irregular and by no means comfortable. There were plenty of people who walked now who would ride it the tramways were in operation. A very large outside Company had made an offer to the Cor- poration to lease the tramways and to guarantee the interest and sinking fund. The company also offered to give a share of the profits after the expiration of a certain period. That communication had satisfied the committee that it was desirable to go on with the tram- ways with a fair prospect of making a profit, In answer to Mr Langdon he said he did not think it would be wiser to start with a lease to a company. , Personally, be was prepared to support the Carporation in taking the risk of running the tramways themselves. , He believed they would make a profit. Mr LANG:DOM : The electric cars will have to compete with the 'buses ? Alderman Moms: No ; the Corporation can prevent the 'buses running. They grant the licences. Mr LANODON : Will they withhold the licences ? Alderman MONIS : They have the power to do so. It is not likely that anyone will ride in a 'bus when be can ride in an electric ear. Mr LANODON : Even if the cars do not run as far as the 'buses, as in tbe case of Stockton Heath Alderman Moxits : I should say they would prefer the cars.</p>
        <p>By Mr Gomm : Don't you think it is a speculation cn the part of the Corporation ? Alderman MONKS : It must of necessity be a speculation. Mr Guyon : You have everylreason to think it will pay? Alderman MONKS: Yes.</p>
        <p>THE BOROUGH TREASURER AND TEIE INDEBTED- NESS OF THE TOWN. Mr JOHN FAIRHURST• borough treasurer, stated that the amount borrowed by the Corporation wail £948,000: the amount repaid £165.540 • the amount outstanding against the Corportion 082,914. The rateable value of the Borough was £238,853., and the population was 64,241. It showed an increase of 10,000 during the last ten years:</p>
        <p>By the IfisPEcTon : Do you think there is a likelihood of the rates increasing ? The Borcoron TREASURER: I don't think so. The INSPECTOR: Then there is no immediate propect of an increase of rates ? The BoRrIIGH TREASURER : No, sir ("Oh, Oh.") By Mr LANGDON The amounl. owing to the bank on the 31st of March was £133,000, was it not ? The BOROUGH TREASURER: It was something like that, but it was owing to the bank temporarily whilst we were negotiating loans. We have got permission to borrow nearly the whole of that amount on loans, and Isince the end of March I have reduced the amount out- standing at the bank to £40,000 by taking up loans. By the INSPECTOR: Then it was only owing to the bank whilst you were negotiating for loans ? The BOROUGH TREASURER : That is so. We have received the sanction of the Local Government Board to borrow for the purposes for which this overdraft was created at the bank, and it has since been reduced by the taking up of loans. In answer to further questions, the BOROUGH TREASURER pointed out that nearly the whole of the overdraft would be taken up in loans within a few days. Ile had received an advantageous offer that morning. He also wished to point out that he had been able to save money for the Corporation by allowing the over- draft to accumulate at the bank, for it bad enabled him to wait until the bank rate of interest had decreased. By the TOWN CLERK : The difference in the price of coal last year cost the Gas Department £7,000 or £B,OOO and that prevented the Gas Committee from contribut- ing something to the rates ? TEIE OPPONENTS OF TI3E SCEIEME. -</p>
        <p>The INSPECTOR asked if any ratepayer bad anything to say. - - -</p>
        <p>MiCLIARLE3 LONGMAw said the Corporation was nearly overhead in deot and they would get drowned directly. (Laughter.) The INSPECTOR You thiak the Corporation is t3o much in debt?</p>
        <p>LososkAw : I do. I don't think the trams will pay. I am told on good authority that the present 'bus service does not clear £3,000. It appeared that the</p>
      </div>
