      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>CASE OF P.C. HURST. Home Secretary to be Petitioned.</head>
        <p>THE CASE OF P.C. HURST. Home Secretary to be Petitioned.</p>
        <p>Active steps are now being taken to obtain,if possible, &amp;mitigation of the sentence of six months' imprison- ment recently passed upon P.C. Hurst of the Warring- ton County Borough Police Force for theft. At tne re- quest of an influential local gentleman, Mr WH J Jenkins, solicitor, has drawn up a petition to the Home Secretary, setting forth the principal features of the case and asking for a reduction of the punish- ceent inflicted. The petition reads as fellows To the Right Honourable C T Ritchie, Secretary of State for the Horne Department. The humble petition of the undersigned Inhabitants of the county borough of Warrington, in the county of Lsocaster, sheweth—That Police Constable John William Hurst, eithe Warrington County Borough Police Force, on the 6th idea kW, was convicted of feleny at the Warrington Borough traiice g9gp ond §egtQueed to two several terms of three I .. • calendar months' itnprisosartiehi, warn Gard laboil,gtiaii sentences to run consecutively. We refrain from criti- cizing the decision of the magistrates, believing that those gentlemen were acuated by a high sense of duty and a desire to zealously protect the public interests. We would, however, respectfully bring to your notice the following facts :—The convicted man, when about 18 years of age, eulisted in the 3rd Grenadier Guards and served in the regiment with credit for a period of 10 years. In the latter part of the year 1898 he was trans- ferred to the Army Reserve, leaving the regiment with • excellent and exceptionally good testimonials from his commanding officer. In January, 1899, he joined the Warringtneßorough Police Force. From that time to October,lB99,his conduct was good,his record of service a creditable one, and he merited the approval of his chiefs. In October, 1899, as a reservist,he was called up for service with the field forces in South Africa, and joined his regiment, his position in the Borough Police Force being kept open for him. He acquitted himself well with the field forces, was wounded,. and invalided home. On resuming his duties with the Borough Police Vorce, after an absence of ten months in South Africa, it was observed that his manner at times was strange and unaccountable, and his behaviour erratic. Prior to his service in South Africa, he had been a life-long abstainer from intoxicating liquors' but it is feared that having been induced to drink that in some measure accounts for his altered conduct. We would respect- fully ask you to take into consideration the strange con- duct of Hurst in connection with the commission of the offences with which he was charged. We would also ask you to take into your favourable consideration—his Previous good conduct and service of ten years with the 3rd Grenadier Guards before joining the Police Force; his service and good conduct in the Police Force before going out to South Africa ; his service with the field forces in South Africa ; that he had a wife and one child entirely dependent upon him, and that his wife is near her coefinement ; that his position and characterdare lost, and that he must enter on a new career hampered with the damaging fact of his conviction. We would ask you to take the &amp;foregoing facts into your favourable consideration ,and mitigate the punishment inflicted by the magistrates, having regard to those circumstances, which we humbly submit should weigh in favour of our petition on behalf of the convicted man. And your Petitioners will ever pray."</p>
        <p>A LAMB WORRIED AT RISTM—DAMAGES AWARDED. On Wednesday,at the Warrington County Petty Sessions before Colonel J C Ridgway and Mr F L Frost,Samnel Boond, of Lymm, was summoned for being the owner of two dogs which committed damage to a lamb, the property of James Jodrell Brook, farmer, Rizton, for which the complainant claimed El lOs damages,—Mr Steel represented the complainant. and stated Mr Brook was the tenant of Mersey View Farm, Rixton. He owned a field known as Rixton Ley which was situated between the Manchester Ship Canal andlthe river Mersey. It was surrounded on three sides by water, and on the other side was a foot- path. On the day in question Mr Brook was watching as ha had had a number of sheep worried of late, and be saw the defendant come along a footpath, which led nowhere, with two greyhounds. The dogs ran at the sheep and caught a lamb, and commenced to worry it. Both Mr Brook and the defendant ran after the dogs, and the defendant pulled them off. One dog, however got away,and ran the lamb down the river embankment into the river, where it was drowned. During the last three months Mr Brook had had twelve sheep worried.— Defendant denied that the lamb had been drowned, and said he had done his best to save it from the dogs. The bench decided to fine defendant in damages, costs, and advocate's fee.</p>
