      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>Former Warrington Bank Clerk Killed.</head>
        <p>Former Warrington Bank Clerk Killed.</p>
        <p>News has been received that Trooper John Bainbridge Wood, who formerly lived in Warrington, was killed in the recent engagement at Warmbaths. The deceased was a son of the late Rev W Wood, of Thorp near Newark (formerly vicar of St. Paul's Warrington], and of Mrs Wood, now of Farndon, and (after beginning big career at Parr's Bank Warrington] was for some years in the baLk of Messrs Samuel Smith and Co., at Newark. Being offered an excellent a. Dointment in a bank at Buluwayo, he proceeded to Sol. 4 Africa, and when the war broke out and the colony endangered be wae one of the first heroic band of 75 e ,lonial volun- teers who bravely offered to take the field against the foe. He took part in several important actions, and thel last communication received from him was about two I months ago, when they were trekking through Willow. more, and he then described himself as " wonderfully fit and well." As an athlete the deceased gained a high reputation during his residence at Newark. It was as an amateur cyclist that he scored his principal achieve- ments, his first novice race being ga;ned at Newark, where he was eeveral times successful. He won trophies at Wakefield and Retford, and in Jubilee year with Preece and his brother, Mr A F Wood, in the team race, carried off the Notts Challenge Shield at the Castle Sports. He was second at Leicester, where he was only beaten by a few inches for the cup ; but perhaps his best afternoon was at Beeston, where he won both the open races. Mr Wood was only 25 years of age. His cousin, Captain Cecil Wood, has recently returned from South Africa, after taking part with the West Yorkshire Regiment in all Geoeral Buller'a campaigns and the Tugela fights. He has just received the freedom of the City of York.</p>
        <p>Lieutenant Reynolds Returns Home.</p>
        <p>The members of the 21st and 22nd companies of the Imperial Yeomanry returned to Chester on Monday, when they received an enthusiastic welcome. Amongst the offia,rs appears the name of Lieutenant Reynolds, whose mother, the late Mrs Sylvanus Reynolds. at one time lived at Raddon-court, Latchford. Lieutenant Reynolds proceeded to Northwich with the Northwich members, who were cordially received. During a halt in the procession which was formed at Northwich. Lieutenant Reynolds on behalf of Captain Daniels arid the men of the 22nd, returned thanks for the very hearty reception given to them. A capital dinner was , given to the companies where a presentation took place. On behalf of the 22nd Company,Colour-Sergeant !dale presented a silver bowl to Captain Daniels, and a silver inkstand to Lieutenant Reynolds. Needless to ' say, this simple act of thoughtfulness was much ap- preciated by the two officers.</p>
        <p>Return of the Cheshire Yeomanry.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>Mr. Bankes' Reception at Runcorn.</head>
        <p>Mr. Bankes' Reception at Runcorn.</p>
        <p>Monday saw the return to Chester of the 21st and 22nd Companies of the Imp.rial Yeomanry from the seat of war. At Chester station the men were welcomed and cheered by but a small crowd ; but this was made up for a little later in a demonstration at the Town Hall Square, where the Mal or extended to them a hearty welcome on behalf of the citizens of Chester and the country. A banquet followed in the Assembly Rooms of the Town Hall, at which, on behalf of the 22nd Company, Colour-Sergeant Male presented a silver bowl to Captain Daniels, and a silver inkstand to Lieutenant Reynolds. At Northwich,linutsfori, Altrincham, and throughout the county the spirit of rejoicing ran high ; and where. ever a " yeoman " tailed from, there was to be found every manifestation of neighbourly good will, and those expressions of thankfulness which make a " heart', welcome home" thoroughly understood and appreci— ated. . . .</p>
        <p>Runcorn, or rather Weston contributed but one to the ranks of the Yeomanry, viz., Mr Stanley Bankes, sou of Mr Bankes, of Weston, and when he returned to Run- corn Station on Monday evening last, he found every— one from Weston and Weston Point there to meet him. The Weston Chapel and Weston Salt Union Bands had also turned out to a man. Mr Bankes was literally taken prisoner, fixed in a conveyance, and a procession being formed (headed by the bands) he was escorted triumphantly through the town of Runcorn, right to his native hearth. Mr Banked is a member of the Runcorn Boating Club, and a movement is on foot amongst the members to recognise his services.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>SUNDAY SCHOOL TanaT.---The scholars attending Hill Cliffe Sunday school had their annual treat on Monday. The children were conveyed in lurries to Winstanley Farm, Lyinm, starting from Hill Cliffe about 11 am,, and enjoyed a pleasant drive to this charming village. A number of friends, as well as the pastor sad teachers, also went in a wagonnette, making a company of about 150. On reaching the ground, buns and milk were die- tributed to all and games Were et once started. At four o'clock a capital eandwich tea was provided for all in the tea room, and an adjournment was again made to the field, where the cbildren engaged in sports and races, for which a large number of useful prizes were given. Just before returning, the children were again provided with refreshments. At a brief meeting, held by the house, two hymns were sung, and three cheersi given for Mr and Mrs Winstan- ley, the donors of the provisioas,and all those who had lent horses and lorries. The journey home was cora- - mei:lced at about 830. . Warrington Postman's Interesting BAPTIST CHAPEL.—The summer anniversary services 1 in connection with the ale ,ve place of worship were held 1 Description. on Sunday, in weather which was more suggestive of Driver H Roscoe, of the Bth Company of Royal Ea- the middle of March, than the middle of June, and, gineers, who, prior Co leaving for South Al rice. was em- doubtless, the cold and doubtful character of the day, played at the Warrington Post Office, has militated to some extent against a larger attendance. written to his parents at Warrington stating As it was the services were all well attended and proved that he has arrived at Avoca. The journey very successful. In the morning, there was a children's from Vereenigniag„ he says, was a pleasant one, the flower service, the first of the kind ever held in Hill country passed through for the last hundred miles of Cliffe Chapel. Toe pastor conducted, and the the journey being much more pleasant to the e