        <p>In the course of an interesting letter dated 25:h May, 1901, received this week by Mr Thomas S Steel, a friend who is "engaged in active service" in South Africa, and is at present stationed at Norvals Pont says:—"l saw the last Aria of the Warrington Volunteers who came out, and I knew several of them. They did not look amiss, although several of them were wishing they were at home again. I wonder how they will feel in another twelve months from now. They have come out at a very bad time, and as the weather is bitterly cold at night, and very warm during they day, so they get the two extrema, and it is this that kills a lot of men. . I . . . I had a bit of a treat last Monday in the shape of a new shirt and a couple of pairs of socks, which our generous Government have, at last, thought fit to give us, and I leave you to guess how acceptable they were when I tell you that they are the first I have bad since left 13003 (18 months ago),</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Treatment of a Padgate Boy.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BOARD OF GUARDIANS. The Treatment of a Padgate Boy.</p>
        <p>meeting of this Board was held at the Workhouse, WarrinL,t )o, on Wednesday morning. Mr A J Jolley nreeded, and there were also present :—Mrs Clay, Revs T R Matthews, J H Riddette, Or Patterson, Messrs S Davies, T H Ashcroft, J Payton, T Halton, William Houghton, A Brooks, W Mather 3 Clarke, G Pilling, A Forshaw, A Jolley, with J C Sutton kclerk), S Sutton (assistant clerk). C a Ward (master), and A. Wright (superintendent Padgate Schools).</p>
        <p>. . GIFT OF BOOKS TO TEIE SCHOOL. The Superintendent of Padgate schools reported the receipt of a present of 50 volumes from the Warrington Library. _ _</p>
        <p>_WALKING DAY.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the Padgete Scho',ls Committee it was reconam-ndeci that the children be allowed to take part in the Warrington Walking Day, that their rail way fares to Warrington be paid out of the Superin tendent's petty cash and that the usual treat and extra be allowed.</p>
        <p>raE ALLEGED CRUEL TREkTMENT OF A PAD.</p>
        <p>GATE BOY.</p>
        <p>The Local Government Board wrote that their atten- tion had been called to a newspaper paragraph contain in an account nf the alleged cruel treatment of a boy named Joseph William Bowen at the Padgate Schools, and they requested that the Board may be informed of any further proceedings that may be taken in the matter</p>
        <p>At the meeting of the Padgate Schools Committee th above letter was submitted. After hearing the Super intendent, the Medical Officers who had examined th boy Bowen and Mrs McKibbin ; the committee wee satisfied that the allegations of cruelty are absolute'- , baseless and that a proper punishment as dul- recorded in the punishment book, was inflicted on the blv Bowen by the Superintendent. They were also satisfied that Mrs McKibbin did not chastise the boy. The Cilaramem, in moving the confirmation of the minutes, said the Committee was quite satisfied That the boy had not been improperly punished. It was within the knowledge of the Guardians that Dr Braide and Dr Peacocke examined the boy within 24 hours of the infliction of the punishment, and they were satisfied that nothing baynnd proper castigation had been inflic- ted, The Committee particularly emphasised one point and desired the Superintendent to acquaint his assist- ants with the fact that any attempt on the part of the subnrdinatt sto castigate the children would be a con- travention of the regulations. The Committee had no reason to believe that any contravention of the regula- tions had been made. The Rev JFI RIDDErTE • What was the nature of the punishment ? Th- CHAD:MAN . Four strokes of the cane. Mr FoastrAve : I suppose a report is made of such punishment.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : It le entered Into a special book which is laid before the committee. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr lisaceovr seconded the adoption of the minutes which were agreed to. HOUSE COMMITTEE. The Committee reported that Miss Sullivan, the general assistant and sewing mistress, had resigned,and it was recommended that Probationer 134iley be appointed in her place, and on the same terms. The following holidays were allowed :—T Brobbia, engine tenter, ono week, he finding a substitute ; superintend- oat nurse, 14 days ; nurses, 12 days each ; probationers 10 days each ; other officials of the House, as in former years.</p>
        <p>The Rev J RIDDETTE said he was sorry to see that the petition of the nurses for a longer holiday had been declined by the Rouse Committee. Considering the arduous duties of the nurses he did not think it would have b.en an excessive allowance, and was worthy of more generous consideration.</p>
        <p>The CHAIRMAN : The mater was thoroughly gone into. Mrs CLAY pointed out that the nurses were allowed a day's holiday per month rn addition to the holiday now granted. The minutes were passed. (HOTS TO THE INMATES.</p>
        <p>The MASTER reported that he had received the follow- ing gifts .—A. quantity of flowers from Mrs Crosfleld, Walton Lea ; papers from the chaplain (Rev E R Tar- buck) ; magazines from Mr W J Wilson ; parcel of papers from Mr Perris. Sankey-street ; flowers from Miss Owen on her wedding day ; plants for hospital, Mrs and Miss Crosfleld. The ladies of the Brabazon Employment scheme gave a very nice tea to the patients in hospital, which was much enjoyed and appreciated. The Master also stated that he had received a parcel of "Graphics " from Mr John Clough, of Vine Tavern ; and a quantity of flowers from Rev T R Matthews, of Earlestown, and the Baptist Chapel, Hill Cliff e. Pet Rev J S Hughes. The CHAIR:W.7 (Mr A J Jolley) received a deputation from Ormskirk Union, and conducted them over the i new hospital, as they had been recommended to see the INurses Home here by Mr H Jeriner Fast. THE CONTRACT FOR OATMEAL. MR HALLWOOD WRITES TO THE LOCAL GOVERNMENT BOARD.</p>
        <p>At the last meeting of the Board, Mr Hallwood called attention to the fact that his orders for oatmeal had exceeded the quantity estimated in the printed tender of the Board, and that the price of meal was advancing. It watt! tea decillcd chat 4r Flailwood be kept to the ' teems of his Contract. Mr JAMES Iler.a.woon, Wilderspool Bakery, no wrote to the Clerk, stating that it was his desire to carry out his contract with satisfaction to the Guardians, and he acknowledged his liability to supply 33 loads of oat- meal, as specified. With all due deference to the Guardians, however, he was writing to the Local Government Board as CP what they considered an I " estimated quantity," as he considered a contractor ought to know very nearly the quantity required, so I that he might provide for it, as the corn markets fluctuated so much. He wished to mention that owing to the new dietary order,une Master had intimated to contractors in another Union that a very much larger quantity would be required.—The Local Government Board forwarded a copy of the communication received from Mr Hallwood, in which he asked the Board's Opinion as to what was a fair and equitable view to form as to" estimated quantity " and the 33 loads stated in the contract. Tne Guardians here had told him that he must supply up to about 120 loads, which, he contended, was not equitable. When he (Mr Hall- wood) tendered he anticipated that he would only be expected to supply from 33 to 401oade, and his purchase providing for that being exhausted, he bad now to go on the market and Pay fron 3s ad to 49 per load more, the markets in the meantime have advanced, thus causing him a very serious loss. He did not know that the Guardians required extra until after he had com- menced supplying when the Master informed him that awing to the new dietary order, he would want more. He thought contractors ought to have been made (painted with the fact that, owing to the dietary order, they woulisl aat So much more, but that had not been done. Rev J H RIDDETTE hoped the Board would take an equitable view of the matter. The Board did not make known te the contractor the quantity of meal required and he did not think they ought to act ultra vires in the matter. He moved that the contract be determined. Mr ASHCROFT seconded. The CLERK thought the contractor would be satisfied if the Board agreed to pay the market price. Mr Wituam HOIIMITON was of opinion that the other people who tendered ought to be given an equal chance. Mr S DAVIES said the .lo.wness of the price of meal in- fluenced the Board in giving Mr Hallwood the order for flour. Mr liallarnod had made a mistake in the price when tendering for oatmeal. The price of meal bad not risen a sh' Ing a sack since the time Mr Hallwood sent in his ti The CLERK: Nr. doubt he made a mistake. The proposition to determine the cpntract was carried. Mr PILLING then moved that fresh teuders be issued for the ensuinfr quarter' Mr DAvIES secluded and it was agreed to. THE NORTE! WESTERN PODR LAW CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>The CLERK stated that he had reeieved notices from Mr Bagger, secretary of the North Western Poor Law Conference. stating that a meeting would be held at the Town Hall. Manchester on July 9th. It was decided that the chairman, vice chairman and the clerk attend the Conference.</p>
        <p>THE POOR LAW AMENDMENT' BILL. MR. PIERPOINI"S OPINION.</p>
        <p>The CLERK stated that he had received a communica- tion from Colonel Pilklngtoo, M.P., stating that he would eiveconsideratiou o tho Poor Law Amendment Bill which the Board of Guardians had asked him to support. The CLERK also stated that be had received the foil, wing letter on the same subject :—" House of Com- mons, June 18. Dear Sir,—l was abroad when your letter of May 23rd, arrived at my address in London. Since my return after the Whitsuntide recess, I have made some inqui,t, about the position of the Local Government Act 1333 Amendment Bill. As far as I can see—it being by the action of the ballot—very low down in the list, has no chance of even being discussed this session. IN: know, of course, that it is a private mem- bers bill. The proposals of the Bill appear to be very reasonable, hut of course. I need not enter into that matter considering it a practical certainty of annihila- tion for this session.—Yours faithfully,Robert Pierpoint J C Sutton Esq." This was all the business.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>Success of a Local Baker.</head>
        <p>Success of a Local Baker.</p>
        <p>At the Liverpool Grocers' and Bakers' Exhihi- tion held in St George's Ilan, the first and only Prize for fancy bread was awarded to Mr Toft, 98, Bridge-street, Warrington. WALKING DAY Boots and Shoes in great variety, now 'hewing at ALP TAYLOR'S Cash Boot Store, Horse- inarket-strett.—(Advt.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON UAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>Cricket.</p>
        <p>Bad they scored three more runs the Warrington teamlwould have had the satisfaction of placing &amp;victory to their credit on Saturday: The Fates seem to be against them, however, and the remit of their visit to Cheetham was one more defeat. Batting first the home team comniled 123 runs, and the Warrington total had reached 121 when the last man was caught out. This result was indeed a disappointment to the Warring- tonians who had practically made sure of success.</p>
        <p>Warrington people are really becoming weary of de- feats week after week. So far as playing is concerned the cricket season has up to now been most unsatisfac- tory. Out of nine matches played, Warrington have secured only a single victory,whilst they hay sustained six defeats, and drawn two games. It is not much worider that poor" gates" are of tea experienced at the Arpley enclosure. Of course hard luck has accounted for some of the reverses, but surely the Warrington players can exhibit much better form than they have bem doieg of late.</p>
        <p>To return to Saturday's engagement, Cheetham batted first on a wicket which had been affected by the recent rain. Their innings was productive of 123, Cll Bolton being top scorer with 27. Gibson wan the most effective bowler, capturing five wickets for 36 runs.</p>
        <p>Thewlis and B Silcick commenced the Virarrington innings, Naylor and Allen sharing the bowling. Silcock started promisingly, placing Allen to the leg boundary twice on the first over. At 28 he lost the company of Mewl's, who was easily caught at mid off, after knock- ing up 5. J Gibbon came net and assisted to add 19 runs for the second wicket, when Parker, who had relieved Allen, at 34 bowled him with a fine ball.</p>
        <p>J. Callon filled the vacancy, and runs coming at a good rate Naylor again took up the hall in place of T Fl Sinclair. Si!cock continued to play with confidence,and tin total reached 70 b.fore Naylor accepted a return, Silcock having made 38 without a faulty stroke.</p>
        <p>J. Callon tried to force the game with fair result, but soon lost the company of J A Briggs, who failed to negotiate a hall from Parker. J. W. Bibby was caught at pol3t at 86, half the side being out.</p>
        <p>With the game in such an even state, the interest was maintained, but E A Connor looked like assisting Callon to place the issue beyond doubt until at 94. Callon was unfortunately run out, the batsmen playing a ball hard and stepping out of his crease.</p>
        <p>Just after the hundred had been hoisted Connor was caught at colter point, whilst at 112 Podmore was stumped. Ilampson vws dismissed at 118, so that seven runs were required and only another wicket to fall. Singles were registered, and the total taken to 121, when Gibson played a ball hard past point, but it being fielded the wicket was broken and Warrington thus lost by two 11.1129.</p>
        <p>The second eleven sustained a serious reverse at the hands of the Cheatham second string. Batting first Warrington werA all dismissed far 117. and the visitors had compiled 155 for six wickets, when play ceased. For Warrington E E Woods batted in splendid style, his innings realising 60. H Buckley was the mainstay of the Cheatham eleven, that player rattling up 74, when he retired hurt, having received a nasty knock in the face.</p>
        <p>Cycling. A nr.tv feature has recently been introduced at the Lon- don aquarium in the shape of the lady cycle races,teams representing England and France taking put in a con- test which concluded last Saturday. The struggle. however, early resolved itself into a soft thing for the English riders, two cr three of the French girls Buffering through spills. The riders are of all ages—from 17 upwards—some of the veterans it is stated being trained down to a shadow.</p>
        <p>r A reader of a daily paper, who has seen the lady- racing at the Aquarium, speaks of it as a " disgusting exhibition," and blarnei the pipers for printing details of such competicions. "If it were not for the press," he goes on, "thee wretched creatures (lady racers they call tla.rnselves) would not perform such feats. The remedy for these exhibitions of speed on the part of women cyclists is in your own hands. Decline to publish even one line of the doings of these 'gay misses' and you will speedily stamp out this lamentable phase of up-to-date cycling."</p>
        <p>The Wlnnington Recreation Club's track has been en- larged and greatly improved in shape, and on Saturday it was in first-class condition, Tickle, of St. Helens, who won the half mile and ran third in the scratch race showed wonderfully improved form, and when he has got rid of a little more weight he will make anue of the best move. An old favourite and champion, in Tommy Astill, of Warrington, turned out, " but," says a correspondent •• he is not the man he was."</p>
        <p>The best way to Blackpool, as far as South Lanca- shire riders are concerned, is to avoid Preston altogether From Chorley nuke for the Wigan-Preston highway, stick to it to the sixth iil °stone, then strike for Pen- worthatn Bridge,</p>
        <p>EarlV morning rides shaul d not exceed ten miles per hour, and half an hour at that pace is sufficient before breakfast if one's hcalth be considered.</p>
        <p>At the English Motor Clubs hill-climbing contest held at Tilburstowe Bill, Go dstone, on Saturday, a sensa- tion was caused by the ascent of Mr S F Edge's 70 h.P. Napier, the car which represented England in the Inter- national race from Paris to Bordeaux. The time occu flied by this vehicle was lmin. 7 4-53ecs., or nearly 20 secs. faster than that of any other car. Mrs M E Kennard, the novelist. was a successful competitor in the class for qUidricycles.</p>
        <p>On Saturday night, two cyclists—one coming from Kaaresborough to Harrogate, and the other from Harro- gate to lioaresborough—had a nasty spill when near the High Bridge, Boaresborough. There was a lady on the road, and in endeavouring to steer clear of her they collided with each other. One, John Wilk, had his collar-bone broken.</p>
        <p>The Rev W Carlife, of Load on, has issued a hearty invitation to cyclists to be present at his 50-minute ser- vice, at nine o'clock on Sunday mornings, at St Mary at-Hill Church, Monument. All the cycles are carefully numbered and taken charge of by the church officials until the elm of the service.</p>
        <p>An Ashton-under-Lyne solicitor,named George Alfred Briggs Pish, aged 34, met with a shocking death on Saturday afternoon. Deceased was cycling past a char-a-band and came into collision with a dog-cart that was being driven in an opposite direction, with the result that the shaft of the dog-cart passed com- pletely through deceased's body. At the inquest the driver of the dog-cart was among those who gave evi- dence. A verdict of " Accidental death" was returned, no blame being attached to anybody.</p>
        <p>W Henley went for the Lmdon to York cycling record which stood at 10ers. 16az1n., on Wednesday. He started from Harriss Corner, London, at one o'clock in the morning, when the roads were free from traffic. and covered the 197 k miles in one minute under 10 hours. He thus succeeded in beating the record by 17 minutes.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>Football.</head>
        <p>Football.</p>
        <p>Leigh Rugby Football Club members and friendi have presented the veteran forward Tom Sc.ith with a hand- some gold watch and medal. in recognition of his fifteen years' service to toe club. Smith, who is now retirine from active participation in the game, in his time filled alm;,st every position in the field'. He on many occa- sions represented Lancashire as a forward under both Rugby and Northern Union rules.</p>
        <p>Angling. The Warrington Workingmen's Angling Association commenced their series of matches last week end at Vale Royal. There was a good turnout of members for the opening match, Se competing. But sport was very poor, only about half succeeded in landing Ash. A Shaw was the gold medal winner with 11b. 4ozs. 12drms., just bea,'ing T Birchall, who succeeded in land- ing 111). 3 Z 3. 15drms. ; the others being a long way behind. The nearest wss T Robinson with 12ozs. iirms. Altogether it was a very enjoyable out, most of the competitors and friends b,ing conveyed to the river by 'buses, St 3. The Society received 16 new members, which brings the total membership up to 1.65.</p>
        <p>A. J. Wail's:, LTD. (SEIGEL'S SYSLIP).-111 their fourth Annual R.oort, just issued, the directors of A J White. Limited (Mather Seigere Syrup), indicates an improve. meet in the business, its volume of trade and profits, which must h. gratifying to its shareholder. Net profits of £68,833 4s 81 are reported for the year ending Starch 31st, 1901, out of which the unexpended adver- tising account of £ll,soo—the remainder of a legacy from the late Board—has been entirely written off. Pour quarterly dividends on the six per cent. Prefer- ence shares have been paid during the year, and a dividend of 50/) is recommended on the ordin”v shares The nroiits for the year ending March 31st, 1900, were £44,833 16i. Tae shareholders of A J White, Limited, are to be congratulated that after years of such manage- ment as resulted in a censiderable depreciation in value of the shares there seems every prospect now that these securities will recover thsir intrinsic value. A gain mf fifty per cent, in protiti in a year when public and trade Conditions were adverse, is a performance signifying a competent and an able management. WHITHICANVAS. Hair Calf and Buck Skin Shoes for Ladies' and Children at all prices at ALF TAYLOR'S Cash Soot Store.—(idvt.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <head>LATEST NEWS.</head>
        <p>LATEST NEWS.</p>
        <p>Hamburg-American Liner</p>
        <p>Ashore.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>Feared Total Loss.</head>
        <p>Feared Total Loss.</p>
        <p>A Lloyd's telegram from Aden, of Thursday, states that the German steamer Asturia, belonging to the Hamburg-American line, is ashore at Ras- Jaid-Hafun, and is expected to become a total wreck. The crew has been landed at Aden by the British Steamer Clan Maclachlan from Akyab. Yesterday's Cricket. Close of Play Scores. Rain interfered with most of the first-class cricket matches played yesterday. LANCS v ESSEX :—Lancs. 93 for 3. YORKSHIRE v. NO r 73.—Yorkshire all out, 204. Notts, 1 for 1. KENT v. SUSSEX ;—Kent, 233; Sussex, 19 for 0. WARWICKSHIRE V. DERBYSHIRE . Warwick- shire, 213 for 3. SURREY v. CAMBRIDGE :—Surrey, 183; Cam- bridge, 95 for 5. M.C.C. V WORCESTERSHIRE :—Worcester, 77; M.C.C., 92 for 7.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>WAR.</head>
        <p>THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The Bravery of the British Troops. Tribute by Lord Gerard.</p>
        <p>At a banquet at Astaton-in-Makerfield in honour of the Lancashire Hussars on Wednesday night Lord Gerard, who was presented with a gold medal in commemora- tion of his services in South Africa, said he was sure if there were any persons in this world who deserved well of thEir country it was these brave men who had been fighting in South Africa. If they had seen them, as he had, going up to the Boer lines and trenches with shots dropping like hail they would think with him that there was no honour and glory which could be given them they did not deserve.</p>
        <p>Warrington Volunteers at Frodsham.</p>
        <p>A party of Volunteers from Warrington, attached to the South Lancashire Regiment, and but recently returned from South Africa, paid a visit so Overton Hills, Frodsbaca on Monday. Arriving by bus about 12.30. a dinner which was admirably provided by Miss Hayes, of the " Rine o' bells " Hotel, was partaken of and subsequently the party which numbered about 40 spent an enjoyable time on the hills,and in visiting the places of interest in the neigh- bourhood. Sergeant Hunt, who was in charge, when the company had re-assembled for tea, expressed the gratification all felt at again being at borne.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>Illness of Trooper A. Ashton.</head>
        <p>Illness of Trooper A. Ashton.</p>
        <p>The many friends of Trooper A Ashton, of the 22nd Company of tile Imperial Yeomanry, whose brother,:dr H Ashton, resides at the Bridge Inn, Warrington, will regret to bear that he has been left at Wynberg, suffer- ing from a dangerous attack of enteric fever. A Grappenhall trooper (Davenport) was also left in hos- pital in South Africa, when theNeomanry returned.</p>
        <p>St. Helens Volunteers at the Front.</p>
        <p>S rgeant W A Howard, of the Volunteer service Com- pany of the 2nd Lancashire Royal Engineers Volunteers St. Helens, writes home stating that the section have been seLt to Kimberley,which will be their headquarters for some time. Assisted by a number of blacks, the Engineers are erecting forts and intricate wire entangle- ments at the places which have been prepared for the defence of the district. At the time of writing the sec- tion had received orders to go to Frankfort,about fifteen miles from Kimberley, to erect a permanent fort on the highest of a group of kopjes. The main road runs through the group, and the Boers have at various times done a great deal of sniping here. The St. Helens men set out along with 150 South Wales Borderers, some artillery, and three guns. Sergeant Howard states that he had a most interesting visit to the De Beers diamond mines, and in the manager's office held in his hands for a few minutes diamonds worth £50,000, so that if he does not make his fortune before returning home he will at least have seen one.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1901-06-21_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>News of the Third Contingent of Warrington Volunteers.</head>
        <p>News of the Third Contingent of Warrington Volunteers.</p>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. ,</p>
        <p>The fortnig,htly meeting of the Council was held at Dutton Wockhouse on Tuesday afternoon. Present : Mr J Knowles (chairman), Mr S Woodward (vice-chair- man),' Admiral Jones-Parry, Messrs J Stokes, T Darlington, E Turner, A Eaton, R Billingron, T Darling- ton, T Baxter, C W Davenport, G Pilling, T Antwiss, W H Bradford, T Lewis, J Spruce, P Gleave, E A Walling- ton, C E Linaker, J B Chadwick, T Wright, James Whitlow, J Bllshaw, E Brethertoo, J Baguley, George F Ashton (elerk),W E Hough (assistant clerk),Dr Adams (medical officer), W Diggle and J Swinton (surveyors) and James Farrington (inspector).</p>
        <p>THE INFECTIOUS DISEASES 110.PITAL. " • DECISION AT LAST ! "</p>
        <p>The Clerk said on looking at the map the site recom- mended for an infectious hospital did not seem to him to be quite eight acres. He wrote to Mr Linaker on the subject and asked him if he would again survey it and say what it really was. He received a reply to the effect that he would look into the matter. . . . .</p>
        <p>Mr Linager said he had not been able to ascertain the exact position of the boundary. The area on the plan as it stood was 711 acres, 'nit when everything was measured up it would be found to be not far abort of eight acres. _ . . _ .</p>
        <p>Mr Antwiss eMd he would like to ask whether they had any estimate as to the probable cost ot the scheme, including the land and buildings, and whether the land would require fencing round. He would also like to kaQw what was the estimated cost of working the hospital!</p>
        <p>The Clerk said the administrative block would be close to the main road. Ha was afraid he could not give all those particulars. The County Medical Officer met their medical officer at his the clerk's) office, and as far as the hospital accommodation was concerned, Dr Vacher stated that the County Council would be satisfied—aod he understood the Local Government Board would be satisfied also—if provision was made for twelve beds, but the whole of the administrative block must be built at once. A hospital with 24 beds was the estimated accommodation to meet the require- ments of their district, but as a start the County G..uncil would be satisfied if 12 beds only were pro- vided in the first instance, on the understanding that the administrative block for a larger hospital was built at once. The hospital proper could be extended As the requirements of the district demanded it. Mr Antwiss proposed that the whole matter be de- ferred until they got an approximate estimate of the cost of the whole scheme. Let the ratepayers see what the Local Government Board required, and then they could not bkme the Council for being so extravagant, They were sure to be blamed for it.</p>
        <p>Mr Stokes asked if any of the adjoining Unions had put.up a similar hospital.</p>
        <p>Thy Clink said be Was not in a position to give that information.</p>
        <p>Mr Woodward said the discussion was out of order. He moved the following resolution of which he gave notice at the last meeting : " That subject to the sanction of the Local Government Board, being obtained to a loan for the purpose, and to the Council being able to make satisfactory arrange- ments for a water supply from the Vyrnwy mains, and .of the sewering of the property, this Council enter into a provisional agreement with A H Talbot Esq., of Aston Hall, for the purchsae of a piece of land in the parish of Dutton, numbered 255, and of a portion of a piece of land in the Parish of Aston by Suttonoand numbered 183 on the 25 inch ordnance map of the said Parishes (1888 edition), and containing in the whole 8 acres or there- abouts for lie sum of £1,050, for the purpose of an Isolation Hospital for the Council's district." This had been carefully considered by a special committee appointed for the purpose. There was no doubt that they were face to face with the absolute necessity of providing a hospital for their district. They might try to evade• the question but they could not—that was his firm conviction. He had been a member of the Hospital Committee for at least half a dozen years, and the question had been shelved up to the present time. But as most of the members knew the county authorities had the matter in hand now and they meant business. They might as well take the question fairly and squarely in hand. They need not proceed with great rapidity, but they had a site which was generally believed to be a very good one, and although they were paying what he considered to be a stiffish price for it he did not think they could meet with a more advantageous site or a cheaper one. They might expect that Mr Linaker would act liberally in the matter and give them a fair frontage. He believed every member was satisfied that it was a fair site. It was within easy access of the workhouse, and was in the centre of their district.</p>
        <p>Mr T Wright seconded the resolution. There was one little matter that Mr Woodward had omitted to men- tion—viz., that they would be entitled to the timber on the site. The land was to comprise eight acres, but it was understood that if it was less the coat should be proportionately less, and if it exceeded the area named they would have to pay more. Mr Linaker : No. No. . .</p>
        <p>Mr Wright, continuing, said the question bad been held over for a long time, and there bad always been the obstacle of finding an eligible site. Now they had the offer of a very suitable site and one that had been approved by the County Medical Officer. Mr Davenport thought it was quite time this matter was settled. He did not think it ought to be a question of what the hospital was going to cost, seeing that they had got what be considered to be a most favourable Site. He did not consider it a high price at all. He thought it was most reasonable when he considered it was close to the main road. They ought not to let this opportunity alio. In his opinion the cost of the whole scheme would be much short of £6,000. Mr Linaker said they might gei, land for £2 an acre, but they would have to incur a heavy expense in fencing it. The site under notice was thought to be the best they could get, The owners were perfectly in- different as to whether the Council purchased the land or not:</p>
        <p>Pilling thought they ought to be told by Mr Linaker as to the position they were in with regard to their sewage. He ought to guarantee that the sewage would be treated in a proper way. Mr Linaker said it they produced a satisfactory Oluent he felt that there would be no difficult in regard to tbe sewage. He was not going to give any guarantee. Mr Spruce said he could not support the resolution because he did not see how the question of water supply was to be dealt with. The whole scheme seemed to him to be a very big one, &amp;IA altogether too costly for the district. He thought it was possible Stockton Heath might obtain urban powers in the future and in that event what guarantee had they that they would contri- bute their share towards this hospital. Their district was sparsely populated, and it appeared that the present scheme was much beyond their means. He moved that the matter be deferred until definite information was obtained as to the water supply. . .</p>
        <p>Mr Antwiss seconded. , Mr Wallington asked how long they would keep the matter open. Mr Mclntyre said before the voting was taken he would like to ask whether they anticipated any great difficulty with respect to the water supply ? Mr Stokes : None at all. Mr Mclntyre : Nor the sewage ? Mr Stokes : Nor the sewage. Mr Woodward did not doubt for one moment that Mr Linaker would be able en satisfy them as to the dis- posal of the purified diluent. It seemed to be a medical officer's question. If Dr Adams could assure them that they would be able, at reasonable cost, to treat the sewage from the buildings—that they could turn out an effluent that would be satisfactory to the County Council—he thought the question was practically settled. Mr Linaker said they would have a right of drainage The field drained into a watercourse. Dr adams said he could give the Council assurance that the drainage could be accomplished at a very moderate cost, and that such an effluent could be pro- duced as would be received into the watercourse. Mr Woodward said it seemed to be a lolly to postpone the decision as to this site. The County Council re- quired them to build a hospital to meet present needs and as those needs grew they would have to extend the accommodation. If they deferred the matter it was an open question whether they would be able to find a more eligible site. Mr Antwiss said he had no objection to their buying the land if they could get more information as to what the scheme was likely to cost. Mr Linaker said apart from the Liverpool water supply they could avail themselves of the supply at the Workhouse. It was a question whether it would not be cheaper to have the water from the Workhouse. On a vote being taken only 2 voted for the amend- ment, and the resolution of Mr Woodward was carried by a large majority, NO OPPOSITION TO THE PORT SANITARY AUTHORITY. It was decided, on the motion of Mr Chadwick, secon- ded by Mr Woodward, that the Council would oiler no opposition to the Order framed by the Local Govarnment Board tor the permanent constitution of a Port Sanitary Authority for the Customs Port of Manchester.</p>
        <p>The Alkali Works Bill—Deputation to Mr. Long. Mr Walter Long received a deputation on Thurs- day afternoon consisting of Sir J Brunner, MIN Dr Mond, Mr John Brock, Sir David C Gamble, and others in reference to tne new Alkali Works Regu- lation Bill. Mr Long said the Bill was only to legalise what the law officers of the Crown had de- clared to be illegal. Els regretted that it had been introduced without first consulting the representa- tives of the trade, as he was very anxious that nothing of an injurious character should be done. The right bon gentleman promised to give the views of the deputation his careful consideration</p>
