        <p>Corporation intended to make the trains pay at the eZ pense of other Corporation departments. Mr Gooott said he objected to the application because the Corporation had failed to make any of their under- takings pay. There was plenty of accommodation in Warrington for travelling by 'bus without going to this expenditure. 'Bus proprietors who hid come Into the town had lost thousands of pounds. The Towx CLERK : Are you prepared to produce evidence ? Mr Gotean : I wish you would not interrupt. The INSPECTOR: If these 'buses don't pay it seems to me that there would not be accommodation provided I confess I don't consider that a very strong argument. Mr Goalie: Our streets are not wide enough for trams. The INSPECTOR: You cannot go into that, Parliament has said they are wide enough. Mr Goma : The ratepayers ought not te be launched into this large expenditure. Our rates have been increased owing to these new schemes. Ile hoped the objections raised would ba considered by the Board of Trade. The INSPECTOR : I promise you they will be carefully considered. The Tower CLERK said the gas and water undertakings had never cost the ratepayers a penny, Mr GOUGH : What necessity is there for increasing the rates ? The Tows CLERK: Other purposes altogether. Mr GOUGH : You have never brought any evidence to show. The Tuw :4 CLERK: You have brought no evidence in support of your statements. Councillor SMETHURST considered that a saving of about £4,000 might be made by leaving:out that portion of the line proposed in Wininarleigh-street, Palmyra- square, Suez-street and Rylands street, and having the terminus of the Sankey Bridge route in Sankey-street. The Towel CLERK: You are a member of the Tram- way Committee. I don't remember you making any suggestion of this kind. Councillor SMETHURST : I did at the time the train - ways were first brought forward. Mr Lattemort said the scheme was one which in ite essence must fail because it depended on the construc. tion of a tramway along Mersey-street, which the Core poration were not in a position to censtruct in accord- ance with the stipulations of the Board of Trade. It wag provided in the order that the width should be greater than at present and the Corporation did not propose to Increase it under the present scheme. As regarded the. general aspect of the scheme, Warrington did not stand on a par with other Lancashire towns who made profit out of their tramways. Considering the present high rates of the town, he thought Warrington might well wait until it was a little larger and better able to embark on this scheme. The Tome CLERK pointed out with reference to the Mersey-street like that it wag not intended to use the line for passenger traffic but simply to enable them to place the cars in the car shed at night. They were act .- mg in this matter with the consent of the Board of Trade officials. Mr PEMBERTON said he objected to the scheme on the ground that the tramway to Stockton Heath would net stop near the present 'bus terminus. The Corporation had simply ignored the Stockton Heath people and it would be a great hardship upon them:if this systeet were substituted for the 'bus service. The MAYOR said Stockton Heath was inhabited hi people whose interests were mainly in Warrington. Mr JAMES CLIFFE opposed the application on tbe ground that the Corporation was so much in debt. The Towx CLERK: You oppose all applications hi the Corporation. Mr CLIFFE : Yes, and you cannot stop me. I oppeee it because the money is recklessly and extras &amp;gentle spent. The lerseecroet : We won't use any harder language than is absolutely necessary. THE MAYOR CROSS-EXAMINED. The MAYOR said he supported the application, not 11 his capacity as Mayor, but as representing in ratabll value as much as half a dozen of the people who he° spoken. They did not anticipate any financial loss froot the construction of these tramways; or in other word° they did not think the rates would be increased in Om sequence of the provision of tramways. The INSPECTOR : What firm do you represent? The MAYOR: Messrs Joseph Crosfield and Sons., am a director of that company, The tramways enable the people living in popular suburban districts have better access to Warrington than at present. „ The INSPECTOR : Da you consider that general, I speaking the provision of these tramways will be I benefit ? The MAYOR: I consider they will be a great pu • - convenience. I speak not only in my capacity 054 director of the company I have named but as a reside" all my life in the town and vicinity, and as one halflul a good general knowledge of the circumstances It°' the town. • ectore Mr PEMBERTON: I want to know whether the dir eee of Messrs Joseph Crosfield and Sons have met and a resolution that they are in favour of this trlravine order, or whether you are giving the opinion:of onir ° of the directors ? The MAYOR : We haven't passed any such reSollltioOr but I have not given my individual opinion, but opinion of the directors generally with whom Jr° discussed frequently the proposed tramways. oot Mr PEMBERTON : Are the suburban districte served with 'bus services The MAYOR: They are served and very b. served. Mr PEMBERTON : Is it not true that there is JO minutes' service ? eel The MAYOR: There is supposed is be a service quarter of an hour. I wish I could say it had serried out. Mr Goma : Do you appear as a burgess or representative of Messrs Joseph Crosfield and send The INSPECTOR : He has answered that question i"' fully. Mr Gocon I believe you are a director of the 5411 r rington Slate Company ? The MAYOR : I am director of several companies fel that matter. Mr Gocron : You are a director of the Slate Colas that trades with the Corporation? The INSPECTOR : That is hardly a proper question. „, • ar Gomm: I want to know if the Slate CornPlw,:r which be represents does not trade with the Coeporato The INSPECTOR : I don't see how it comes within " scooe of this inquiry. Mr GOUGH: Did you ask the Chairman of the Tro; ways Committee to open :he tenders for the perroau` way of these trams? ee The MAYOR: lam the Chairman of the Comm-- ;d myself. Mr Houma: Did you ask Mr Monks if you should OP° the tender? The MAYOR: I didn't ask him any such queation;oo Mr Gotten: Has the Warrington Slate Company Ince an application to the contractor. Mr E wart, with object of getting an order for steel rails The MAYOR: You had better ask him. The INSPECTOR : I cannot allow questions oft kind. elt Mr Go: If he le interested in this contract I al Is unjust and unfair for him to give evidence. The MAYOR: lam not interested. _at? The INSPECTOR: Are you interested in this cont.:hie The MAYOR: I haven't the slightest interest in " Contract. bole Mr GOUGH : I ask whether the Slate Company er not made application to Mr Ewart. I think I Pere fectly in drder. This may be one of the points w he may be interested• cot I The MAYOR: I have !already told you I 10 Interested. Mr GOUGII : Has your firm made application to contractor with reference to steel rails ? The MAYOR : You must ask them that questioo. Mr CLIPPE : Did Alderman Bolton tell you at the ' Council meeting that you were a good deal too (58" this matter? The MAYOR: He never did. The inquiry closed with an acknowledgment 0- Inspector's services.</p>
